#passage(("When the prophet, peace be upon him, was in Mecca, surrounded by his foes, these verses were revealed: \"Say, if mankind and the jinn gathered in order to produce the like of this Quran, they could not produce the like of it, even if they were to each other assistants.\" Making this challenge itself is having knowledge of the unseen. Now keep in mind that the enemies of the prophet, peace be upon him, could have simply produced something like it, and that would have been it: no more claiming to be a prophet. So how is it that he could make a claim like that? How is that even possible? Isn't there someone out there, an Arab out there, a poet that he's not aware of, someone with great ability, that could produce something like the Quran? Couldn't all the poets come together and produce something on the same level of eloquence as the Quran? The fact that he had the confidence to make such a challenge proves that this was knowledge of the unseen. And this challenge is something that one would put forward only if they were certain that no one would step up and match the eloquence of the Quran.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood 11: The Challenge of the Quran”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ge9XxfiZzhs", ((15, "0:15–1:35"),))
