#passage(("Is it the words? Is it the composition? It's all of that, of course! If I say bring something like the Quran, imitate the Quran, then you have to look at the model of the Quran. So you investigate the Quran: you look at its usage, the words, the combination. You look at all of these things and you take that as a model, and then you bring something like it, you simulate it: something unique of your own, not copying it, something like it. That is the challenge of the Quran.", "There are books written on that. It's called المُعجِزة (The Miracle:) إعادة قراءة الإعجاز اللغوي في القرآن الكريم (A Re-reading of the Linguistic Inimitability in the Holy Quran), and it's available in the Arabic language, that presents to you the objective methodology of what it means to imitate the challenge of the Quran. And it goes through the chapters, and it shows you the miraculous parts, the miraculous usages in the chapter, and then it tells you this is what the challenge means. So part one explains to you what the challenge means, and part two is an actual implementation, where it goes through the chapters and shows you how each chapter fits that objective methodology.", "So what does it refer to when we say the challenge of the Quran? It refers to the words and terminology of the Quran. For instance, the words used in the Quran: the Quran introduces new words into the Arabic language that the Arabs did not know, but the Arabs understand, like the word العالَمين (al'alameen), which the Arabs never knew, which means worlds, multiple worlds or universes. There's multiple meanings that are given by the exegesis, because it's a new word, but they understand. They had the word عالَم ('aalam), one world, but this word, عالَمين ('alameen), they never knew! It also introduces new usages for previous words that existed: a word that already existed in the language, it had a meaning, but the Quran introduces a new usage for it. And this is in every chapter: every chapter has multiple words like this.", "So 1st thing is the words and terminology used in the Quran. Number 2: the internal relations and linguistic expressions in the chapter. And number 3 is the Quranic word compositions and grammatical structures that it offers. And on top of all of that, the challenge is telling you that, while you're doing that, you need to offer meaningful, eloquent speech that adds, like the Quran has, for instance, knowledge of the future, moral teachings, historical information, societal structures, societal practices. So what you need to do is to do the same thing!",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Islam Should Be Banned?! The Quran CAN Be Replicated?! | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6hIakD_VbeU", ((1417, "23:37–24:03"), (1449, "24:09–24:40"), (1489, "24:49–25:46"), (1554, "25:54–26:32"),))
// U ML103:477-502 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("The false premise that he starts with: he says the Quran cannot be replicated. Replicating is bringing something that is the same word for word. The Quran doesn't tell you that. Quran doesn't challenge you to copy it word for word. The Quran doesn't tell you to bring the same structure and give me the same structure with different words. The Quran doesn't tell you that. That's copying. That's plagiarism. The Quran doesn't tell you to plagiarise it. That's not a challenge. You can bring a 10-year-old child and they can plagiarise the Quran. What's so unique about that? The Quran tells you to IMITATE it. Imitating and replicating is not the same thing.", "The second thing you're doing is, you're referring to a verse in the Quran that talks about stories; but even if it doesn't talk about stories, even if it talks about actually imitating the Quran, then where is the imitation? Produce this imitation of the Quran.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Islam Should Be Banned?! The Quran CAN Be Replicated?! | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6hIakD_VbeU", ((1314, "21:54–22:24"), (1367, "22:47–22:56"),))
// U ML358:3-129 | The Muslim Lantern | 6
#passage(("How do you know that Islam is from God? You look at the Quran, you look at the evidences that display that this is from God or not. For example, the Quran cannot be imitated in the Arabic language. It's a scripture that had been there, and the challenge had been for Christians, especially Christian Arabs. No one has been able to imitate the Quran, and they admitted this is the highest eloquent speech in the Arabic language. It has its own genre in Arabic, because it's a miracle by itself.", "If I want to simplify it, it's not about being beautiful, because the criteria is objective. If I say to you: speak to me in English, using English letters, use English grammatical constructs that we use, but also use new English grammatical constructs that we didn't use before, and the speech has to be eloquent. Also bring words we did not know but we still understand. Also, bring an old word that was used in a specific way and use it in a new way in that sentence that you make. So do all of that in one sentence. This is a part, which is not all; it's just a part of the criteria of what makes a chapter of the Quran not being able to be replicated.", "For example, the first chapter of the Quran, Surat Alfatiha: you start by ٱلۡحَمۡدُ لِلَّهِ رَبِّ ٱلۡعَـٰلَمِینَ. The word \"alhamdu\" means praise, and it's a prasie without return; it's not a praise where you get something in return, like thanks. Thanks and praise are different in the Arabic. So thanks: if you give me something, I say thank you, شكر; but praise is nothing in return. So praise is a word the Arabs used, but the Arabs never used الحمد, The Praise, because they never had a human that they would attribute it. The concept wasn't even in their mind, that you would give all praise to a specific object or a thing. So they did not use that in their language; that's a new linguistic construct that the Quran brings. So alhamdu لله, to Allah, رب العالمين. Now العالمين is a new word that didn't exist before in pre-islamic poetry. العالمين means everything conceivable other than Allah.", "Allah says: ٱلرَّحۡمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِیمِ, The Most Merciful, the Very Merciful. He uses 2 words, الرحمن الرحيم, which come from the same root, but they have different meanings. And you do not speak like this in language. You do not say \"in the name of the King, the Great, the Generous\". People don't speak like that, generally, even in the Arabic language. So that's a very unique thing that the Quran introduced. It's to describe Allah with those attributes, صِفات, and then describe those Sefat in combination when speaking about the name of Allah.", "مَـٰلِكِ یَوۡمِ ٱلدِّینِ, The Owner of the Day. The concept of owning time, I don't think even exists in English history. Do you have a poetry where they speak about owning the day? Owning the year? Owning the hour? So Allah says he is the Owner of the Day; this isn't a concept that even Arabs had, or even the Non-Arabs, to begin with: someone owning time. مَـٰلِكِ یَوۡمِ ٱلدِّینِ, the Owner of the Day of Recompense. You say the day of the match, but you don't say the day of Recompense; you don't say the day of Debt. You don't use these terms.", "This is the challenge of the Quran: use Arabic letters but bring grammatical constructs that were never used before, and do all of that in one chapter of the Quran, and it is the highest eloquent literature that can be there. So you can't bring it and someone can phrase it in a better way, in a more eloquent way.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Introduced To The Challenge Of The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=n-flvFktgzU", ((5, "0:05–0:25"), (37, "0:37–1:24"), (87, "1:27–2:13"), (152, "2:32–3:01"), (187, "3:07–3:36"), (228, "3:48–4:15"),))
