#passage(("This expression, وَمَآ أَدْرَاكَ مَا, is unique and new to the Quran. The way the Quran uses it, it's unique to the Quran. How does the Quran use it? The Quran used this term وَمَآ أَدْرَاكَ مَا to refer to something that you're not aware of, something that you haven't seen, that you do not understand its greatness and majesty. So it is used: \"And how would you know what X is?\" It emphasises the greatness; it magnifies the greatness of that thing. So it brings your ear to listen to it. So it introduces to you a new concept, and then it brings your attention to that concept, and highlights its greatness and magnifies its significance. And even today it's been introduced into our language after the Quran, and we say وما أدراك ما فلان (and how would you know what this person is?), like he's a great person, he is like such a generous person, he's such a king of high majesty or something. So you would use that expression in this way after the Quran. Before the Quran, it has never been used in that way. So here, when we talk about the Quranic challenge, that's an example of a linguistic expression that is unique to the Quran, that is being introduced and added.", "Now that expression that the Quran uses, وَمَآ أَدْرَاكَ مَا, is used in the Quran 13 times. Each of those times, the Quran refers to something unique. So, for instance, ٱلْقَارِعَةُ (The Striking Hour), which is one of the names of the Day of Judgment. So it refers to a unique concept; that concept is the Day of Judgment. We don't know how the Day of Judgment is going to be; we don't understand its significance, it's majesty. So the Quran brings your attention to that.", "Chapter:97 of the Quran, or Surat alQadr: you see, in that chapter, it uses لَيْلَةُ ٱلْقَدْرِ (The Night of Decree). The word لَيْلَةُ ٱلْقَدْرِ, it's used 3 times (alQadr97:1-3). The chapter, by the way, is 30 words; it's only 30 words! But it uses لَيْلَةُ ٱلْقَدْرِ three times in the chapter, which is already 6 words out of the 30 words, and it maintains its eloquence and uniqueness. Now this word, لَيْلَةُ ٱلْقَدْرِ or ٱلْقَدْر, it's not used anywhere else in the Quran. So each chapter of the Quran has a unique word that is only used once in that chapter, and not used anywhere else in the Quran; each chapter of the Quran has that. So this chapter, chapter:97, has the word ٱلْقَدْر; it's used 3 times in this chapter. So it's not used multiple times because you don't have other words to use, because it doesn't exist anywhere else in the Quran, in 6,000 verses. But the Quran highlights that it can use the same term multiple times and maintain the highest level of eloquence. You look, for example, chapter:114 of the Quran (anNas): it uses the word ٱلْخَنَّاسِ (the retreating whisperer i.e. Satan). It's only used in that chapter; it's not used anywhere else in the Quran. Chapter:113, ٱلْفَلَق (The Daybreak) (alFalaq113:1). Chapter:112, ٱلصَّمَد (The Eternal Refuge) (alikhlas112:2). Chapter:111, أَبِى لَهَبٍۢ (the name \"Abi Lahab\") (alMasad111:1). And I can go on and on. I go with every chapter of the Quran, and here are one word or more (because it doesn't have to even be one) that is only unique to that chapter in the Quran.", "Then the Quran uses that expression, وَمَآ أَدْرَاكَ مَا: How would you know what لَيْلَةِ ٱلْقَدْرِ is? So no one knew what لَيْلَةُ ٱلْقَدْرِ was. It's a complete new concept the Quran introduces, which is this magnificent Night in the month of Ramadan, which, as the Quran describes it in the same chapter (alQadr97:3), خَيْرٌۭ مِّنْ أَلْفِ شَهْرٍۢ (it is better than 1,000 months), meaning the deed that you will do: it's like you did that one deed for 1,000 months. So you will do one action, one deed in that Night, you will times it x 1,000 months. That's how magnificent that Night is! So Muslims usually, typically in Ramadan, try to do their best in this Night, so they can get the most amount of blessings and rewards as much as they can within that Night, because it's a magnificent Night. Then the Quran highlights how magnificent it is by the angels descending in that Night.", "Another example: this word ٱلْقَدْر. The Arabs always use this word ٱلْقَدْرِ to mean the مكانة of someone, the stature of someone. But the Quran introduces a complete new meaning now to this word ٱلْقَدْرِ, to mean judgment: The Night of Decree, or Night of Judgment. So it adds a complete new meaning to the wordٱلْقَدْرِ, and it links it to قَدَر and مقادير and \"writing the actions of the people\". So it links it to Judgment and Accountability and the Decree of the actions of people, because in that Night, the actions of the people of the entire year, the decree, is determined. That's why it's the Night of Decree. So it became الحُكم there. So it became a Decree, instead of it was the stature of an individual. And it's clearly understood by the Arabs. So the Quran introduced a previous word, ٱلْقَدْر, which means مكانة (makana: status), that the Arabs knew to mean that, and the Quran introduces a complete new meaning, and then it links it to this new Night that you've never known or heard about, and then it introduces a new structure and linguistic expression, وَمَآ أَدْرَاكَ مَا, to signify its importance. And all of that is in one verse.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Islam Should Be Banned?! The Quran CAN Be Replicated?! | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6hIakD_VbeU", ((1658, "27:38–29:05"), (1750, "29:10–29:34"), (1776, "29:36–31:02"), (1870, "31:10–32:07"), (1931, "32:11–33:24"),))
