#passage(("Now, what I also find really fascinating are matters that have to do with the Quran. And again, you have to look into these reports and not read them casually. Read them with a critical eye. Read them trying to get every ounce of detail out of them. Some are a bit clearer than others. So in one report Aisha says that \"I saw the prophet, peace be upon him, sweating on a cold night when receiving revelation\", for no reason. He's just sweating and dripping with sweat. A woman came complaining about her husband. The revelation came and he recited immediately. Now, those that are familiar with some poets, the top poets at the time or even before: for example, [Zuhayr], he'd spend a year fixing up his poetry, making sure it's perfect. With [him], it's instantaneous. Now, yes, there are some poets that can freestyle. However, the meanings are there; the meanings are ready. This is not a matter in which a meaning or meanings can be ready. These are specific instances, specific scenarios, specific questions.", "Aisha was asked about poetry and the prophet, peace be upon him: did he like to listen to poetry? Did he have gatherings, the poetic gatherings that the Arabs would have? And she said that there was no type of speech that he hated more than poetry. But this implies something about him. This was someone that didn't do this, that didn't attend these gatherings, that had nothing to do with poetry, and yet crushed the poets of his time. You'd have a poem, and even [Imru' al-Qais], who was seen as the greatest poet of all time: someone would critique a line or a verse that he has said in his poetry, and they would say, by the way, in the Quran there's something that's similar. These were from critics, by the way, of eloquence. It was the benchmark.",), "Farid Responds, “Proofs of Prophethood - A'isha”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=R2n1dP6j9iI", ((2601, "43:21–45:37"), (2755, "45:55–47:33"),))
