#passage(("The question should be: could Prophet Muhammad have possibly come up with the Quran, and the knowledge, and the information that he conveyed, whether it is to be the Quran itself as divine speech, or the prophecies of the future, or the events of the past that he talked about that nobody knew, or the knowledge of the Quran about the world that we're realising and discovering today? Could Prophet Muhammad have possibly done that as a human being on his own? Is it humanly possible without a Revelation from God?", "Plus, something interesting: you have many scholars, who were scholars of Language, who studied the stylistics of the Quran and Hadith, and say these aren't from the same author, because you can read the Quran and see how the stylistics are (even you, you would recognise that as a normal person who reads books), and you would look at how the Hadiths are. There are so many phraseologies, words used that are only in the Quran and not in the Hadith, and vice versa. As humans we only have a limited set and amount of words that we use; it's a limited vocabulary that we use often. And it's impossible for you to be in 2 characters! \"The Quran is coming down right now, those people are coming asking me a question, let me jump into the character of the Quran and bring this miraculous revelation, words which no human beings can bring, then I'll come back to being myself again and speak.\" It's impossible! You just cannot do it.", "If you talk about that 100% or 1000% certainty, it is only as simple as: could it be possible that this is not from God, that this is from a human being, from Prophet Muhammad? If the answer is no, there's no logical possibilities, no other alternatives to this possibility, then it's a 100% certain! Once you exclude all other options, you only remain with certainty.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Shares His Experience With Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kEUpYAh5b3E", ((2182, "36:22–36:52"), (2233, "37:13–38:03"), (2305, "38:25–38:44"),))
