#passage(("David Wood is claiming that the Quran is one of the worst books that he's ever read. Of course, David Wood is affected by his bias, being a career islamophobe and all, and that has gotten in the way of him judging the Quran fairly. So simply by making a statement like that, David Wood has disqualified himself from being an objective critic of the Quran. David Wood presents his astitute critique of Suratul Kafiron. By the way, he picks this chapter because he thinks it's the easiest chapter to deal with, the easiest and simplest chapter, one that shows absolutely no eloquence. It doesn't really provide any real critique, doesn't show why it's horrible or weak in eloquence or anything like that. And it's natural, because he doesn't know Arabic. Think about it this way: imagine you're a blind film critic at a film festival; your opinions don't really mean much.", "And of course we get the response now: \"Ah, but it only works [in] Arabic.\" You see, without knowing the language, there's no way of having access to all the intricacies of the language: you fail to appreciate the rhyme, you failed to appreciate the Rhythm, and you lose the essence of the work. Which is why Arbury, when translating the Quran, felt the need to keep a rhyme scheme going. Of course, he did that at the expense of the meaning of the actual text, because you can't force Rhymes while keeping true to the meaning. Just to give you an idea of how oblivious David is to the intricate meanings of the verses, we find him relying on a translation that's filled with errors. In fact, there is an error in every single verse. Of course, he did not catch any of these.",), "Farid Responds, “Eloquence of Quran Explained [Surat al-Kafiroon]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HaXafEFMMDE", ((3, "0:03–2:26"), (146, "2:26–3:16"),))
