#passage(("Arab Christians, for example, they're the most tough-headed, or very stick on following whatever their traditions, and they wouldn't want to become Muslims. A lot of the Christians in the West are more like liberal Christians; they don't care much if a Muslim comes to you. But you have this idea of تَعَصُّب (sectarianism) when it comes to Christians that are Arab. It's more difficult, actually. But in reality, they have more of an evidence against them, because they understand the Arabic language. When you listen to the Quran, that's not a speech of a human being! You can read your Bible, الكتاب المقدس (The Holy Book), whatever the term you want to call it, and you can see the clear difference between that text and the Quran. Anybody who reads the Quran, anybody who knows the Arabic language, there's NO WAY that person is going to read the Quran and say, \"Oh, that's like other speech of humans.\" It's just a reality! Poetry is completely different, again: whatever you're going to read, you're going to realise it's nothing like the Quran. And that's the biggest حُجة on you already, the biggest evidence against you as someone who is an Arab.", "So you can have عصبية (sectarianism), you can have this love of your nation, or country, or tradition, or culture, or lineage, and leave the Truth; or you can be someone who's sincere and who's honest. But also know that there are people who are more stubborn, who were around the time of the Prophetﷺ, who cared a lot more about lineages and these things, and they still accepted Islam. So you never know whom Allah can guide and whom Allah cannot guide.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Shares His Experience With Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kEUpYAh5b3E", ((659, "10:59–12:15"), (738, "12:18–12:42"),))
