#passage(("Now, a couple of years later, after the death of the Prophetﷺ, came the time of the first Caliphate of Islam, Abu Bakr asSiddiq. There were a lot of the people who memorised the Quran at the time of the Prophetﷺ. Whenever a verse was coming down, it was written down, it was also memorised by the people. There were multiple memorisers of the Quran, like the 4 people the Prophet told the people to learn the Quran from, because there was this tradition of people learning the Quran, memorising the Quran.", "So, there were people who were called the Memorisers of the Quran, and within the Battle of Yamama, which is two years later or so after the death of the Prophetﷺ, a lot of these reciters of the Quran died. So, Umar ibn al-Khattab, who was another close companion of the Prophetﷺ, came to the first Caliph Abu Bakr, and he told him that there are a lot of people who died in this battle, and I'm afraid that all of the reciters will die and some of the Quran may be lost. Now, he's talking in future tense. That's because we've seen a lot of these reciters die, so I'm afraid that they will continue to die and maybe a part of the revelation will be lost. So, he made a suggestion to Abu Bakr asSiddiq to collect the Quran in written form.", "Now, something very important to understand here is: the Companions here clearly looked at the Quran as an oral text, because they looked at the main way of preservation at those people who were memorisers of the Quran. They didn't look at preservation to do with written text; that's why they didn't collect it from the beginning! They looked at it from an oral tradition, because people memorised the Quran and they were reciting it in each prayer. There were memorisers of the Quran, tens and thousands of memorisers of the Quran, so there was no worry for the Quran to be lost. But when they saw the primary method of preservation, which is the oral transmission, is in danger, those people who are memorising the Quran, they're dying, so they thought of an alternative way, which is an extra way to just make sure, as a backup to the oral tradition: \"Let's also collect the written Quran.\"", "What happened is Abu Bakr asSiddiqؓ, he brought Zayd ibn Thabit, who was one of the people who wrote the revelation. He's also one of the memorisers of the Quran, and he was one of the people that Abu Bakr asSiddiq called, and he said: \"I have a mission for you. Collect these parchments that were written at the time of the Prophetﷺ.\" Zayd ibn Thabit then went, and he collected the Quran, the written Quran, the full written Quran, to be preserved and compiled with Abu Bakr asSiddiq.", "And they had very strict criteria! Each person who will bring a verse and say \"This is a verse of the Quran\", he needs to have 2 witnesses witnessing him take that revelation from the Prophetﷺ, even though they were already memorising the whole Quran! But they still applied that criteria, just to be completely sure and to have full transparency when it comes to the compilation of the Quran. This was now the second stage of the compilation of the Quran, which was to bring all of these parchments, which were written down at the time of the Prophetﷺ.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The History Of The Quranic Text| A Breakdown From Revelation To Compilation| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JjBR2JbHN6o", ((1042, "17:22–17:48"), (1075, "17:55–18:34"), (1114, "18:34–19:18"), (1165, "19:25–20:00"), (1200, "20:00–20:40"),))
