#passage(("At the time of the Caliph of Islam Uthmanؓ there were certain wars taking place, so armies were coming from different lands. Now, when these armies came from different lands, there was a problem happening here, because companions had their own Qiraa, and in these lands people were learning from the companions. So, if Abdullah ibn 'Abbas was in one land and Umar ibn al-Khattab was in a different land, the people in this land learned from Abdullah ibn 'Abbas his Qiraa, and the people here learned from Umar ibn al-Khattab. When the time of war came, now they have two different Qiraat, two different recitations, because they took from two different companions. Even though it's all Quran, the choices that these companions made from the 7 Ahruf is different. They will end up reciting the Quran slightly differently from the other companion that is coming from the other land.", "When these armies met, people started disagreeing with each other. They're saying to each other: \"What is this? This is not the Quran that I was listening to!\" And this happens even today: if some Muslims today go, for example, to certain parts of the world, to Morocco, and they are reciting the recitation of Hafs, and they start listening to Warsh, then they don't recognize many of the words are being said, so they think something is wrong here, if you're not aware. And these were new Muslims, bear in mind. They were not aware of the 7 Ahruf, and they were not aware of the concept of Qiraat. Hudhayfa ibn alYaman came to Uthmanؓ and he said that the people are disagreeing about the Qiraat.", "So, what Uthmanؓ did is he consulted the Companions, and then he made a committee, and he made a standardised text of the Quran. Why did he make a standardised text of the Quran? So he can send them to these lands and then say to the people that \"Whatever you're reciting has to be restricted to the standardised version of the Quran.\" What this standardised version of the Quran does is two things. First, it restricts the recitations of the people, the choices that they can make, to that standardised version of the Quran, and then, if there is any disagreement, they can always come back to that specific standardised version of the Quran.", "Where did this standardised version come from? What Uthmanؓ did is he brought the same person, Zayd ibn Thabit, who Abu Bakr made compile the Quran, and he brought that compiled Quran that was written at the time of the Prophetﷺ, and he used it to make that standardised text. Then he did not put any diacritical marks, so people can recite multitude of different recitations of the Quran using that same text. It's one text, but you can recite in different modes, different Qiraat. That standardised text, that was based on the same Quran that was written down at the time of the Prophetﷺ in accordance with the final revelation that was revealed, then what Uthman did is he sent it to the different lands. So, it restricted the full 7 Ahruf, so it's not all of them; right now, there is a restricted version of the 7 Ahruf.", "Something that is very important to understand here is that any Harf is enough, as the Prophetﷺ said: كلُّها شافٍ كافٍ (Each Harf of it is enough). If we just have one recitation or one Harf of the Quran, that is enough for people, because all of it is Quran in the end, and all of it is the 114 different chapters, but it's just different ways of reciting these 114 different chapters. Something very important to understand: they never learned the Quran from the text, which is why Uthmanؓ left it without diacritical marks, because you cannot just pick it up and start reading a specific recitation. The learning was always oral, and the text was always secondary.", "And then what Uthmanؓ also did is he burnt any other copies. Now, what copies did he burn? He burned these personal Masahif. So, what Uthmanؓ did, he said: \"Bring any of these Masahif, personal Masahif of the people, we're going to remove them. We're just going to leave that standard text, so people can come back to it and do not go to multitude of different sources.\" Only one source to come back to.", "Now, a very important point to note here as well: the parchments that were written at the time of the Prophetﷺ, these parchments were still preserved, even after Uthman made the standard text. Uthmanؓ did not burn this original, if you want to call it \"Original\". They remained until 50 to 60 A.H, which is 20 or 30 years later after Uthmanؓ. All he did is that he made a standard text with the agreement of all of the companions. Like Aliؓ said: \"whatever Uthman did was under the supervision of all of us.\" So, it was not a personal decision that Uthmanؓ made. Uthmanؓ and all of the 4 Caliphs, the Prophetﷺ told us to follow their way and their tradition.", "So now, the people are restricted to the text, but there are still many recitations that you can recite using that text, because the text does not have diacritical marks. You can recite \"مَلِكِ\", you can recite \"مَـٰلِكِ\", you can recite \"تَبَيَّنُوا\", you can recite \"تَثَبَّتُوا\", and you can recite these different words using the same text; you can say \"وَٱلضُّحَىٰ\", you can say \"وَٱلضُّحي\". This was the third stage of the recitations of the Quran and the preservation of the text, the final stage.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The History Of The Quranic Text| A Breakdown From Revelation To Compilation| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JjBR2JbHN6o", ((1644, "27:24–28:13"), (1698, "28:18–28:54"), (1754, "29:14–29:43"), (1788, "29:48–30:57"), (1866, "31:06–31:51"), (1921, "32:01–32:34"), (1956, "32:36–33:28"), (2027, "33:47–34:22"),))
