#passage(("The Quran was revealed in multiple modes, and yes, some Scholars differed when it came to categorization of those modes, and a very small minority of Scholars simply said \"we don't know what it means\". Most Scholars had an idea what it means, and they were clear with what it meant, and the vast majority of them say these are dialects. And just because they're dialects doesn't mean that they're limited to dialects, but they would include words that would have different meanings. Now, this is again an issue of categorization. At the end of the day, everyone is in agreement that Muhammad, peace be upon Him, recited in different modes; it doesn't matter how you label.",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood Fails to Refute me on Qiraat”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6KsYZK-YGEI", ((152, "2:32–3:09"),))
// U FR080:91-129 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("The seven modes are revealed by Allah, and the scholars of Quran simply differ in terms of categorizing the reports; that's pretty much it. And not knowing the correct categorization doesn't change that they are revealed by Allah. So, for example, we have a Hadith by the prophet, peace be upon him, where he says that Allah has 99 names, and we have Scholars that differed in regards to some of what those names are. Does that mean that we don't know what names are, because we don't know some of the names? Or: the prophet said there are 73 sects; now we have Scholars differing in regards to what the sects are. Does that mean we do not know the definition of sect?", "So Sam's mentioning that some Scholars have listed a lot of categorizations: we don't even know who these Scholars are. So, in terms of the authoritative or the weighty opinions, just a handful have a strong backing from [classical] Scholars.",), "Farid Responds, “Response to Sam Shamoun Fresh & Fit Debate on Quranic Preservation”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j8wo1OmaDZU", ((258, "4:18–5:28"), (330, "5:30–6:00"),))
// U FR080:130-165 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Now Sam says that, in regards to dialects, it couldn't have been dialects (both [from Quraysh]), so how are they differing in their recitation, if the modes are specifically about dialects? There are two theories that come to mind. The first is that even though the categorization is about dialects, that doesn't prevent different modes or different variants that affect the meaning to be included in the revealed dialects. So, for example, you have Malik and you have [Melik], Malik meaning owner, [Melik] meaning King, and these obviously have got nothing to do with dialect. But let's say the Quran was revealed with the term Malik; as for the term [Melik], it would be a word that would be included in a different mode, in another tribe's dialect, even though those specific words have got nothing to do with dialect. Now, that's one theory that comes to mind.", "Another theory is that the modes are seven and these are dialects; as for any other words that actually influence the meaning, then these fall outside the seven modes. They are revealed by Allah; however, they just fall out of that seven mode categorization.",), "Farid Responds, “Response to Sam Shamoun Fresh & Fit Debate on Quranic Preservation”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j8wo1OmaDZU", ((365, "6:05–7:21"), (441, "7:21–7:48"),))
