=== 26.2 Ahruf, qira'at and the canonical readings
// NODE P05.C03.S02
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML256:289-380 | The Muslim Lantern | 8
#passage(("After that, came the second Caliph of Islam, Umar ibn al-Khattab, and then after him the third Caliph, Uthman ibn Affan, and Islam spread far and wide; Islam spread all over the world. Keep in mind that, as we said, the Quran was revealed in these 7 different modes, these 7 different dialects of recitation. People were reciting the Quran orally. They were taking the Quran from their teachers, from the Companions, etc. Just like 'Umar ibn alKhattab used to say, and many of the companions, they said: القِراءة سُنّةٌ مُتَّبَعة, the Qiraa is a tradition, that you take from your teacher, from his teacher, going back to the Prophetﷺ. They were always taking the Quran orally. They never looked at the text as a means of taking the Quran. They only collected it as a backup plan for preservation, but they always took the Quran orally from their teachers, going back to the Prophetﷺ, from the source.", "So, the people were reciting the Quran and learning from their teachers. When Islam spread far and wide, those companions travelled to different lands. You had a companion travelling to a specific land, and another companion travelled to a different land. Then the people in that land started learning the recitation from that Companion.", "Now, there's something very important to understand. If you understand this concept properly, you'll understand the Ahruf and Qiraat a lot more. There's something called the concept of choice, and that is: when Allahﷻ revealed these 7 different dialects and modes of recitation, with the words and enriching the meaning of the Quran, as we said in the beginning, He allowed people to recite whatever is easy for them. What happened is, the Companions, for example, can recite a part of the first Harf and a part of the second Harf and a part of the third Harf, because all of these different dialects, they are the Quran. A Companion might recite 30% of the first dialect with 10% of the other dialect, etc. So, these choices were completely open. Those Companions of the Prophetﷺ could choose whatever is easy for them to recite. That is what a Qiraa is.", "Now, there's a difference between a Qiraa and a Harf: a Qiraa is the choices that a reciter makes. You have the 7 different Ahruf, and you can choose from these 7 different Ahruf whatever is easy for you to recite. Then in the end you end up with your own Qiraa. All of these companions had their own Qiraa, their own recitations of the Quran, collected and combined from the 7 Ahruf, because, as we said, these 7 Ahruf are all revelation from Allahﷻ. For example, Abdullah ibn 'Abbas had his Qiraa, Ubay ibn Ka'b had his Qiraa, etc.", "That's why, if you look at the Hadith of Umar ibn al-Khattab and Hisham ibn Hakim, which are two of the Companions of the Prophetﷺ: Hisham ibn Hakim was reciting a part of the Quran, then Umar ibn al-Khattab heard him reciting, and he heard him reciting something he didn't hear before, because they were all taking the Quran from the Prophetﷺ. So, he dragged him, and he took him to the Prophet, and he said: \"O Prophet of Allah, I saw this companion reciting the Quran in a different way to what I heard from you!\" This here shows 2 things. First, if anyone tries to change the Quran, the Companions, straight away, they're alert! Because they know the source. They already memorise the Quran, so they know any differences when they hear them. That's number one. Number two: that they also come back to the Prophetﷺ if there is any disagreement or there's any issues.", "Then, when Umar ibn al-Khattab brought him, and he recited the Quran in front of the Prophetﷺ, the Prophet told Umar: \"You also recite.\" Then Umar recited, then the Prophetﷺ told him: \"You are right, and you are right.\" These are different modes, different ways of recitation that are all coming down as a revelation from Allah, and you're allowed to choose to recite this way, and you're allowed to choose to recite that way. This is the Prophetﷺ telling these Companions that you can have your Qiraa, you can have your choices.", "An easier way to bring it to the mind of the people listening is to look at the Quran, for example. You have Chapter 2 and Chapter 3. Imagine if Chapter 2 is one Harf and Chapter 3 is a different Harf. So, you take a part of Chapter 2, and you recite with it a part of Chapter 3: you end up with a new chapter, other than Chapter 2 and Chapter 3, but in the end what you end up with is just a combination of these two revelations, two chapters of revelation. So, what you end up with is also a revelation, because it is just combining these two chapters. This is how you come up with a Qiraa: you combine between the different Ahruf, different dialects that were revealed, and you come up with a Qiraa in the end.", "All of the companions had their Qiraa. Then when they travelled to different lands, the people started learning the Quran from the companion in their city, in their country, in their land. Now, the people who are learning the Quran can learn from two companions. They can also do the same thing; they can have their own Qiraa by choosing some of the recitation of this companion, as well as some of the recitation of this different companion. So now, you come up with a different Qiraa. But, and this is the important thing to understand, whatever Qiraa you come up with, combining from different Qiraat or from different Ahruf, it is all in the end Quran that is coming from Allahﷻ. You're just combining different parts of revelation with each other, reciting that which is easier for you, what is most eloquent and easier for your tongue. There were tons of different Qiraat. So, the Qiraat were not only limited to 7 or 10. The companions had their own Qiraat, and the تابعين (Tabi'in: Followers of the Companions), many of them had their own Qiraat.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The History Of The Quranic Text| A Breakdown From Revelation To Compilation| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JjBR2JbHN6o", ((1266, "21:06–21:55"), (1320, "22:00–22:12"), (1337, "22:17–23:14"), (1394, "23:14–23:49"), (1437, "23:57–24:36"), (1491, "24:51–25:12"), (1531, "25:31–26:18"), (1578, "26:18–27:17"),))
