// U FR268:1-61 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("There are multiple mass transmitted reports that says that the Quran was revealed in seven modes; you'll find this in any book of hadith. This isn't something that David just discovered; this is something that's been taught since day one in Islam.", "Historically, there's no doubt that the [qira'at] existed among the Sahaba themselves. Is it possible for the Sahaba to have completely forgotten how to recite the Fatiha? That's something that's simply too hard to swallow. Keep in mind that not a single kid today [would] mess up reciting it, [let alone] the first generation of Muslims.",), "Farid Responds, “Are the Qiraat mistakes?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K11XfOfTFAU", ((42, "0:42–0:57"), (119, "1:59–2:34"),))
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML280:117-163 | The Muslim Lantern | 5
#passage(("Muslims have been reading the Quran; they travel to different worlds; they read the same book, the same Scripture. Wherever you go in the world, you'll be reading the Quran; it's the same Quran. So the Quran, it is the same Quran. When we say the Quran, we're talking about the 114 chapters.", "The Prophetﷺ said that \"the Quran was revealed in seven different ways, seven different dialects\". Why? Because there was different groups of Arabs at the time of the Prophetﷺ, and they spoke different dialects. And to bring it closer to your mind, for example, you've got today different dialects in America, in Australia, in Canada, etc. All of them speak English, but the English they speak is completely radically different. If you bring someone from Scotland and you say: OK, speak to someone from the US, he might not understand half of what he's saying, especially the terminology that he's saying, and the usage, and how he's pronouncing the words. So it's still the same language, still the same letters, still the same thing, but it's just the way he's pronouncing that is completely different.", "The Arabs had these different groups, and they had different dialects, and the Quran was revealed first at the dialect of Quraish, the dialect of the Prophetﷺ, because primarily the Prophet was preaching to his people first there, before moving to anyone else. But when people were entering Islam, it became difficult for all people, children and this and that, who speak a different dialect now, to recite the Quran in a dialect other than theirs. Then Angel Gabriel came to the Prophetﷺ, and he said that Allah has given you now the capacity to recite the Quran in seven different dialects, to make it accessible for those Arabs to be able to recite the Quran in these seven different ways. Now it's easier for the children, for the old, for anyone who it might be difficult for him to recite the Quran in a different dialect.", "For example, when you read the Quran, there's a recitation that would say: \"وَٱلضُّحَىٰ\" (By the morning sunlight); the other one would say: وَٱلضُّحي. It is the exact same word, the exact same meaning; there is no difference between the two, it's just how you say it. For example: \"الْأَرْض\" and \"الَارْض\" (earth): there is just a letter not being pronounced, but it's the same exact word, same exact meaning.", "So here Allah, when He revealed these seven different dialects, He also enriched the meaning of the Quran, by revealing certain words that are similar to another word, but it's not the exact same. For example, Allah revealed in certain chapters \"تبيّنوا\" (make sure) and \"تثبّتوا\" (investigate). Make sure and investigate: they are of very similar meaning, very similar understanding, but it's a different word. Now these few different words that Allah has revealed in these different seven أحرف (Ahruf: Dialects), it is very few, very minute, and you will find them in the different recitations of the Quran, and they're very well known. For example, if you recite the recitation of Warsh, you will say مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ, and if you recite with Hafs you will say مَٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ. So these are differences; the Muslims know them, and they know the history where they trace from, because they come back to the source, the Prophet Muhammad, and Revelation from Allah. All of these different ways of reciting are coming from the same source, so all of them are Quran. So when we say the Quran is the same, we're referring to the 114 chapters and the information within the 114 chapters. The way to recite those 114 chapters can be different, depending on the recitation that you're using.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Claims The Quran Contains Satanic Verses?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=27xa01f05ag", ((415, "6:55–7:19"), (443, "7:23–7:52"), (479, "7:59–8:35"), (515, "8:35–8:51"), (540, "9:00–9:50"),))
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR088:267-364 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("What you will find classical Scholars saying is he removed the dots from the [mushaf]. What does this mean? When someone says he removed the dots, it means that there were dots, and now there are no dots. So the problem with David and his guests is that they think that the existence of multiple recitations after the time [of Uthman] is a problem.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((812, "13:32–14:27"),))
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML256:468-560 | The Muslim Lantern | 8
#passage(("Now, there's a skeletal text, which is the text that Uthmanؓ standardised and he sent to different lands, and there's, as we said, tons of different Qiraat that people took from the companions and then from their students.", "Later on in history, there was an individual called ibn Mujahid, and ibn Mujahid was a scholar in the Quran and recitations of the Quran. What ibn Mujahid did is he looked at where are the best recitations of the Quran: there's a multitude of different recitations, so let me look at the best reciters, the people who are most eloquent in their recitation, most eloquent in their understanding of the Arabic language, have the best chains tracing back to the Prophetﷺ, the most authentic chains. What he did is he took 7 of these recitations, and he wrote a book about these 7 different recitations. He took 7 different recitations, not the Ahruf; a recitation is combining between different Ahruf or recitations of the companions. He collected the most eloquent and the best recitations that he found at the time.", "People did not have to follow that book, did not have to take information from that book. That was just a personal decision that he made, ibn Mujahid. This was not like \"Muslims have to follow this!\" or something. There were still many other different recitations that people were reciting and taking, but what happened later on is that book became so popular, and then the people started restricting themselves to these 7 different recitations, and a lot of people had a misconception: they thought that the 7 recitations of ibn Mujahid are the same as the 7 Ahruf, which, we've already highlighted, they're completely two different things. And that's why many of the scholars blamed ibn Mujahid. They said: \"He could have collected 8 recitations, or 6 recitations, or 20 recitations. Why did he have to choose 7?\", which confused many of the people, thinking that these 7 recitations are the 7 Ahruf.", "Some of the scholars also came later on, and what they did, to clear that misunderstanding as well, they added 3 extra recitations from the eloquent reciters of the Quran, the people who have the best chains, etc., the most understanding of the Arabic language, like Ibn al-Jazari in his book \"النشر في القراءات العشر\". Then we have 10 recitations.", "The 10 recitations that we're reciting today, they were not the only recitations available, as we said. There were tons of other recitations. The only reason that they are popular today is because they became popular later on. People started adopting them because of their eloquence. Something interesting for people to understand is that even today, if you become a scholar of Qiraat and highly eloquent in the Arabic language, etc., you can even have a new Qiraa: you can combine between these 10 different Qiraat. So, even today you're not restricted to the 10 different Qiraat! Why? Because all of them are Quran, all of them are Revelation, all of them are coming from Allahﷻ.", "These recitations of the Quran, the 10 different recitations of the Quran, they are HIGHLY mass transmitted. We're talking about tens of thousands, hundreds of thousands of chains of narrations transmitting these 10 recitations of the Quran, tracing back to the Prophetﷺ, and we have voluminous works that have the names and biographies of the people in the chain, the people transmitting the information, not the information itself, but just the people transmitting the information, for example الضوء اللامع or الحلقات المضيئة or النشر في القراءات العشر or طبقات القراء. All of these are books which contain the biographies of the narrators of the Quran, and the chains themselves are available as well; you can see them in \"النشر في القراءات العشر\" and \"طبقات القراء\".", "Anyone today who recites any of the Qiraat has something which is called \"Ijaza\". An Ijaza is: you have a certificate, and this certificate has the names of your teachers of one of the 10 Qiraat that you recite, the names of your teachers going back all the way to the Prophetﷺ, and today we have millions of these. Millions of memorisers of the Quran: we have millions of certificates of Ijaza of people transmitting that same text.", "So, there is not a shadow of a doubt, when it comes to the oral preservation of the Quran, that these 10 recitations that we're reciting are Quran, the revelation that is coming from Allahﷻ. So, when we say: \"The Quran is preserved letter for letter, word for word.\" Yes! It is preserved letter for letter, word for word, because each of the recitations is Quran, and each letter and each vowel and each way to recite is Quran. The Quran is preserved letter for letter, word for word, vowel for vowel, and will always be preserved in that way.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The History Of The Quranic Text| A Breakdown From Revelation To Compilation| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JjBR2JbHN6o", ((2062, "34:22–34:37"), (2092, "34:52–35:43"), (2147, "35:47–36:27"), (2202, "36:42–37:00"), (2242, "37:22–38:04"), (2289, "38:09–38:53"), (2350, "39:10–39:32"), (2403, "40:03–40:30"),))
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR263:112-198 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Not all [recitations] are authentic according to [Ibn Mujahid]. However, the reason for his canonization is to familiarize readers of the Quran with the correct recitation. He's not trying to tell you that the [recitations] are similar to conflicting rulings; that would defeat the purpose of him compiling his book in the first place, if it clearly points out that there are levels and ranks of [reciters] of the Quran: some of them are Imams, some of them should be followed, some of them are followed by their communities as a whole, and some of them are rejected completely. Again, the purpose of his book is to introduce you to the correct recitations.", "Furthermore, if you go through the book, you'll find that his introduction is all based around how the recitation is a sunnah. He specifically points out that these are taken from these Imams, and recitation from their predecessors.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((285, "4:45–5:40"), (340, "5:40–7:52"),))
// U FR088:452-532 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("David's guest is saying that anyone who recited something that's outside the seven was taken to jail. So here's the thing: there's two instances. One was taken to court and flogged, not because he recited something outside the seven; he recited recitations that conflict: he wrote in a way that conflicted with the way [that Uthman] wrote. So that's what happened. It's not because there were 70 recitations, and if you recite anything out of the seven, then you're going to get flogged. So if you recite [Ya'qub's] recitation, you're not going to get flogged: that's not one of the seven. The other reciter, he would recite things that were not recited by anyone. So those were the two instances in which that happen.", "He makes it seem like [Jazari] adds three recitations that, if you recited them, you get flogged, and this is like an invention in the religion, or something new that's added in the religion. Now, these recitations existed: the recitation of Abu [Ja'far] existed from the beginning of second century; there was no period in time which this recitation did not exist.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((1206, "20:06–22:18"), (1366, "22:46–23:29"),))
// U FR088:1113-1164 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("I think the term canonized is a bit weird: [Ibn Mujahid] writes a book in which he explains the differences between the seven. David is arguing that there's one correct thing, there's one thing that Muhammad, peace be upon Him, recited; that's David's assumption. David believes Muhammad recited one recitation, and then it became 70, and then [Ibn Mujahid] shrank it down to seven. Why didn't he shrink it down to one? That's the question. Can anyone find an example of a classical Muslim scholar that believed in one recitation? I can't think of a single example. This concept of one recitation, it's not traditional, it's not Orthodox.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((3092, "51:32–53:49"),))
// U FR263:550-558 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("[There are] works written a few decades after [Ibn Mujahid] that revolved around the seven. Within the century, the seven [recitations] became canonical as far as Andalusia.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((1446, "24:06–24:24"),))
// U FR263:278-314 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("[Recitations] were definitely judged; this occurred from day one. This is natural. Now, we're not only talking about words; we're talking about diacritical marks, pronunciations: every little thing needed to be attributed to Allah correctly, perfectly. Now, [Ibn Mujahid] has criticisms in regard to the seven, mainly in regards to false attributions to the seven. However, I'll give you this: [Ibn Mujahid] sometimes did criticize a few words in the recitation of three reciters, out of the seventy-seven thousand words in the Quran, or something along those lines.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((715, "11:55–13:03"),))
// U FR263:361-369 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Early Muslims did reject [recitations] that were not agreed upon by consensus; however, the recitation that [Ibn Mujahid] had listed out (I'm referring to Basra, [Kufa], Mecca, Medina and Sham), they themselves fell under the consensus of those communities. Due to this, these seven readings were seen as unquestionably correct.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((929, "15:29–15:52"),))
// U FR263:527-541 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Actually, the recitation of [Hamza] was the most popular recitation in [Kufa] before [Ibn] Mujahid was even born; this is affirmed by Muhammad bin Haytham. Basically, Hamzah's recitation was referred to as an innovation not due to the variances in his recitation, but rather due to [matters that] had to do with tone.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((1393, "23:13–23:44"),))
// U FR076:178-199 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("You've provided an example of a scholar that didn't accept everything that he heard, and, by the way, that was the case with all Scholars: just because something is recited and attributed to the prophet, peace be upon him, doesn't mean that it is an authentic recitation. Recitations and things that he was not familiar with, that were not widespread where he was, he just simply rejected, simply due to caution. So the question is: why accept multiple modes, despite being skeptical enough to reject some recitations? And the answer is simple: the whole Community accepted that the prophet, peace be upon Him, recited in multiple modes.",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood Fails to Refute me on Qiraat”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6KsYZK-YGEI", ((418, "6:58–7:54"),))
// U FR176:168-293 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("The issue is: the Quran is learned; it was taught by the prophet, peace be upon him. You're ignoring all the diacritical marks in the Quran, and you're guessing what the correct recitation should be. What if we all did that? Wouldn't that lead to ultimate chaos? It's not only the 10 main reciters of the regions; it's the actual five regions completely. Did you base your translation on a manuscript without any diacritical marks or dottings, or were you heavily influenced by a popular recitation, like [Hafs an Asim], for example?",), "Farid Responds, “Debate with Quran-only Scholar Edip Yüksel”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WL8QjGB7s7w", ((424, "7:04–12:26"),))

=== 26.3 The claim of different versions and public awareness of the readings
// NODE P05.C03.S03
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML195:514-543 | The Muslim Lantern | 1
#passage(("It's one Quran: 114 chapters, 114 suras. This Quran is memorized in the hearts of millions of people. We don't have different groups with different numbers of books like Christians do: 66 with the [Protestants], 73 of the Catholics. This is not something that exists in Islam. Every group that calls itself Muslims believes in one Quran.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Questions Muslim About Quran Preservation! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=m5tQ8QfHxw4", ((1143, "19:03–19:25"),))
// U ML098:318-356 | The Muslim Lantern | 3
#passage(("This is where they play the game: they bring the different Recitations of the Quran. But you have all of these different Recitations of the Quran that are ALL revealed from Allah. So الأَحْرُف (the different modes of Recitation) are all revealed from God, so they're all Revelation. And they have verses that are revealed; some of the wording in these verses are different from one to another. None of them contradict the meaning; they all harmonise in meaning. They will add an extra layer of miraculous nature to the Quran, an extra layer of meaning into the Quran, like for instance: \"you know\" and \"I know\" (alisra17:102): that Pharaoh knew about the signs that Moses was doing, that Pharaoh knew that this was from God, and Moses knew that this was from God. So it adds an extra layer than you just saying that Moses knows, or that Pharaoh knows: it adds a layer that both of them knew.", "None of them are contradictory, and it's very small differences. And all of these differences can be read from the same one Mushaf of 'Uthman, because that same writing of ‘Uthman did not have vowels or dots. So you can read both readings using that same written Mushaf of ‘Uthman. So it's one Mus'haf, it's one Quran: one Mus'haf, different modes of Recitation that were revealed, all of them from Allah, from the Prophet.", "Now when you look at the Bible, when you talk about all of these different versions, they're not all coming as a revelation from God. When we talk about Bible being corrupted and verses added, we're talking about added by human beings. We're talking about extra versions that some believe is from God, some believe it's not from God. We know the Quran is preserved, because we know all of these modes of Recitation traces back to the Prophet. We have them all recorded; we have all Chains of Narrations for them; we have memorizers of them from the time of the Prophet until today. Everything is recorded and passes down and goes all the way back to the Prophet. You look at the Bible: you don't know the sources, 400 years later, written by unknown people; extra additions that they themselves admit that random people added in the scripture, not a revelation from God.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| 5 Reasons I Left Islam?! Hijabi Says Christians Will Go To Heaven?! | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=wIhGWmIQTJY", ((1033, "17:13–18:05"), (1091, "18:11–18:34"), (1116, "18:36–19:14"),))
