=== 26.4 Whether the readings are errors of memory
// NODE P05.C03.S04
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR079:1-190 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("The natural assumption, when you have variants attributed to a specific text, is that it's a mistake: if you see any text, a book, a poem, a saying, a sermon, with multiple wordings, the most common assumption is to assume these are mistakes. So when you find centuries of scholarship all agreeing that there are multiple ways of reading the same book, then this shows that this is the Orthodox position. That's the first point.", "The second piece of evidence is that much of the variant are dialects: when you have multiple dialects, you don't assume that these are mistakes. They were there from the start and were acknowledged. If they were not there from the start, if this was a view that was introduced later on, you'd have two views; but you only have one view, which is: the Quran is revealed in multiple modes.",), "Farid Responds, “Why not assume the Qira'at are just mistakes?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uU1C23atotE", ((496, "8:16–9:21"), (589, "9:49–10:42"),))
// U FR076:96-132 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("David claims that these are mistakes by Muhammad, peace be upon him. Fair enough, you can claim that; but you're agreeing with me that Muhammad, peace be upon Him, recited in multiple ways, and that's good enough for me.",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood Fails to Refute me on Qiraat”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6KsYZK-YGEI", ((230, "3:50–4:06"),))
// U FR078:206-241 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Of course, the problem with this theory is that when one is forgetful, they don't randomly start speaking in a different dialect; that doesn't really make any sense. And even the actual variants that affect the meaning, words like King and owner, aren't words that you would mix up.", "So the prophet, [peace] be upon Him, is in [Mecca] for the majority of his prophethood; he only has [Meccans] around him. Why would he be reciting in different dialects in the first place? As David correctly pointed out, it was during the time [of Hisham's] acceptance of Islam that Omar first realized that there are multiple modes. Well, the natural assumption is that these multiple modes were revealed very late into the prophethood, and that makes sense, because that's when you had multiple tribes from different regions converting to Islam.",), "Farid Responds, “Don't tell Sam Shamoun about this Video!!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=C4P3sNDhViU", ((525, "8:45–9:05"), (575, "9:35–10:12"),))
// U FR076:1-31 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Why assume we have multiple recitations? We have isnads to the prophet, peace be upon him. Let's imagine that you were in the second century, you were in Kufa, and you decided to create an isnad to this new Quran that you've come up with: no one's going to be falling for that.",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood Fails to Refute me on Qiraat”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6KsYZK-YGEI", ((25, "0:25–1:04"),))
// U FR076:200-277 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("[Sa'id] used to recite in two recitations: in Ramadan, in one night the recitation of [Zayd], and the other night he's reciting the recitation of [Ibn Mas'ud]. The problem is: everyone believed in multiple modes; it's not just [Sa'id] himself. So why were they upon this belief? It's because the prophet, peace be upon Him, indeed recited in different modes.", "What's the probability that the prophet, peace be upon him, had dementia and would constantly forget the words that he was reciting, and that's why there were so many recitations, but at the same time he was able to convince over 10,000 people, the Medina community, that these were all Revelation? How likely is that?",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood Fails to Refute me on Qiraat”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6KsYZK-YGEI", ((481, "8:01–9:38"), (625, "10:25–10:52"),))
// U FR268:217-243 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("To summarize: the 10 qira'at, they were taught by the Prophet, peace be upon him; these couldn't have been mistakes. You cannot mess up in [Fatiha], recited multiple times a day, twice at [Fajr], twice at [Maghrib], twice [Isha], at least, every single day: you cannot mess that up; you simply cannot. You cannot mess up Malik and [Maalik], and these were recited, mass transmitted, by the Muslim communities. And the dialects: the Quran was only revealed in seven modes; that does not encompass every single dialect, every single way any Arab recites.",), "Farid Responds, “Are the Qiraat mistakes?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K11XfOfTFAU", ((702, "11:42–12:38"),))
// U FR079:246-267 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Another point that's also very important is: what is this amazing book that comes in multiple modes? This is something that we need to focus on in our [da'wah]: we're supposed to tell people: don't think there's an argument that is used against Islam. Muslims are supposed to say: check it out: is there anything like this? Look at these meanings, how they're [complementary], and how they add to the meaning of the text.",), "Farid Responds, “Why not assume the Qira'at are just mistakes?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uU1C23atotE", ((818, "13:38–14:33"),))

