// U FR176:671-767 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("You're appealing to reports about a battle occurring and the Muslims winning that battle; you're saying this is a prophecy about that. But the issue is: you've went against the consensus of reciters of the Quran. You are a Quranist, someone who holds on to the Quran: this is how all the Muslims have always been reading the Quran, the consensus of Muslims; and, guess what, they all claim that they all got it from Rasulullah. However, you're saying they all messed up.", "The issue here is: when they're all reciting the same thing, even though there's no diacritical marks, what are the odds of all of them screwing up? All the recitations traced back to before the Umayyad empire.", "That's how we have the actual Quran: it's through these specific people; that's how all the Muslims in the world read the Quran. The crazy thing about this whole subject is: I thought the [Quranists] held on to the Quran to at least a small degree, but no, it seems like you guys are just making this up completely and rejecting what we've inherited from our predecessors. This verse, it's not only [mutawatir] up to each of these reciters; not only that, they're all agreeing; and yet you're saying maybe it was all falsely attributed to all of them. It's wild.",), "Farid Responds, “Debate with Quran-only Scholar Edip Yüksel”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WL8QjGB7s7w", ((1732, "28:52–29:26"), (1814, "30:14–31:04"), (1917, "31:57–32:41"),))
// U FR263:331-349 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("All these classifications: this has absolutely no bearing on the authenticity or the weakness of the chains themselves. To suggest that criticisms of [qira'at] didn't exist before [Suyuti] came up with these classifications: just plain deceptive.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((865, "14:25–14:45"),))
// U FR088:2228-2301 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Why don't they use best manuscripts? What best manuscripts? What are you talking about? These are reciters from the [third] century; the manuscripts that are written are there to fit into their recitations. If you're going to go for a canonical manuscript from the first century, what's that going to lead you to? You're still going to have different recitations there. Let's say, for example, you got a [Medinan] manuscript, so it fits into the recitation, and then you got a manuscript which is closer to recitation, and then what? Now we're going to get rid of half, and we're just going to rely on these two?", "In one classical text, the recitation was recited by one, two, three, four, five, six reciters, reciting it like that. So, frankly, it doesn't matter if someone's unfamiliar with the recitation or critiques it for sounding strange, according to some Arabic Scholars, because you had major Quranic reciters from different regions reciting it.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((6608, "1:50:08–1:51:29"), (6732, "1:52:12–1:53:19"),))
// U FR263:382-420 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("What I'd like to show you is an example: check out these two letters in the beginning of Surat Yaseen. Now, without the [diacritical] marks, you'd have absolutely no way of knowing how to recite this. It could be recited as, naturally, Yasin; it could be recited as Tahseen, ba seen, noon seen. The number of examples that you could come up with are innumerable, and we're just referring to one word, referring to two letters. So you could come up with, not really unlimited, but a really huge number of possibilities for these two letters, which can actually be [four letters], if you think about it. And yet all the reciters have recited this as [Yasin].",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((982, "16:22–18:10"),))
// U FR172:152-199 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Sometimes Shady will make egregious claims and misattributions to scholars. One example is this misattribution to Ibn [Atiyyah]. He says, on page 40 of Transmission of Variant Readings: \"Their origin was attributed to the Quran readers and the transmitters themselves, i.e. to their own selectivity and [ijtihad] in reading and deciphering the Uthmanic consonantal outline, and not to the Prophet.\" In the footnote he openly states that the seven readings originated due to the eponymous reader's interpretation.", "First of all, when we return to the quote, we find nothing about the defective [rasm]. Secondly, Ibn [Atiyyah] is clearly saying that this wasn't based on an interpretation of the consonantal skeleton, but it is based on what the reciters learned from their teachers. So the early reciters who learned Surah Nas would recite which they learned from their teachers, and then they would look at the undotted [mushaf] and say: \"Yes, this fits in exactly as we learned it\", and they would then recite it. If the recitation that they learned didn't fit into the consonantal skeleton, they wouldn't recite it.", "According to Shadi, the reciters would look at the manuscript and say: \"Hmm, what does this word look like? Maybe it's [ya's], which means despair? I think I'm going to choose a nas, since it makes more sense to me.\" Now, this is the impression that we get from Shadi's misquotation: he makes the Quran appear like it's some sort of crossword puzzle, in order to undermine the Quran itself.",), "Farid Responds, “Exposing Harvard Professor Shady Hekmat Nasser”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SRvc6YARzRY", ((407, "6:47–7:23"), (449, "7:29–8:10"), (490, "8:10–8:45"),))
// U FR263:421-520 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("The ten authentic [mutawatir recitations] all recited in one way. Why do they all say the same thing? Because the recitation is not taken from the [consonantal] skeleton: they learn this from one another; they took this from their teachers. There is not a single authentic recitation that says the Romans were victorious, even though both can be read using the [consonantal] skeleton.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((1168, "19:28–20:12"),))
// U FR176:517-576 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Are you saying that we should all pick up a [mushaf] without any diacritical marks and try to figure out what it says, and the Quran will still remain preserved? If everyone's doing this guesswork by themselves, we will ultimately have different Qurans.",), "Farid Responds, “Debate with Quran-only Scholar Edip Yüksel”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WL8QjGB7s7w", ((1333, "22:13–22:56"),))
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML285:226-288 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("I sometimes wonder: do people have no shame to attribute things to the Sahaba and the Tabi'in, and say that they said this, they said that? Yahya ibn Ya'mar, who was not a Sahabi (he died in the second century, actually): did he say to you, \"I have put these dots as a Bid'ah Hasana to bring people closer to Allah\"? Is this a matter of religion? Dots on letters: is that a matter of religion? SubhanAllah, why would you attribute things to people that they did not attribute to themselves, that did not quote themselves? Is it because you want a specific thing for yourself? That's why you quote these things, and you attribute things which are not correct to them?", "Yahya ibn Ya'mar, he's not the first person; he did not introduce dots. That is completely incorrect. Either the person doesn't know, or the person is deliberately misrepresenting this. Here's an inscription. This inscription here is dated to 22 AH, the هجري Hijri calendar (Islamic calendar). Yahya ibn Ya'mar died in the second century Hijri. This is dated 22, and who is dating it? The writing has the date من سنة 22 (at year 22), and the person who wrote it is there: he wrote in the inscription what time he wrote this information. This inscription is dated to 22 AH, the time of the Sahaba, the time of the Prophetﷺ, and it has dots. That's one inscription.", "Here's an inscription written by Zouhair. It says بِسْمِ ٱللَّٰه (Bismillah: In the name of God), and it has أنا (Me): all of these dots, for this person who's saying dots came in the second century! And he said, \"Bismillah, أنا زهير (my name is Zouhair). I wrote this when 'Umar died in year 24 AH.\" So, you've got an inscription dated 24 AH that has dots on it, meaning at the time of the Prophetﷺ there were inscriptions; at the time of the Sahaba there were inscriptions, there were dots written. So this is absolute evidence that is refuting this claim: before even Yahya ibn Ya'mar was born (in the end of the first century, or whenever he was born), you have inscriptions there, and you have the dots. So this idea that he introduced the dots is completely incorrect.", "And also, that is why the scholars say that Uthmanؓ left the مصاحف (Masahif: Written Books of Quran) without dots for a reason: it was intentional, which means that there were dots at the time, that they were already using dots. And that reason was: it was because of the different recitations, so that it can include as much of the different recitations as possible. So, when people speak about the Deen of Allah like this, they need to at least understand what they're saying.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Quran Scientific Claim Debunked? Bida'ah Is Good? | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5z_gp4XDYzs", ((705, "11:45–12:12"), (738, "12:18–13:06"), (794, "13:14–14:03"), (843, "14:03–14:29"),))
