=== 26.6 Asim, al-Sulami and the transmitters of the reading
// NODE P05.C03.S06
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR172:64-108 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("[Shadi], in Transmission of [Variant] Readings, page 111, makes the claim that the seven/10 readers and their two main rawies were all deemed weak and careless in their transmission of hadith. The claim that all the reciters are weak in hadith is an audacious lie, which Shadi refutes in his second book, page 134 to 135.", "On page 131, Shady awkwardly tiptoes around the subject when providing the biographies of the reciters, stating that he will list the negative information only. The reason I find this very interesting is because Shadi accuses the scholar of trying to systematically undermine the value of the canonical readings, but, unlike Shadi, at least had the decency to provide the positive information about the reciters. Isn't it ironic that a sectarian ayatollah is fairer than someone from Western academia, despite \"trying to systematically undermine the value of the canonical readings\" (his words, not mine)?", "In Shadi's Second Canonization, page 132, he states that [Qunbul] was a corrupt chief of police. This quote is from volume 7, pages 284 to 285, in the Abu Huda edition. But when we actually go back to the source he's quoting, we find the exact opposite, which means that he was praised, not that he became corrupt. So a positive statement turned into a negative statement. Shadi, you referenced this page, in this book, in this edition: how the heck did that happen? This is a Harvard professor.",), "Farid Responds, “Exposing Harvard Professor Shady Hekmat Nasser”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SRvc6YARzRY", ((173, "2:53–3:13"), (193, "3:13–3:59"), (245, "4:05–4:47"),))
// U FR172:35-63 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("[Asim] bin Abi Najud is one of the main reciters of the Quran, and his recitation is adhered to by most Muslims today. When we return to Shaadi's Transmission of Variant Readings, page 21, we find him making the unfounded claim that [Asim] is unanimously known to be a weak hadith transmitter. He references Ibn [Hajar's Tahdhib], volume 1, page 627, which has absolutely nothing to do with the claim that he made. He got the volume number wrong, the page number wrong and the information wrong. But this is a recurrent theme of sloppiness that I'm used to when going through Shady Nasser's work.", "In volume 2, page 250 to 251, we find scholars praising him. I find it confusing how Shady Nassar was able to overlook all of this positive information about Asim. Perhaps it has something to do with his bias, or his agenda against the Quran.",), "Farid Responds, “Exposing Harvard Professor Shady Hekmat Nasser”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SRvc6YARzRY", ((89, "1:29–2:06"), (128, "2:08–2:34"),))
// U FR088:1939-2217 | Farid Responds | 9
#passage(("[Bazzar] says that he's not aware of anyone that has dismissed the hadith. [Abu Hatim] and [Abu Zur'a] differed regarding his degree of reliability, with the latter considering him to be trustworthy and the former arguing that he is truthful but not on the level of those described as trustworthy.", "Ahmed says: \"The people of Kufa choose his recitation, and I choose his recitation.\"", "\"Everyone who is named [Asim] becomes weak\"? This was not understood by anyone. He's just making this general statement: the people who are named [Asims] generally have a bad memory. It's a pattern he noticed.", "He said he became [senile] before he died. What's the problem, if it doesn't affect his recitation? Did this affect his recitation? Prove it. Imagine you're teaching the Quran for 40 years, and then your family notices that you're forgetting your phone in the car every time, you're messing up when you're counting money all the time, and then they say: stay at home. You think that someone that is unable to memorize something will be sitting there in the Masjid, and he's mumbling to himself, and he's clearly lost his marbles, and everyone is just going to be going to him and learning the Quran?", "The argument here was that [al-Sulami], who was the main teacher in Kufa for 40 years (by the way, the teacher of Hussein), we don't know where he got his Quran from, and therefore he's unreliable. Actually, when people ask me, I say he took it from [Ali]; the report's clearcut.", "\"His recitation was hated by scholars\": he's referring to one specific example, one word. And then he says the scholars of Arabic language disliked this recitation; they disliked his pronunciation of this word because it conflicts with common recitations.", "Two things. Is this referring to the full Quran, or is this referring to one word? This is one word, it's one example, and we have scholars of Arabic having an issue with it. That's the first point: he took one example, he made it seem like the scholars of the Arabic language have an issue with it.", "Number two: if the main reciter [of Sham] and the main reciter of [Kufa] recited in that specific way (Kufa, Sham; Syria, Iraq), then guess what: anyone that criticized them for that is mistaken. So don't make it seem like they're unreliable or they're going against the Arabic language. These are second-generation scholars of the Quran; they're in different places, and they're taking the Quran from the first-generation Muslims, or from a second-generation Muslim. But either way, they're both living in the first century, in different regions, and they're reciting it in the same way.", "To be honest, I'm not even sure if this is a correct recitation, and I haven't looked it up yet myself. But he took an example of one word and he turned it into \"the scholars hated his Quran\". This is a joke.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((5704, "1:35:04–1:35:41"), (5842, "1:37:22–1:37:40"), (5901, "1:38:21–1:38:50"), (5956, "1:39:16–1:41:20"), (6218, "1:43:38–1:44:26"), (6308, "1:45:08–1:46:17"), (6381, "1:46:21–1:47:14"), (6434, "1:47:14–1:48:46"), (6526, "1:48:46–1:49:09"),))
// U FR088:2333-2360 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Who are the kings of Arabic that thought that Abu [Abd al-Rahman's] recitation was garbage? Someone found a single example of Arabic scholars. We don't even know who they are; we don't have names for these people; we don't know which Arabic scholars. But we have one example, and now we got David Wood regurgitating this.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((6930, "1:55:30–1:56:38"),))
// U FR172:109-151 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Sometimes Shady is just simply incompetent. For example, he provides this little biography about the [rawi] of the recitation of Ibn Amer. One problem, though: this person that he is referring to, there's a century between their deaths; they lived in different regions. This is just shameful. If you're going to write about the canonical reciters, then the least that you should be able to do is identify them correctly.", "Another example of Shadi's incompetence comes in the form of this misquotation from Tafsir Tabari. Shadi says al-Tabari claims that the consensus of the readers of his time is against the variant maliki (of course, I'm referring to the word maliki in Surat al-Fatiha). Then Shadi comments: \"Could the consensus have changed so rapidly, within few years only, between [al-Tabari] and Ibn Mujahid?\" So, according to Shadi, Tabari is saying that reciting maliki is impermissible according to the consensus. What does this mean? It means that the vast majority of Muslims are reciting el Fatiha incorrectly.", "Sure, it's just a little mistake by Shady, and everyone makes mistakes? No. You can't expect people to take you seriously if you're claiming that there's a mistake and all the Muslims are reciting it incorrectly. It's the first variant in the Quran.",), "Farid Responds, “Exposing Harvard Professor Shady Hekmat Nasser”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SRvc6YARzRY", ((294, "4:54–5:25"), (332, "5:32–6:09"), (382, "6:22–6:37"),))

