#passage(("For example, [Yahya] is a very good example of someone that was very harsh against [Hafs], said that he's not trustworthy. However, when Yahya was asked about his recitation, he said that [Hafs's] recitation was even better than Abu Bakr's recitation from [Asim]. Generally, this is a statement based on Yahya bin [Ma'in's] understanding of what the recitation of [Asim] is. So he's saying that he is the person to go to for the recitation. There's even a report: \"We were told that the recitation is the recitation of [Hafs].\" And then you have later scholars like [Dhahabi] and Ibn [Hajar] saying things like: well, [Hafs] is weak in hadith, but he's an imam in recitation.", "Now, how is that possible? How is it possible that major scholars of hadith in the past would make statements like these? Were they not aware that there's one brain, and there's no section for hadith and section for Quran? If you have a bad memory, then you have a bad memory, that's it. How is it possible for someone to have a good memory in the Quran, bad memory in hadith? Well, the thing is, they understood how the transmission of hadith and the Quran worked more than we do. What we're doing today is we're just briefly glossing over some statements in books of biographies of these narrators, and we're coming to these conclusions. They understood the reality of these things; they understood how you can fall into mistakes in hadith.", "Briefly, one of the main causes for someone to be weak in hadith is to not care about hadith; don't care about your own hadith. So picture this: you're someone who started to collect hadiths when you're 20 or mid-20s, even perhaps earlier. You write them down when you're young. Now you're a prominent person when you're older, when you're in your 60s, your 70s, when people start coming to you asking you about your hadith. Why? Because you're an old person. And they find you, and they say: you have some hadith that you heard a while ago. And you start reciting some of what you've heard a long time ago, or you go through some of your papers that you've written ages ago, and you start sharing some of the hadith that you have. Now, if you're not someone who revises your narrations frequently, you will make mistakes. Sometimes you'll make mistakes on the names of the narrators; sometimes you'll make mistakes when it comes to connecting a hadith that's supposed to be disconnected.", "And that's the thing: it's so different than his recitation. When he recites the Quran, he's constantly teaching people the Quran; this is something that he does on a daily basis. He was one of the closest people to [Asim] ever, and due to this, it's something that he took from [Asim] again, and something that he gave to the people again and again on a daily basis. And he was a major imam. And due to that, you cannot compare his narration of the hadith to his recitation of the Quran.", "And there are different examples, not just [Hafs] himself. So Hisham bin [Ammar], the famous reciter, one of the main rawies of the recitation of [Ibn Amir], he used to also make mistakes in hadith, and he was criticized by some of the major hadith critics. One of them was Abu [Hatim], and when Abu Hatim was asked, he said: the main issue with Hisham is that Hisham didn't revise his [ahadith]. So that's one of the main reasons why one can be weak in hadith and reliable in the Quran. It's as simple as that: they didn't focus on it, they didn't review it; it's something that they left on the side, and they mainly focus on the Quran.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting Missionaries on the Quran Recitation / Hafs 'an Asim”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lHwC71c2m5c", ((169, "2:49–3:53"), (233, "3:53–4:52"), (295, "4:55–6:05"), (378, "6:18–6:51"), (411, "6:51–7:43"),))
// U FR088:805-849 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("When he says \"more reliable\", is he referring to the Quran or hadith? [Abu Bakr] is more reliable in [hadith] specifically; that's what he's saying. This is from arguably the most important critic against [Hafs].",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((2241, "37:21–37:58"),))
// U FR088:1246-1318 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("You don't really take narrations from young hadith narrators; they don't really matter. You take from them when they're old, because you want a short isnad.", "The only criticism, or at least the only thing that was shown so far: [Ibn Khirash] said he's a liar, rejected, fabricating hadith. He's not a major hadith critic, one. And, number two, you have scholars just rejecting what he says.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((3528, "58:48–59:00"), (3587, "59:47–1:01:08"),))
// U FR088:1692-1749 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("Someone is a master at identifying fingerprints at crime scenes, but that person happens to be a drunkard. Does that mean that we need to toss away the whole field of crime scene investigation, or the whole science behind identifying fingerprints? No. We're not saying that the guy is drunk while identifying fingerprints. And similarly, if the statement is correctly attributed (which I doubt), if [Yahya] is saying that [Hafs] is a liar, and yet he's saying that [Hafs] has the best recitation from Asim, that means that he didn't lie in his recitation; that means that he's correct in his recitation. It's not confusing.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((4987, "1:23:07–1:24:36"),))
// U FR088:1829-1864 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("By the way, if you notice, none of the hadith scholars here called [Hafs a] liar. Those that weaken this hadith, they don't call him a liar; they just say [Hafs] is the only narrator of this hadith, and due to this we don't accept it. They say things like that.", "So he says these narrations, they're not verified or not known. I don't even think that is referring to this specific hadith; I don't think that is accusing him for this report, but I need to check. Is he calling him a liar, or he's saying he's weak? He says: because he's the sole narrator of this hadith, we [can't] accept it, because there's a possibility that it's wrong; there's a good possibility that it's wrong.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((5322, "1:28:42–1:29:02"), (5352, "1:29:12–1:30:11"),))
// U FR088:862-996 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("This can be understood in two specific ways. One is that he is actually fabricating reports that he's attributing to Simak; that's one way of understanding it. The other way of understanding it is that he would narrate reports that he may have heard from somewhere else, and he would attribute them to Simak. So, for example, let's just say Jordan lies a lot (hypothetically speaking), and I hear Jordan's lies, and I'm narrating hadiths through Jordan, and I accidentally attribute them to Muhammad Dolan. So what I'm doing here is I'm attributing to Muhammad Dolan what I actually heard from Jordan.", "The literal translation: \"he narrated from Simak and others false narrations\". And this is what he translated: \"he narrates from Simak and others fabricated hadith\". Actually, this is correct; there's no problem with the translation here. The problem is with the understanding, because what he's doing is he's claiming that this is a charge by Saji that [Hafs] is fabricating. The quote doesn't say he fabricated hadith; the quote is saying he narrated from Simak and others fabricated hadith. So there's a difference between that and \"he fabricated hadith\".", "The claim is he attributed some things to him that were false, that he never said. This, by the way, is a severe mistake that would cause someone to reject [Hafs] as a reliable transmitter. So how does this happen? This happens because you spend your day listening to many people narrating hadiths; you may spend weeks, months listening to people narrating hadiths; you memorize the hadiths, and sometimes you confuse who you took the hadith from. Now, it's not necessarily a problem at the time; it's a problem 10 years from now, 20 years from now, 30 years from now: someone says, \"What's that hadith that you narrated about pudding being tasty?\" And [Hafs] is like, \"Oh yeah, I heard it from Simak\" (hypothetically). So [Hafs] makes the mistake of attributing the false tradition to [Simak]. That's the mistake. That's what you can understand these words to mean; you don't have to understand it to be a fabrication.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((2414, "40:14–41:48"), (2517, "41:57–43:15"), (2684, "44:44–45:59"),))
// U FR088:1339-1439 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("He says that [Ibn Hibban] said he used to forge and fabricate chains of narration. I never came across that. This master of Arabic is saying this.", "Now, this statement: if you're going to translate it literally, it means flip; it means flip the chain. So, for example, he would mention a chain with a report that's supposed to be the chain of another report. Now, is this something that's done intentionally? About another narrator called Muhammed Abdah, he would say: he used to flip chains with a lack of understanding due to the weakness of his memorization. He also says: he was a good man, but used to flip narrations. So flipping a report, I know it's a weird description, but this does not mean that he's lying. Now, this is translated as \"he used to forge and fabricate\". Doesn't say that in Arabic.", "Now, this is not you; I'm pretty sure this is [Shadi] who did this. But you, master in Arabic, you're supposed to catch this. I assume the best of you, and I think that you relied on [Shadi], who I think wrote something very similar, if not identical, and what Shadi did is he manipulated the text. But you see what you did that was messed up? Don't write the original reference; write the secondary reference that you're quoting, because people need to know that you're not going to the original source, you're going to a secondary source written by a Western academic. And you're trying to show off as if you're going to the original source. You read articles that you don't understand, and you're relying on twisted translations; that's your level. That's my criticism. I'm not going to accuse you of lying; that's his problem.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((3736, "1:02:16–1:02:33"), (3801, "1:03:21–1:05:09"), (3934, "1:05:34–1:07:53"),))
// U FR225:324-493 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("There are two people who have (or at least who has been attributed to that) said that [Hafs] is a liar. So the main concern is really: how is it possible that this major scholar referred to [Hafs] as a liar?", "Now, I did some research into this, and there are six students that have quoted him sharing his opinions. The only person who quotes Yahya referring to [Hafs] as a liar is [Ibn Muhriz]. That's the first thing.", "Secondly, his quote from Yahya: this is the actual quote. He says: the recitation of Abu Umar is more solid than that of Abu Bakr, and Abu Bakr was more truthful. This Abu Umar is a liar. And this is my challenge to anyone: provide an example of a lie that's recited by [Hafs]. And if you cannot find an example, then why are you still bringing this argument up? People that make this claim, they often repeat this claim, but they never bring an example.", "Now I have something more to add. That quote I just provided, that says that recitation of [Hafs] is better than the recitation of etc., however Abu Bakr is more truthful, more accurate, more trustworthy: there are two other students of Yahia that quote the same thing. You'll find the quote by Ali, you'll find the quote by Leith. However, they don't quote him saying that.", "So where does that quote come from? The quote is something that Yahya is asked about separately. Now, here is something really interesting: it opens up a possibility that Yahya may have been speaking about someone else. And I've actually found someone who he may have been referring to, and who is actually a better candidate to be referred to as a liar. That's just a theory so far, and it's nothing set in stone, but at the moment I'm leaning towards this; it makes a lot of sense to me. You had a narrator called Abu Umar al-Bazar. He used to be one of the officers of the notorious liar false prophet who used to claim that he used to be able to see the unseen. So that person was accused of [lying], and it seems to be quite likely that [Yahya] may have been referring to him. And none of the major critics of hadith call [Hafs] a liar.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting Missionaries on the Quran Recitation / Hafs 'an Asim”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lHwC71c2m5c", ((741, "12:21–13:17"), (800, "13:20–14:11"), (854, "14:14–16:01"), (992, "16:32–17:31"), (1062, "17:42–20:20"),))
// U FR088:1463-1676 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("To summarize: I doubt the attribution, and I've provided my reasons as to why I'm doubting it.", "They cut this clip out of me affirming this attribution to [Yahya]; they cut out the five minutes of me rejecting this, or claiming that there's a problem with this, and stating that all the other students of [Yahya] don't quote this, and that there is another person that most likely is the person he's talking about. He cuts it out and makes it seem like this is something that's said in stone about [Yahya]. Why are you doing this? I thought that this was an airtight topic; why do you need to be deceptive?",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((4686, "1:18:06–1:18:23"), (4756, "1:19:16–1:20:57"),))
// U FR225:494-598 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("If you're someone who's studying this stuff, you should know that this alleged quote is supposed to be from [Ibn] Hibban. This is not about the chains of transmissions of the Quran. It doesn't say anything about fabricating reports, and if you go back to the source (it's available, it's printed, it's online; all you got to do is check it out), there's nothing here about him fabricating chains.", "In any case, he's saying that he's connecting disconnected reports. That's due to mistake; that's a form of a mistake that narrators make. You hear a hadith from Muhammad bin [Sirin], from Abu Hurayrah, and that's it. However, you assume that: Muhammad bin Sirin, from Abu Hurairah, from Rasulullah. You make that assumption; you think it's a connected hadith, when it's a statement. It's a mistake; it's not intentional.", "A third century scholar of the Quran and hadith, when asked, said that Hafs was the most knowledgeable in the recitation of [Asim]. Ibn Munadi, who was born in the middle of the third century, said the same thing: the recitation of [Hafs] is even superior to the recitation of Abu Bakr.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting Missionaries on the Quran Recitation / Hafs 'an Asim”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lHwC71c2m5c", ((1262, "21:02–22:12"), (1353, "22:33–22:57"), (1455, "24:15–24:35"),))
// U FR225:233-307 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("It's something that you do find in much of [Hafs's] biographies. The earliest work that we get (it's earlier than any of the others that mentioned this specific report of stealing books): it's actually under the chapter about [Hafs] Muslim, not Sulaiman, so it's a different [Hafs]. However, due to the similarity, there was confusion, and that's the origin of the story.", "But even that: someone taking a book and someone not returning it, that's not necessarily theft in that sense of the word. I don't think others have considered [Hafs] to be a thief or to be a liar.", "This is about them saying that he wrote down the hadith, or copied the hadith. Unless he writes down that he heard from those people that he didn't hear, it's not a lie; it's not a fabrication. The only issue is if he actually says \"I heard from those people\" and he didn't hear from those people; then it would be a problem. But there's no indication of that happening in the report in the first place, so it's not an issue.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting Missionaries on the Quran Recitation / Hafs 'an Asim”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lHwC71c2m5c", ((530, "8:50–9:33"), (577, "9:37–9:53"), (664, "11:04–11:39"),))
// U FR088:2879-2954 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("If we assume that this statement is correctly attributed to him (I'm not affirming that he said this), then when he says that the best person is a liar, does he mean that we cannot rely on anything from [Hafs], we cannot rely on any recitation? Is that the intention from the claim? Or is the intention from the claim: despite him being a [liar], he's completely reliable in the recitation of the Quran? Clearly it's [the] second, because there's no reason to even say something like \"the best is a liar\"; that would be rejecting all recitations. Why doesn't he say there are no proper recitations from [Asim], for example?", "This guy, it's been three years, and he's still unable to provide a single example of [Hafs] lying in a recitation.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((8441, "2:20:41–2:21:44"), (8594, "2:23:14–2:23:31"),))
// U FR088:3052-3081 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("If you ever hear anything about [Hafs], tell people that there's no evidence of him lying ever in a recitation, let alone even hadith. You'll find classical scholars saying this hadith is weak, but the claim that there is something definite about [Hafs] lying: never come across anything like that. When narrators speak about [Hafs], they just say things like: he's weak, he's abandoned, he's rejected, we can't rely on him for hadith. That's more or less it. But in regards to accusing him of [lying], they don't do that.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((8990, "2:29:50–2:30:34"),))
// U FR225:694-706 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("When anyone comes to you with the accusation that [Hafs] is a liar (your Quran is not preserved because you're relying on [Hafs]), tell them: where did [Hafs] lie? Give me one verse, one word, one letter, one tashkeel that's a false recitation. Just give me one. And they never do.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting Missionaries on the Quran Recitation / Hafs 'an Asim”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lHwC71c2m5c", ((1707, "28:27–28:52"),))

