#passage(("Once again, I feel like Hollen is simply unfamiliar with the material. The transmissions to [Hafs] that he's referring to here mainly have to do with matters that have to do with application and tone; this has nothing to do with differences of opinions being attributed to [Hafs] himself.",), "Farid Responds, “Yasir Qadhi: \"The Standard Narrative has Holes\" - A Response to Orientalists”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=B0GGbZeGQOw", ((1485, "24:45–25:03"),))
// U FR225:613-662 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("What kind of argument is this? He was earlier, therefore people relied on him more? That makes absolutely no sense. Is Jay referring to the other rawies? Because they all knew [Asim] personally; they all took their recitations from him, and yet [Hafs] was chosen.", "You guys have access to these books: all praising the recitation, saying that [Hafs] was the most accurate in the transmission of the recitation of [Asim]. Why [aren't] you just sharing that to your audience? Let them make a decision, instead of coming up with these theories like \"he passed away before others\", which isn't even true.",), "Farid Responds, “Refuting Missionaries on the Quran Recitation / Hafs 'an Asim”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lHwC71c2m5c", ((1533, "25:33–26:18"), (1585, "26:25–27:01"),))
// U FR088:1185-1231 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Sometimes you'll find some additional matters; sometimes you'll find the eloquence of the verse being elevated because of the additional meanings. So it really depends on the verse.", "He's saying that the [Ottoman] Empire was the main reason for the [Hafs] recitation spreading. Personally, I haven't seen evidence against this. I wouldn't be surprised, because here's the thing: even the [Hanafi] was spread mostly through the [Ottoman] Empire. Does it change anything in terms of reliability? No, doesn't change anything.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((3306, "55:06–55:19"), (3357, "55:57–56:58"),))
// U FR088:601-718 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("The idea is that almost every single time that a quranic verse is quoted in a book (in a hadith book or whatever), you will find the verse being quoted written as the most common recitation, and it's readily available: you find it quickly, copy paste, get the book printed. And this is one of the most common mistakes that editors will make, because what the editor needs to do is provide the text in the way that the author himself had it written. So if the author himself wasn't reciting [Hafs], then you're not supposed to have it.", "Of course, in many cases there's not going to be any difference, in the vast majority of cases. Here's a good example. Imagine a scholar is speaking about the verse that speaks about [wudu], meaning: what it implies is that you should wash your feet. But you'll find the author commenting, saying this verse is understood to mean wipe the feet. You see, that's where the problem is. Some editors, they will fall into that mistake. The seasoned editor that knows what he's doing, that's worked on editing in the past, someone who's qualified to do so, they won't make that mistake. But this is actually a very common mistake made by amateurs, and this is something that all [editors] need to avoid. But does this affect the transmission of the Quran? Does this cause the Quran to not be preserved? Of course not.", "David thinks that this has to do with reliability. If a specific scholar is quoting [Nafi'], and the book has the recitation of [Hafs], it's got nothing to do with reliability. You got to write it down in the way the author intended it to be written; that's what you're supposed to do. Doesn't matter if [Hafs] was there with the Prophet (peace be upon him) and he was recording it with audio; it wouldn't make a difference.",), "Farid Responds, “Free Education for David Wood (Condensed to 2.5h)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hBH4MpJeYIw", ((1599, "26:39–27:51"), (1674, "27:54–30:14"), (1858, "30:58–32:01"),))

=== 26.9 Examples of variant readings and their meanings
// NODE P05.C03.S09
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML256:145-211 | The Muslim Lantern | 6
#passage(("So, the Quran came down, and while it came down in these 7 different dialects, also Allahﷻ added more new features in these 7 Ahruf that were revealed. For example, He revealed certain words different from the first revelation that He revealed; these words increase the meaning, or bring an additional meaning that enriches the meaning of the Quran. For example, the Quran says in the first chapter, Surah (alFatihah1:4): مَـٰلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ | مَلِكِ يَوْمِ ٱلدِّينِ (The Owner of the Day of Judgement | The King of the Day of Judgement). These two meanings do not contradict one another; they enrich the meaning of the Quran further.", "And also, these dialects had different ways of reciting the same word: so it's the same exact word, with the same diacritical marks and everything, but it's just the way you pronounce it that is different. For example, you say وَٱلضُّحَىٰ and you say وَٱلضُّحَي, depending on the dialect of the Arab tribe of the time.", "And also, Allahﷻ had certain words read in a different way to also enrich and increase the meaning. For example, when Moses came to Pharaoh in the Quran, and he showed him the signs, like the stick and all of the signs that Moses showed Pharaoh, he said to him in one recitation (alisra17:102): لَقَدْ عَلِمْتَ (You know that no one revealed these signs except the Lord of the Heavens and the Earth). So here in one recitation Moses is telling Pharaoh \"You know\", and in another recitation he says: لَقَدْ عَلِمْتُ (I know). Moses is speaking from the first person here, that he knows that no one else can reveal these Miracles except the lord of the heavens and the Earth. So here we have an enriched meaning added to this verse: that Moses himself, he knows, he believes, he's certain that these signs came from the Creator; and also, the other recitation is that Pharaoh also knows this, even though he's denying. So that is enriching and increasing the meaning of the Quran.", "Abdullah ibn Mas'ud was actually one of the four people that the Prophetﷺ recommended that people take the Quran from. There's a Hadith of the Prophetﷺ, he said: \"Take the Quran from 4 people\", i.e. these four people are the best reciters: Abdullah ibn Mas'ud, Ubay ibn Ka'b, Muadh ibn Jabal, and Salim. Abdullah ibn Mas'ud said: \"I've seen the reciters in the beginning\", i.e., \"I've seen these different Ahruf, these different dialects of the Arab, these different Revelations that came from Allahﷻ, and they're very close! They're like هلُمَّ and أقْبِل\". Both of these two words mean the same thing: they mean \"Come\", but it's just different ways to say the same thing. This was essentially the 7 Ahruf: the 7 Ahruf were 7 dialects that also added more unique words that enrich the meaning of the Quran, and unique ways of reciting certain words that add and increase the meaning of the Quran.", "Now, the very important thing to understand here is that all of these different things are all considered Quran. So, when we say \"there's only one Quran\", there's only one Quran! because that is the same text, but it is recited in different ways; these differences are all coming down from Allah. So, Quran is the recitation of the text of the Revelation that comes from Allahﷻ; that's what the Quran is. And all of these different Ahruf, all of these unique words like \"مَـٰلِكِ\" and \"مَلِكِ\", or \"عَلِمْتُ\" and \"عَلِمْتَ\", or \"تَبَيَّنُوا\" and \"تَثَبَّتُوا\": all of these are coming from Allah, from Angel Gabriel, and the Prophetﷺ recited them, word for word. So, when you look at different Qiraat, or you hear different ways of reciting certain things like \"وَٱلضُّحَىٰ\", \"وَٱلضُّحَى\", these are all coming from the Prophetﷺ; they're all Revelation coming from Allah.", "Unlike the differences that you have in other religions and manuscripts: those differences are Corruptions! They're not coming from the source; they are coming later on, added by individuals. And Allahﷻ said: (alMuzzamil73:20) فَٱقْرَءُوا۟ مَا تَيَسَّرَ مِنَ ٱلْقُرْءَانِ (Recite that which is easy for you). So, you can recite from these 7 different dialects, whatever is easy for you. From these words that are revealed, you can choose to say \"مَلِكِ\" if you want to say \"مَلِكِ\"; you can choose to say \"مَـٰلِكِ\" if you want to say \"مَـٰلِكِ\"; that's up to you. These are all things which are accessible, because the Prophetﷺ, he gave you that permission, and the whole reason for them to be revealed is to make things easy for the people.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The History Of The Quranic Text| A Breakdown From Revelation To Compilation| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JjBR2JbHN6o", ((623, "10:23–10:57"), (657, "10:57–11:12"), (676, "11:16–12:16"), (743, "12:23–13:21"), (808, "13:28–14:15"), (861, "14:21–15:01"),))
