#passage(("So the first question is: could this be a mistake? And it's pretty much impossible for this to be a mistake. Now, I find it hard to believe that the first generation of Muslims were unaware of the difference between a king and owner, and they got confused by both words; that's simply something that I cannot fathom.", "Another interesting thing to keep in mind here is that [Kisa'i], who recites it as [Maaliki], is a student of Hamza's, who narrates it as [Maliki]. This is not some sort of screw-up or some sort of a confusion, but rather he recited in this way because he's aware that Rasoolallah recited that in both manners. You also find multiple early scholars simply stating that [Rasulullah] recited it in both ways as well. Of course, keep in mind that the reciters, they're basically following the Prophet, peace be upon him, in his own recitation; this is not something that they're doing from their own selves.", "It's also very important to keep in mind that not every recitation ends up being accepted. So just because someone somewhere recited something doesn't mean it gets into the Quran. The reason why these [recitations are] accepted is because they were correctly attributed, and they were mass transmitted.", "That combination of maliki yawmi, that's called the [idgham] Kabir, and this is mainly found in eastern Arabian tribes at the time, of course, and middle Arabia tribes. These include tribes like Tamim. So this as well shows that this is intentional.",), "Farid Responds, “Are the Qiraat mistakes?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K11XfOfTFAU", ((244, "4:04–4:43"), (283, "4:43–5:53"), (355, "5:55–6:34"), (435, "7:15–7:44"),))
// U FR079:268-301 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("I'll tell you what's so interesting about King versus owner, because you'd say: what's the point? What are we gaining from this idea of King versus owner? And Subhan Allah, every time you find a difference in meaning, you'll find some amazing reason behind it, and it's amazing how it adds to the eloquence. When talking about King versus owner, he says: notice this. An owner is, of course, someone that owns something, but they're not necessarily in a very high position. But a king is always in high position, but sometimes the king doesn't have full control, even though an owner has full control of what they're controlling. But if it's king and owner, then it's someone of a high position that has full control. I enjoy that one.",), "Farid Responds, “Why not assume the Qira'at are just mistakes?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=uU1C23atotE", ((897, "14:57–16:15"),))
// U FR268:154-216 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("[Sirat] is pronounced three different ways. Now, the difference between these three, again, have to do with dialects. To the people of the time, it would be pronounced with a [sin] according to most tribes, or with a [sad]: that's how the [Quraysh] spoke.", "So what do we take away from this? The existence of these recitation, and [them] fitting into these dialects, shows that these are intentional. Not only are they intentional, but they are what the Prophet, peace be upon him, intended. Also keep in mind that there are some very famous dialects that never ended up being endorsed by the Quran. So keep in mind that the dialects were revealed: dialects that had to be confirmed. These are not the dialects of random tribes just putting in their twist.",), "Farid Responds, “Are the Qiraat mistakes?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=K11XfOfTFAU", ((493, "8:13–8:48"), (648, "10:48–11:39"),))
