#passage(("Who recited like that? And the reason I'm asking is because the ten reciters, all ten, from all the main regions, read with the existence of the [diacritical] marks to read it. Why should we accept your alternative recitation if you don't even know who it's based on?",), "Farid Responds, “Debate with Quran-only Scholar Edip Yüksel”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=WL8QjGB7s7w", ((297, "4:57–5:20"),))
// U FR080:255-329 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("It's very strange that Sam says that this exists outside the [Uthmanic] codex, because that's absolutely false. This recitation was recited, and these are two of the seven canonical recitations, and of course they both fit into the [Uthmanic] skeleton. So Sam's issue isn't with preservation; his issue is: it's just a theological point against Islam. He's arguing about Allah being astonished and whatnot; that stuff has got nothing to do with this part of the debate. And by the way, the term doesn't necessarily mean that someone is ignorant of something. [Ibn Taymiyyah] himself is saying that you can have ['ajab] towards something even if it's something that you are aware of, if it's something that is rare, a rare occurrence.", "First of all, 20th century translators and their opinions, that's not sufficient to establish what is the Quran and what isn't. Now, question to you: is this recitation found in any classical quranic manuscript? Well, no. Then is it available in any canonical recitation? No. How about this: do you find any authentic chain to a companion that recites this? No, again. Well, then you cannot assume that this is a part of the Quran, and you cannot make the claim that the Quran is not preserved due to this, because you're assuming that the whole nation of Muslims just ignored a verse that they memorized, that they were aware of, or that there's some sort of a reason for them to intentionally drop it. Again, it seems like Sam is just finding any strange thing that he's come across in classical works and using it as an argument against Islam, even though he doesn't really know where the argument is going.",), "Farid Responds, “Response to Sam Shamoun Fresh & Fit Debate on Quranic Preservation”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=j8wo1OmaDZU", ((715, "11:55–13:11"), (842, "14:02–15:23"),))
