// U FR258:91-244 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("There are a few things I'd like to point out in regards to the multiple recitations. Firstly, do we reject every single recitation that we do not recite today as a false recitation? No, we don't do that. And I don't think David understands or appreciates that when we find recitations that don't fit into the consonantal skeleton of the Quran, we don't simply dismiss it; we look into the chain, and we look into evidence, to determine if it's correctly attributed to the Prophet, peace be upon him, or not.", "In regards to this specific recitation, as David pointed out, it's recited by Abu [al-Darda], who clearly says: I heard this from Rasulullah. This is one of the recitations, one of these seven recitations that the Prophet, peace be upon him, taught, so there should be no doubts about that. This chapter is one of the shortest chapters of the Quran, and these three major scholars made the same mistake in the same place, and it's like the third verse in the chapter? It's impossible for them to make this mistake, so we can scratch that off.", "Secondly, the majority of the Muslims at the time recited it. What does this mean? It means that this is also correctly attributed to Rasulullah. Again, there is more proof for the common recitation that we recite today than the recitation that bin Masood and Abu [al-Darda] recite. Conclusion: both are from Rasulullah; not one, both.", "What's really frustrating is: David sees these two recitations, and he immediately picks the one that Muslims don't recite. He says this must be the correct one, this must be the false one, because surely what Muslims are doing now must be false. There's no reason; you haven't given us a reason to reject the common recitation, apart from: [Ibn Mas'ud] is one of the main reciters of the Quran. The people of Islam as a whole recite differently.", "So now that we've accepted that, one will naturally ask: why would he recite both recitations? What does that have to do with the verses? Now, if you look at the verses, you'll come to a conclusion. The following verses clearly speak of the relationship between the actions of man and the actions of God, and how they're interlinked: if man gives, then God will make doing good easy for him. Now, due to this connection, we can only assume that the reason behind the variant recitations in this specific verse is to draw an emphasis. As we can see here, in the recitation by Ibn [Mas'ud], the emphasis is upon man and woman; this lines up quite nicely with the verses that follow it, which speak of the actions of man and woman. Now, in the recitation of the rest of the sahaba, the common recitation that we recite today, the emphasis is upon God: when we look at the verses that come after it, we notice that God's actions here are linked with the actions of man. Thus the emphasis. And that's why the [qira'at] are so beautiful.", "\"Anyone who doubts a word or even a letter is not a Muslim.\" Is he referring to rejecting a recitation that you're not familiar with? La, he's not. The Quran was revealed in seven modes. If you heard one mode, and you're not familiar with the other modes, and you reject a recitation, it doesn't make you an apostate; it just makes you unaware. So in regards to Abu [al-Darda]: when he came across a recitation that he was not aware of (which is, subhanallah, the recitation that we recite today), he said: I heard [Rasulullah], so I'm not going to recite it the way you recite it. That's natural.", "When we find these recitations, we look into them; we try to understand the wisdom behind them, the history behind them. We don't just shrug it off and say: oh, no one read this, let's edit it out of our books. We don't do that.",), "Farid Responds, “Muslim Scholars Hiding Different Quran?! Surat al-Layl [Response to David Wood]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3utz4WvILfk", ((221, "3:41–4:20"), (260, "4:20–5:05"), (308, "5:08–5:47"), (381, "6:21–6:54"), (414, "6:54–8:25"), (513, "8:33–9:26"), (582, "9:42–9:55"),))

