#passage(("First of all, you find that [bariyya] and [bari'a] are the same exact word: this is like saying [nabi] and, in another recitation, [nabi']. Originally, some of these words would have a hamza; however, with usage, the Arabs drop the hamza. So it's the same words. That's the first thing.", "Now, according to Fadi, he says that it's supposed to say: those are the worst of the innocent. And then it says: indeed, they who have believed and done righteous deeds, those are the best of the innocent. [Bari'a] here means the innocent? Well, no, it doesn't.",), "Farid Responds, “Dr. Jay Smith and Al Fadi are Embarrassing”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=a3gPpl-ixPU", ((268, "4:28–5:06"), (340, "5:40–6:14"),))
// U FR265:1-247 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("Very, very briefly, in regards to this word: one is [bariyya] and one is [bari'a], and both words mean the exact same thing. The verse says, speaking about disbelievers: they are the worst of creation. And Fadi claims that this means they are the worst of the innocent, which doesn't mean anything. More importantly, it doesn't only mean they are the worst of the innocent; it also means they are the worst of the innocent female, and it's in a singular form.", "Shabbir Ali is not saying that the pronunciation changed after the time of the Prophet, peace be upon him. It was originally used as [bari'a]; however, it also became pronounced as [bariyya]. During the time of the Prophet, peace be upon him, both pronunciations existed; hence there are both. Both of these are recitation; this isn't something that happened later. So please be careful, and don't misrepresent people in order to score some points.", "It has no theological impact, because the two words mean the exact same thing. It has theological impact according to your interpretation, according to your silly, ridiculous interpretation, which doesn't even fit in with a structure of the verse. Yes, it would have theological impact there; however, if it's the exact same word, there obviously is no theological impact.", "Lane is going to refute [Fadi], who's absolutely oblivious to what's going on. So if we were to substitute this word with, for example, [khalq], which also means creation, would that conflict with the structure of the verse? [Khalq] is not a feminine term. If we were to use a correct word, it would be [abriya'].", "[Bariyya] is singular, right, but it's referring to a plural, and the same way one would say \"fish\" while referring to plural, or saying \"sheep\" and referring to a plural. It's the same idea: it's plural in that sense, even if the word in itself is singular. And the same cannot be applied to [bari'a]. This is Arabic 101.", "So, once again, here's what it says: the creation, as meaning the beings or things that are created, or particularly mankind; pronounced without a hamza, originally with. They mean the exact same thing.",), "Farid Responds, “Is al-Fadi a Scholar?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kaPF47X0Evs", ((34, "0:34–1:27"), (168, "2:48–3:37"), (232, "3:52–4:17"), (278, "4:38–7:57"), (509, "8:29–9:16"), (624, "10:24–11:00"),))

