#passage(("One of the craziest examples of Christian Prince's lies and manipulation: he's arguing that the first chapter of the Quran speaks about farting. Yes, I kid you not; this is how low this person is.", "There are no examples of that ever happening. Now yes, [durat] means farting, but what Muslims recite is [sirat]. Yes, Christian Prince does know the difference; these are two completely different words. [Zirat] is a different way of saying [sirat], and it's the pronunciation of the tribe of [Qays]. Christian Prince is reading a line that clearly uses the word [zirat], and he's reading it as [durat].", "One of the really interesting parts in this discussion: the person that's discussing with CP asks him to click on the link, and CP refuses to click on the link. So why does he refuse to click on the link? There's definitely something odd going on. The only link that comes up is this, the only link on the Internet, and this is what he was quoting. This is why he didn't want to click on it: when you click on the link, it says [surut] is the plural for [sirat], and this makes perfect sense. So that's a clear evidence that there's some manipulation going on. I'm going to type in [durat], meaning farting: no preview available for this page. Why is there no preview for this page? I was just on the same page. So there's something fishy going on here. I think you owe us an explanation: why didn't you click on the page, and why is this preview page unavailable?", "Christian Prince argues that Muslims read this verse as meaning \"guide us to the straight path of farting\". One, he says Muslims recite this, scholars recite this. The second is that he says that Allah [revealed] this as one of the recitations. And thirdly, he shows a Google result but will not click on a link, deceiving the person that he's speaking to, and he knows very well if he clicks on the link he's going to be exposed. So there are at least three lies by Christian Prince here. The fourth possibility, and I'm not sure if this is from him or from someone else: there clearly appears to be a manipulation on the website.", "Now that's really low. Anyone with a shred of dignity would not use such a silly and low argument. The context itself refutes this absurd understanding: what does farting have to do with any of this? How low can you get? And again, this guy is the teacher of David Wood and the Christian apologists.",), "Farid Responds, “Christian Prince: Verse of Farting [Lies #19-22]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=UP8z2pdej1U", ((3, "0:03–0:28"), (53, "0:53–2:27"), (261, "4:21–6:55"), (438, "7:18–8:30"), (510, "8:30–9:14"),))
// U FR323:1-108 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("The [basmala] is not a verse. The only time when it could be a verse is when it is read in the Fatiha, but there's even debate on whether the basmala is the first ayah of the Fatiha or it's just read before the first ayah of the Fatiha.", "You know that a person has no idea about a language when they have to resort to Google Translate. \"Be ism\": there's no such thing; no Arab ever said that. It would be dividing two words: ism, which means name, and B, which is a preposition, and this preposition has the meaning of [with]. You would be dividing these two words apart.", "CP claims that we as Muslims historically removed the [alif] from the basmala so that it can be easier to read. Ironically, the same text that he holds on the screen refutes him, because he speaks of the expression [iqra'] bismi rabbika, which is found in surah 96, surat al-Alaq, which is literally the first piece of revelation. In this ayah, to be even more precise, the Aleph is written. And why is that so? Well, the same text CP has opened, and that he just read partially in front of us, says, in translation: \"unlike His words, 'Read in the name of your Lord', because it, the letter Aleph, is not removed due to the small amount of usage.\" So in other words, the Elif remains because the expression bismi rabbika is not used often.", "Another thing that we can notice here is that, despite the fact that the Aleph is found there, we still say bismi. Separate like this, like \"be ism\", then they will combine and become a connection in phonetics, become \"bismi\". For example, we can have another word: [ibn] means son. If I want to say \"to someone's son\", I would say [libni]; I wouldn't say [li-ibni]. This is a rule in Arabic grammar.", "But really the main point is: had the Muslims really wanted to remove the Elif so that the expression can be pronounced easier, they would have done the same thing in surah 96, which is simply not the case. The Elif is still found there, and yet we pronounce it bismi. The same way that we pronounce bismillah r-rahman r-rahim, we pronounce bismi rabbika. It's really simple as that.",), "Farid Responds, “Christian Prince fails to read: In the name of God [Lie #99]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Rajbrqw4W44", ((5, "0:05–0:49"), (76, "1:16–1:58"), (153, "2:33–3:30"), (213, "3:33–4:12"), (252, "4:12–4:34"),))

=== 26.12 Early manuscripts and the Sana'a palimpsest
// NODE P05.C03.S12
#author("The Muslim Lantern")
// U ML256:561-638 | The Muslim Lantern | 8
#passage(("What about the written text? Do we have any evidence that the written text is also preserved? The Quran was always an oral text; this is how the companions understood it. There were always these chains of narrations being preserved, because we always looked at it as an oral text. But even the written text, if you look at it, it's also another sign of the preservation of the Quran.", "For example, you have the Birmingham manuscript, which is a parchment that was either written in the life of the Prophetﷺ or his companions. If you compare it with what we have today, it is the exact thing that we have today. This parchment very likely could be one of the parchments that the Prophetﷺ asked one of the companions to come and write down when the revelation was coming down, because it's from his lifetime, and it is exactly in accordance with what we read today. And we have the Topkapi manuscript of the Quran, which has the same exact Quran that we're reciting today. When we talk about the written text, it has the same text as Uthmanic text; there is no difference. This Uthmanic skeletal text is the same exact thing.", "You have the San'aa manuscript, and it is very interesting, because when it was discovered it's 15,000 different parchments that trace to almost 950 different Qurans from the first century onwards. And when you look at all of these manuscripts, they show only one thing: that the Quran that we're reciting today is the same Quran that was there from the beginning. There were many non-Muslim textual criticism scholars who went to San'aa and examined the San'aa manuscripts. All of them say that the Quran that we have today is the same Quran that existed at the time of the Prophetﷺ. These non-Muslims, they went, and they actually investigated and have written books about the San'aa manuscript and how they show the preservation of the Quran.", "Now, what about the San'aa manuscript and the fact that there is something called an \"upper text\" and a \"lower text\"? A lot of people don't understand what is \"upper text\" and \"lower text\" and why there are differences. The Quran was written on what? Parchments, and bones, and whatever was accessible. People didn't have papers. What people used to do is they used to wipe down the ink on whatever they had; they used to wash it and then write again. The upper and lower text that you find in some of these parchments regarding the San'aa manuscript: it's because those people who were writing down, they were wiping down the previous text and then writing a new text when they made a mistake, on the same parchment, on the same bone. That's why you might find slight disagreements there in the lower text.", "That's one thing. The other thing is: these were 950 different Qurans, and 15,000 parchments. They were not professional scribes; many of these parchments were written by people personally writing for their own benefit, like at home, or learning from their teachers, at a school, writing down. They will have scribal errors, because they're not professional scribes.", "But how do we know that these are scribal errors and not changes in the text? There's a very simple way that any beginner textual criticism scholar knows: you look at any change or difference within that text, and then you compare it with a text older than it, a text from the same time, a text that came later on. If you look at the text from the same time, text from before, and text that came later on, if it has the same chapter but does not have this extra mistake, now you realise that this is not corruption, this is a scribal error, because that error has not been copied later on. Before, it was not there. Other manuscripts from the same time do not have it, and manuscripts later on do not have it. Now we know that this is a scribal error. If you compare with other religions, for example the Christian tradition, you see that that error is repeated in manuscripts later on. So you realise that there is corruption taking place, because that change has now been copied and copied in later manuscripts. Then it has been put in the Bible and people are reading it. This is how you know that there is a corruption.", "Even though you can look at the upper text and the lower text, and you can look at certain scribal errors, the conclusion of scholars (we're talking about non-Muslim scholars, secular scholars who went and studied the San'aa manuscripts and have written works on it), their conclusion is the same: the Quran is preserved. Whatever error a manuscript could have, from the 8th century or the 9th century, whatever it is, any errors or mistakes that these have were scribal errors, and we can prove it.", "Now, whether you want to look at the written text or you want to look at the oral text, there's only one conclusion that you can come to: that the Quran that we have today is perfectly preserved. It is absolutely perfectly preserved from the oral text. We don't even have to rely on any of these written manuscripts, because yes, someone can write and make a mistake; so what? That does not mean anything. We're saying even if this happens, this does not mean anything, but even that is not the case. Whether you look at written manuscripts of the Quran and you compare them to what we have today, or you look at the oral tradition of the Quran, with these mass transmissions of tens of thousands, [hundreds] of thousands, millions of certificates today that trace back the Quran to the Prophetﷺ, the Quran is preserved and will always be preserved.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “The History Of The Quranic Text| A Breakdown From Revelation To Compilation| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JjBR2JbHN6o", ((2470, "41:10–41:32"), (2497, "41:37–42:13"), (2537, "42:17–42:59"), (2582, "43:02–43:45"), (2632, "43:52–44:12"), (2655, "44:15–45:18"), (2741, "45:41–46:06"), (2782, "46:22–47:02"),))
// U ML015:522-556 | The Muslim Lantern | 2
#passage(("We have, for example, the Sana'a manuscripts. They've been examined by non-Muslims, like Dr. Ursula Dreibholz and many others.", "We have also the Topkapi manuscript, which is late 1st century, early 2nd century. It has 99.something of the Quran. We have a parchment which has parts of the Quran from chapter two, and we have it in Birmingham. Who is dating it is the British. It's in the Birmingham Museum.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Muslim Surprises Intelligent Lady With Undeniable Evidence For Islam! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qzJXd-FotIo", ((1773, "29:33–29:47"), (1822, "30:22–30:48"),))
// U ML317:257-341 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("If you believe something, you need to have evidence to believe. Can you name one scholar, academic, whether western or otherwise, who studied the Sana'a manuscript and said that they oppose the Quran that we have in our hands today?", "What are the Sana'a manuscripts? Is it one manuscript? So it's one manuscript that was written and overwritten? That's completely false. In Sana'a in Yemen, the manuscripts that we found are various, many, multiple.", "There are many people who talked about the Sana'a manuscripts and how they are evidence that the Quran is preserved. For example, you have Dr. Angelika Neuwirth from Germany; she's not a Muslim. She studied the Sana'a manuscripts and she said that this is evidence, and I can name you many: Dr Tayyar Altıkulaç and many different people, academics who studied the Sana'a manuscripts and said these manuscripts are actually proof that the Quran is preserved. Actually, Angelika Neuwirth literally said: the Sana'a manuscripts ended the debate, any debate, on whether the Quran is preserved or not.", "This is not how academia works. You've got major academics who barely have anyone follow them; they're just in universities, professors and doctors; they just teach the people there. The level of followers is not an indication of qualification: just because you've got many followers does not mean you're a qualified individual. I know the biggest idiots today have millions of subscribers on YouTube. Point is, I have mentioned professors in universities, people who have whole PhDs and Masters, who actually went and examined the manuscript. No one here is arguing from authority, because I started by asking you to provide an individual who's an academic respected in the field, who claimed that they're not preserved, that the Sana'a shows that it's different, and you didn't have anyone. You cannot go to a doctor and ask him about engineering and then you say that he said this about engineering; it's irrelevant, it's not his field. You've got to find people who actually studied the manuscript.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Sikh Challenges Muslim On The Preservation Of The Quran! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=3FaWv_XB4Eo", ((809, "13:29–13:38"), (844, "14:04–14:28"), (891, "14:51–15:17"), (931, "15:31–16:55"),))
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR293:1-79 | Farid Responds | 6
#passage(("It's irresponsible to talk about something that you're not familiar with, and that's exactly what Samir does in his video. Samir has the audacity not only to talk about a subject that he knows nothing about, that he has no access to the classical texts, but he's so incompetent that he fails at providing the correct information, even though he has access to it in English. In his video, he showcases a paper which he contradicts again and again.", "If you ever took a single class in Quranic sciences, you would have known about this. The information about Quranic [variants] can be found in most Quranic commentaries. So no, this content wasn't hidden away from Muslims. This was documented for all to see.", "If you actually bothered to read the research that you're showcasing, you would have realized how ridiculous you're being: \"Nothing was revealed about the manuscripts beyond the finding that there are [variants], a [banal] observation from the standpoint of traditional Muslim scholarship.\" Onto the footnote: \"The journalists and some of their academic informants suggest that Muslim scholars are unaware of textual variance. They disregard the dozens of volumes written on [variants] and the textual critical discussions about them in the [tafsir] genre and other sources.\"", "Now, in regards to this specific variant, can we trust it? No, we can't. And the reason is: we don't know who wrote this. We don't know where it's from. We don't know if it's correctly attributed to [Anas] or not. Sure, some late commentators said it is, but do we know that that's the case for sure? No, we don't. And at the end of the day, we're not going to trust an attribution in favor of the collective consensus of the community of Sahaba of Medina during the time of [Uthman]. So why does Samir specifically accept this variant while rejecting the collective consensus of the companions? He came to this conclusion because it's supposedly the oldest surviving copy of the Quran, which Samir himself refutes in the fine print. But more importantly, the reason Samir holds the view that this manuscript is the allegedly correct Quran is because it would undermine the view that the Quran was preserved, which is quite a biased reason, and Samir knows it.", "There's another matter that Samir is trying desperately to push, which is that all variants in the [palimpsest] are intentional. This is something that I've refuted a year ago, when I briefly examined the variance and came to the conclusion that it was riddled with errors. By returning to the single page, I found two inexcusable grammatical errors as well as a missing verse. But again, it seems that I was not the only person to arrive at this conclusion. The same academic document that Samir showcased in the video displays this: \"One theory would be that transmission involved reciting the text and the simultaneous writing down of the recitation by a companion, but not precise word for word dictation. The [variants] indicate a recitation that was performed faster than a hearer could take down with complete fidelity.\" In other words, most of these variants are mainly due to scribal errors.", "[Samir] also provided one more reason why he believes that this manuscript is reliable. So it was in a mosque, and therefore it's automatically the correct Quran, and what we have today is false? Is that the case?",), "Farid Responds, “Educating Sameer on Sana'a Quran Manuscript”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=HnhJokcNZPw", ((25, "0:25–1:05"), (188, "3:08–3:36"), (233, "3:53–4:37"), (295, "4:55–6:16"), (403, "6:43–7:47"), (478, "7:58–8:19"),))
