#passage(("He makes all of this, the old school, the same narrative of the Quran copying from the Bible and copying from Apocrypha. It's always repeated by a Christian apologist, this absurd claim. But let's break down what he's saying first. So he's saying, first, Prophet Muhammad was a caravan trader who's going all the time here and there to Syria and Jordan, and therefore he was hearing stories. Of course, that's false. That's not the Islamic narrative. He's claiming that this is Islamic narrative. That's false.", "There's only two incidents that we have Prophet Muhammad traveling. The authenticity even of these narrations are debated to begin with, but let's accept that they were true. So we have one time Prophet Muhammad going to, let's say, near Syria, but he didn't fully go into the middle of it, when he was young. He was like 12 years old, with his uncle Abu Talib. So we have this one time where he went, and then they came back quickly. And then you have another narration where Prophet Muhammad was 25 years old, with Khadijah, his wife, where he went for trade. So you have two instances of Prophet Muhammad going.", "Then at the age of 40, for 23 years, he's regurgitating, allegedly, for lack of a better term. He's bringing back all of these fictional narratives. Oh, he has the memory of the best person on Earth! And he's bringing back all of these stories in 23 years, and people on the spot come and ask him about the story of Joseph, the entire chapter of Joseph, how big it is. How is he accurately giving you this description of these narratives and stories? We know this is completely absurd. This idea of Prophet Muhammad traveling here and there learning stories is false. It's not the Islamic narrative, and it's not the Islamic idea.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Wess Huff Claims The Quran Was Copied From The Bible | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hb-kHiXbGxI", ((670, "11:10–11:39"), (700, "11:40–12:15"), (735, "12:15–12:56"),))
