#passage(("This apocryphal writings: who determined they were apocryphal writings? The church. When? Who chose the 27Books of the New Testament? Which specific church father, for the 27new books of the New Testament? So you'll find in Athanasius' letter him mentioning the 27books of the NT are determined to be canon. So now, let's say 300 years, 200, whichever how many years after Jesus, we have a church father coming and deciding: \"OK! We're going to read these specific books in our churches as canon. These are the ones we're going to accept. And the tens of thousands of other gospels and writings, we're going to put on the side.\" Now, why do we have to accept that to begin with?", "The 1st definition of the word Apocrypha, the early definition, before the new definition of the church, was \"something which is hidden\"; it wasn't \"something which non-canon, rejected\". That is a later definition. So the earlier definition was something which is concealed or hidden. So it was historical sources. Anyone who comes and says the apocrypha are just to be left and rejected, left, right and center, they have no historical bearing, just because Athanasius or whoever church father decided at some point: \"Oh! We're going to read this in our churches and we're going to take this as canon\", we say: we don't care! Where is your proof that these apocrypha are not based on actual historical facts, realities, events which took place?", "I'd say it's more strengthening the narrative of the Quran that these things are mentioned in the apocrypha. Why? Because these apocrypha writings were not translated, and it was not existed in the Arabic language at the time of the Prophet Muhammadﷺ, anyway! There was no translated Bible in the life of the Prophetﷺ into Arabic language. It's only bits and pieces that some people here try to translate of the Bible, not even of the apocrypha writings.", "So that's the first thing: we reject your premise. Just because your church rejected it later on, that doesn't mean we also have to reject it, and it does not mean that it does not have an historical source. Now, they have to prove that whatever is mentioned in the apocrypha, it is not historically accurate. Because if this was historically accurate, meaning the events happened (not necessarily the details, because the details can be changed, but these events happened), then it would make sense that the Quran mentions actual events that happened. What is the issue with that? Of course, Allah mentions things which happened in the life of Jesus, mentions things which happened in the life of Moses, etc. Allah mentions them because they are valid historical events. So to find different sources of something usually strengthens its historicity.", "For instance, I can come and say now the book of the Christians is corrupted. Why? \"It mentions the flood of Noah because it's mentioned before in the Epic of Gilgamesh, and there's a story of a flood, and there's many, many other historical sources that mentions a flood.\" We use the same stupid argument! Therefore now the Bible is false, and put it on the side! The fact that there are so many sources having the story of Noah is more strengthening the historicity of the event than it is taking away from the books that mention it. There are many sources that mention it; it's because it did happen.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Unitarian Christian Questions The Similarities Between The Quran & The Bible! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t9EQwV9zMIE", ((244, "4:04–5:04"), (310, "5:10–5:48"), (352, "5:52–6:14"), (382, "6:22–7:20"), (444, "7:24–7:59"),))
