#passage(("All what happens is we have a story that traces to the 2nd century or so, 2nd or could be even 1st century, a story about, for example, Jesus creating clay birds. Now, the question is: how can we falsify this as false information? It's very early on, so it must come from somewhere. Someone sat down one day and was like: \"You know what? I'm going to make up the story about Jesus growing up.\" And then, specifically, he was making clay birds. Why? For what reason?", "This is a question that everybody who comes up with this claim need to answer. Now, you have a multitude of apocryphal texts, and heretical texts, or whatever: many, many texts and stories that are found that are not necessarily a part of the Bible. Now, these are found in all types of languages: Syriac, some are in Aramaic, some in Greek, some in Hebrew, some in all types; found in all sort of locations in the world, because this is a manuscript that you have, this gospel or whatever that has this: there's one manuscript that is written that is found somewhere. Now, I'm going to ask this question: how did Prophet Muhammad go to all these parts of the world, access all these manuscripts, and learn the language of all of these manuscripts, when he wasn't going traveling around the world? And then he came back, and he brought a story that is not even the same story exactly as that one that is found in these apocryphal texts. How did he do that?", "If he heard the stories, then why the stories in the Quran are different from these stories? They're not exactly the same, the stories; they're different, actually. The story is not only the clay birds. There's a larger story that the Quran doesn't record the same way as it does. Plus, the Quran doesn't even say Jesus was young when he was doing this. The story talks about the upbringing of Jesus. It's like a completely different story if you read the Quran. So, first, you got to explain this.", "Number two, you have to prove that these stories were circulating around the prophet. There's no proof for that whatsoever. These manuscripts that we're talking about are not found within the desert of Arabia next to Prophet Muhammad or something. They're found in other countries and other parts of the world, further away. So the person who makes the claim, they need to prove who did the prophet copy this from, and that these stories were actually circulating. If these stories were rejected as apocryphal texts, why would they be circulating around?", "You have a story that the church says it's apocryphal. Are people going to keep passing down that story, or they're going to rely on what is canon? What is the rational behavior for the overwhelming majority of Christians? If the church or the authority says that this is not a part of the main scripture, don't rely on reading this, read the canon, what are the overwhelming majority of people going to be doing? And those who wouldn't, they wouldn't be following the teachings of their own authorities. So these people are the smallest minority by definition. So now, hundreds of generations before Prophet Muhammad, it's already been established that these stories are not canon, so don't rely on them, don't read them in the churches.", "It's only a select number of people that could read the Bible, actually. It was forbidden for the common folk. It was only a select number of people and the scribes, and for hundreds of years was the case. So those scribes that are following the authority of the church are told: \"Rely on the canon.\" They're not going to teach these stories to the common folk. So the common folk are not going to have these stories; they're not going to pass down these stories. So now, we have these small stories: hundreds of years before the prophet, they were stopped. So now, it would be very rare to find someone that still has the story at the time of the prophet, hundreds of years after this has been declared as something that is apocryphal and not canon. Now, you're saying all of these stories: we're talking about tens of stories now, not one or two. Someone could come and say: \"Oh, maybe one story he happened to find someone.\" Even though they need to provide proof for that. The revelation was coming down upon the prophet for 23 years, and people would come asking him the question, and he would respond directly to them.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Agnostic Grills A Muslim On The Quran Copying The Bible! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=24D1h0UflFg", ((72, "1:12–2:51"), (220, "3:40–4:36"), (284, "4:44–5:13"), (313, "5:13–5:38"), (354, "5:54–7:05"), (439, "7:19–8:11"),))
