#passage(("According to the Quran, the Pharaoh claimed to be God. According to the Bible, there's no such evidence that he claimed to be God. According to you now, using your reasoning, Apocrypha would be the source for whoever was authoring the Quran to bring this information, because he's not taking it from the Bible, clearly, because the Bible doesn't say Ramses the Second was claiming to be God. Pharaoh in the (Quran79:24) says \"فَقَالَ أَنَا۠ رَبُّكُمُ ٱلْأَعْلَىٰ\", I'm your lord, the most high.", "You asserted right now the Pharaoh was Ramses the Second, who was at the time of Moses. Let's accept that assumption. According to the Bible, it doesn't say that; there's no Apocrypha that says that Ramses the Second claimed to be God. Question is: where would the Quran bring this information from, if it was looking and thinking that Apocrypha was the true source? Where does this information come from? Because it's not in the Apocrypha. And you were just claiming that, just because you found this information in the Apocrypha, you're thinking the Quran is taking information from the Apocrypha. Now, if your reasoning is consistent, then this idea that Pharaoh claimed to be God should also be in Apocrypha.", "Another example is that the people of Abraham worshipped the Sun and the Moon and Venus: should be in the Apocrypha, but it is not in the Apocrypha. People of Abraham worshipped three Gods: Sun and Moon and Ishtar, which was goddess of Venus. In the Quran, Chapter 6 of the Quran, it says that his people, he was debating with them, about the Moon and the Sun and Venus. Point is this: these are all information that are not in the Apocrypha. If your argument was actually consistent, then you would find all of these things in the Apocrypha. Not star deities: specifically, Sun, Moon and Venus. The Quran specifies, and it says the Sun and the Moon, which by many people will not consider stars, or not viewed as stars in the past anyways. So, according to your reasoning, this extra information should all be present in the Apocrypha, if your reasoning is consistent.", "You have to give me now an answer, an alternative: where is the source of these stories and information, since it's not found in the Apocrypha? What you're saying is: few stories are found in the Apocrypha, therefore I'm gonna say all of them are taken from the Apocrypha. You have to prove that all stories are taken from Apocrypha. Otherwise, you have to demonstrate which source brings this other information that is within the Quran, that is not within the Bible or the Apocrypha.", "You claim that, in your mind, whoever was having these stories in the Quran, because they're not in the Bible, he must have taken them from a source: he thought the Apocrypha was the original, and the Bible was a corruption of the Apocrypha. Therefore he was taking this information from the Apocrypha. But we just established this information in the Quran is extra biblical: it's not in the Bible, and it is not from the Apocrypha, which shows that your reasoning is inconsistent.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Claims The Quran Is Historically Plagiarised! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ngAbQzpSvMw", ((478, "7:58–9:23"), (573, "9:33–10:11"), (617, "10:17–11:38"), (714, "11:54–12:28"), (816, "13:36–13:57"),))
