#passage(("That's only part of the [verse]. The actual verse goes like this: \"because of that, we decreed upon the children of Israel that whoever kills a soul, unless for a soul or for corruption done in the land, it is as if he had killed all mankind, and whoever saves one, it is as if he had saved all mankind.\"", "There's no reason to assume that this text was removed from the Old Testament, since there is no motive to do so. It is far likelier that the text belongs to the oral law or a non extant scripture. There were many books that were seen as Scripture, and were even referred to in the Old and New Testament, but were lost through the passage of time.", "There's no doubt that these words are quite similar. However, this doesn't mean that the Quran plagiarizes the commentary. There is reason to believe that the origin of this interpretation comes from a common source, in other words the lost authoritative Scripture. Proof of this can be found in the Jerusalem Talmud, which expresses that whoever caused a single life to perish from Israel, he is deemed by Scripture as if he had caused a whole world to perish. More importantly, there are several key differences between the Quranic verse and the Talmud, most importantly that the [Quran] is polemical here, refuting the Talmud, which asserts that killing a Jew is as if one is killing the whole of creation. The Quran goes on to correct this concept by affirming that this is for all man and not exclusive to Jewish victims. So no, this isn't plagiarism; this is a correction.", "What Jews loved doing most is talking about the Mishnah verses in Arabic in front of an allegedly false prophet! The suggestion is ridiculous, since it would imply that the Prophet [memorized] a random discussion, then waited several years before including it verbatim, with corrections, as part of the Quran, since [Surat al-Ma'idah] is the last revealed chapter. [Ridvan] knows well that there were no Jews in Medinah after the year 5 after hijra. So perhaps he did this with the hundreds of other verses that speak about early prophets as well: he just happened to bump into Jews that mindlessly recited these works.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] The Most Violent and Awkward Quran Verse - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=SL3GLr7XW30", ((144, "2:24–2:41"), (195, "3:15–3:35"), (308, "5:08–6:05"), (387, "6:27–7:05"),))
// U FR077:64-101 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("As Chris pointed out, there are some phrases that can be found within the Quran that show that the author of the Quran is very familiar with the Bible. And no, it's not only that the Quran is familiar with Biblical stories, but it's quite familiar with Biblical phraseology, and not only that, but the author of the Quran actually knows Hebrew: \"Our eighth example is the phrase 'we hear and disobey' in Arabic, which the Quran puts in the mouth of the Israelites on two occasions. Here the Quran seems to follow quite closely the wording of a phrase in the Hebrew Bible, which however has quite the opposite sense.\" He continues: \"Tellingly, however, is the negative version Arabic that matches the positive version in Hebrew. Here we have one of the relatively rare cases where Hebrew phraseology seems to have influenced the Arabic text of the Quran.\"", "So what's happening here is the Quran is taking a Hebrew phrase and arabize the term, not translating it, but arabize it, in order to give it the opposite meaning. So instead of the Jews saying \"we hear and obey\", it's the Jews saying \"we hear and disobey\". And this is brilliant, because not only does it show that the Quran is familiar with Biblical phraseology and that the Quran is familiar with Hebrew, but it's using it in an eloquent way: it's putting some sort of a Hebrew pun within the Quran.",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood's Latest Theory BACKFIRES BADLY!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=NxKzV523Jz4", ((161, "2:41–3:49"), (229, "3:49–4:22"),))

