#passage(("I stumbled across a video by Samir called The Meccan Poet: How the Quran resembles pre-Islamic poetry. Wow, that's quite a claim to make.", "This is actually something that Samir puts forward as something that matches the Quran. The meanings are silly, which is a condition of eloquence, by the way. However, he thinks that anything that rhymes is considered eloquent.", "Strangely though, Samir apparently forgot to tell you who wrote these poems. He lists six examples in his video. For those that aren't aware, [Musaylima] actually went public about his prophethood and attempted to mimic the Quran style after the death of the prophet, peace be upon him. So not sure how the Quran plagiarized from him.", "We find a chain of narration for this particular report: a chain of liars, actually. But wait, I thought you didn't like chains of narrator, Samir. Oh, that's right: the report supports your theory. Therefore, we should accept it as authentic and believe that the Quran copied this style from this poem.", "I'm sure that right now you're thinking: \"Wow, Sam is so incompetent. Five of the six poems weren't even attributed to pre-Islamic poets.\" And you know what? I felt the same way. I thought he was just that incompetent. Then I realized something, and I was surprised to find this, because I rarely ever read the video descriptions. If we scroll down past the links to his channel, his website, links to other videos and articles and his social media, we find this: \"[some are] pre-Islamic and others may be from Muhammad's time.\" What the heck?",), "Farid Responds, “The Deception of Abdullah Sameer (Meccan Poet / Pre-Islamic Poetry)”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=D5ZPQETfI4I", ((8, "0:08–0:22"), (112, "1:52–2:07"), (143, "2:23–3:11"), (196, "3:16–3:40"), (220, "3:40–4:42"),))
// U FR283:58-139 | Farid Responds | 5
#passage(("First of all, you can't read the line of poetry, which isn't that big of a surprise.", "These are the similarities, supposedly: this is the copy paste, this is the plagiarism that he's speaking on. On the top you have the Quran: \"He knows what is spoken openly [and what] you conceal\"; and in the bottom the poem by [Zuhayr]: \"so don't conceal from Allah what is within you to hide it, for whatever is concealed from Allah is known by Him\". This is plagiarism, and he says it's plagiarism because there's a similarity in style. No, there isn't. There is no similarity in style. The structure is completely different, a lot of different words. This can't be plagiarism.", "He's arguing that the concept of emphasis is exclusive to pre-Islamic poetry.", "This is what he's quoting: \"the angels are on its sides, and on that day eight angels will be carrying the throne\". On its sides, meaning the sides of the throne. The poetry says: \"the feathers of pigeons are on its [sides], the sides of the water, and that causes hearts to race\". Now he's claiming that saying \"on its sides\" is an act of plagiarism. So the Quran can't say \"on its sides\"? This is so silly, this is so dumb. This is not something specific to the Quran; there's not something specific to poetry. These are words that people use: everyone says \"on its sides\". There's nothing special.", "There's not something specific to or exclusive to [pre-Islamic] poetry: it just means \"no one from among them\". This is a basic common line; you even find this in hadith.",), "Farid Responds, “Ex-Muslim: Quran plagiarized Poetry!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=V3jTOmkzUcg", ((200, "3:20–3:24"), (220, "3:40–4:24"), (294, "4:54–5:02"), (338, "5:38–6:46"), (434, "7:14–7:30"),))

=== 27.5 The Syriac Alexander legend and Surat al-Kahf
// NODE P05.C04.S05
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR025:1-70 | Farid Responds | 7
#passage(("Professor Tommaso Tesei: he's been making the rounds doing interviews about this topic. He is the expert on the subject.", "Before dealing with the main claim, I'd like to point out that this idea that the Quran plagiarized the Syriac Alexander legend has two parts. One is the actual text, and the other is pre-Islamic texts. That's often what's brought up in these discussions. So the sources that are usually brought are Josephus, Jerome, and what they mainly mention is: Alexander built a gate against barbarians. And Procopius mentions that you can actually detour around the gate. So it's not really comparable to the wall that Dhul-Qarnain built that's supposed to seal Gog and Magog. Alexander built a gate in order to prevent barbarians from coming through.", "In fact Josephus, in his Wars of the Jews, mentions that centuries after Alexander there was a nation called the Alans that requested permission to pass through the gate, and the king allowed them to. So they just went through the gate, and then they killed the civilians and they pillaged, and then they went back through the gate into their land. Josephus is not saying that Alexander built a gate against Gog and Magog or anything like that. He's not even saying that Alexander built a gate against the Alans. All he's saying is that Alexander built a gate, and then centuries later a tribe or a nation called the Alans asked permission from the king to pass through that gate, and they were granted permission. So in the Quran you're not going to have Gog and Magog asking permission to go through a gate.", "Now, in these classical texts you don't have Alexander building a gate or a wall or anything like that against Gog and Magog specifically. And we have [Emeri] Van Donzel and [Andrea] Schmidt: in their book Gog and Magog in the Early Syriac and Islamic Sources they point out that the first time that this is ever claimed, this idea that Alexander's gates emerged to ward off Gog and Magog, was in the Syriac Alexander legend: \"This is the first source in which we find together the motive of horned Alexander and that of the wall built against Gog and Magog.\" So, due to this, there are no Western academics that say that the prophet, peace be upon him, picked up the story from Jerome or Josephus or Procopius and that he substituted the name Alans or the Huns or the Scythians with Gog and Magog. That idea never came to anyone's mind.", "What we need to be aware of is that the actual argument against Islam is specifically the Syriac Alexander legend. The argument that's brought forward by these Western academics is that the Quran plagiarized the text called the [Neshana], or the Syriac Alexander legend. You should read it if you're actually confused by this argument, because it portrays Alexander in a very strange way, in a way that if you read it you'd think there's no way the Quran plagiarized this in the first place. It has single-breasted women warriors. It has Alexander appearing as a pre-Jesus Christian prophet. It's a wild story. It even has God joining Alexander's men in war.", "What's interesting to me is that people have contacted me about this argument in the past, but it's never people that have actually read the text. It's people that are affected by the claim: \"Is it true that the Quran quoted a legend or borrowed from a legend?\" \"Did you read the text?\" \"No, I haven't read the text, but people are saying that this is the case.\" Read the text. Just read it; you're going to feel that there's no way that the Quran took from this.", "But then the question comes to mind: why would anyone think that Dhul-Qarnain is Alexander? There are three main parallels that Professor Tommaso mentions in order to argue that the Quran is plagiarizing the Syriac Alexander legend. The first is that Dhul-Qarnain built a gate like Alexander, which is incorrect. Dhul-Qarnain built a wall. It's not something that you can ask permission to go through in the same way that the Alans did. The second claim is that Dhul-Qarnain had horns, which also is not true, because we have a narration in which the prophet's cousin, Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, in multiple reports denies that Dhul-Qarnain ever had horns. These are sahih, authentic traditions. And the third claim is that Dhul-Qarnain is a prophet, which also is incorrect, because the prophet, peace be upon him, in an authentic hadith says, \"I don't know if Dhul-Qarnain is a prophet from God or not.\" And this is a great proof, because Alexander is clearly described as a prophet in the Syriac legend: \"Above all we must say that Alexander here appears as a Christian prophet.\"",), "Farid Responds, “Dhu al-Qarnayn ≠ Alexander [ft. Prof. Tommaso Tesei]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tKyy0weOjow", ((25, "0:25–0:38"), (49, "0:49–1:46"), (106, "1:46–2:39"), (161, "2:41–3:54"), (236, "3:56–4:52"), (292, "4:52–5:17"), (319, "5:19–7:44"),))
// U FR025:112-145 | Farid Responds | 3
#passage(("Now, according to the classical Islamic scholars from the first century (Ibn Abbas, Ibn Mas'ud, Ibn Zubayr, al-Hasan al-Basri, Ikrimah, Ata, Jabir ibn [Zayd] and Mujahid), they all agree that Surat al-Kahf was a Meccan surah, meaning that it was written before the year 622-623. On the other hand, up until recent times the consensus of Western academics when it came to dating the Syriac Alexander Legend was around the year 630. So if we go by the most basic apparent evidence, then we conclude that the Quran is earlier than the Syriac Alexander Legend by at least a few years.", "Now why did Western academics come to the conclusion that this text was written in the year 630? The text itself, the Syriac Alexander Legend, mentions the number 940 being the time in which the Huns would come through the gate, like an end-of-times prophecy, and they would go to war with the Persians and the Arabs, and they would fall upon one another, and in the end the Greeks will emerge victorious. Now if you actually do the math, this matches the time of Heraclius: he allied with the Gokturks, and they supported him in his war against the Sassanids. Historians concluded this is proof that this text was written at around the year 630. In other words, the prophecy was written after the fact, after these events had transpired, as a propaganda piece about Heraclius and his victories.", "This position had been the consensus for a very long time. However, recently folks like Professor [Tommaso] concluded that this specific evidence, which is the prophecy and this specific number, is actually an interpolation that was added into the text late. So Professor [Tommaso] is not only rejecting and dismissing all the information about the Muslims in terms of the dating of Surat al-Kahf, but he's also dismissing the information that has to do with dating the Syriac Alexander Legend, in fact the most explicit proof from the text itself.",), "Farid Responds, “Dhu al-Qarnayn ≠ Alexander [ft. Prof. Tommaso Tesei]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tKyy0weOjow", ((729, "12:09–14:14"), (855, "14:15–15:18"), (921, "15:21–16:52"),))
// U FR299:102-131 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("The Alexander Legend in its current form is from an 18th century manuscript, 11 centuries after the Quran. [Reinink] generously gives this work a very early date of around the year [630], and even with this date the chapter predates the Alexander legend by 8 years, since the chapter was revealed before the migration to Medina.", "The reports mention that he was attacked on the [qarnayn], meaning the two sides of his head, and hence the name.",), "Farid Responds, “Abdullah Sameer: Alexander is Dhu Al-Qarnayn”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=taW1dn5Bn0k", ((302, "5:02–5:31"), (350, "5:50–6:02"),))
// U FR025:146-208 | Farid Responds | 4
#passage(("I'm accepting the traditional dating provided by first century Muslim scholars. I'm accepting the internal evidence from the Syriac Alexander text. I'm taking everything at face value, and that is how I am concluding that the Quran predates the Syriac Alexander Legend. I don't believe that there is manipulation going on or any interpolation. And it's weird, because Professor [Tommaso] is claiming that this text itself received a lot of attention and many texts were based around it, and this is extremely significant.", "The weird thing here is that if this text was circulated, and you have all these works that revolve around it, quoting from it or engaging with it or responding to it (you even have the Quran allegedly borrowing from it), this means this was exceptionally popular at the time. However, despite that, how likely is it that the only surviving text includes this interpolation? Where are the other texts that don't have the 940 date within them? There are five manuscripts we have for the Syriac Alexander Legend. The earliest is from the 18th century. So all five of these allegedly copy the interpolation that allegedly wasn't in the original, and all the original texts that didn't have the interpolation, that's all lost in time. Of course, this is very unlikely, which is why I reject the theory.", "However, what if someone really feels that they need to accept this theory? I argued in my paper that if this is indeed the case, assuming that the single most important line for dating the text is actually an interpolation, then why not assume that everything that sounds like the Quran in any way is also interpolation? For example, Professor [Tommaso] says, \"If it swims like a duck, if it looks like a duck, then it's a duck.\" The horns of Alexander that's mentioned in the text, that is an interpolation. The usage of the names Gog and Magog, that's also taken from the Quran. That's an interpolation. Why not extend it to that? Anything that you can connect to the Quran or the Dhul-Qarnain narrative, you could simply claim, \"It was an interpolation. There were many centuries between the composition of this text until the actual manuscripts that we have attained. Therefore, these interpolations could have happened at any time.\"", "Luckily enough for us, this question was actually posed to Professor [Tommaso] in one of his interviews. He's actually answering the question of the origin of the text, when it was originated, not the idea of the text receiving interpolations due to late manuscripts or anything like that. The reason why he's answering a question that he was not asked is because he's got nothing. Now, personally, I don't know why he would even say, \"Theologically, you can make the point,\" because the interviewer asked a perfectly reasonable historical question. So I do find it interesting how one would be so desperate in attempting to make the Syriac Alexander legend predate the Quran, dismissing away all the evidence in order to push for this idea. And after making such a claim, they refer to the Islamic response as faith-based or theologically driven.",), "Farid Responds, “Dhu al-Qarnayn ≠ Alexander [ft. Prof. Tommaso Tesei]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tKyy0weOjow", ((1016, "16:56–17:35"), (1119, "18:39–19:57"), (1197, "19:57–21:11"), (1271, "21:11–23:51"),))

