#passage(("When I first came across this [idea] that Islam has some sort of demonic origins, I was completely caught off guard. I actually thought that the person that was saying this had some sort of issues, and that's what caused him to say something so wild. And then I found out with time that it's something that a decent number of Christians actually believe in, and that just completely blew my mind. Even some of the more popular critics of Islam, like God Logic, seems to have this belief.", "Though, if you think about it, I guess it is a pretty natural cope. Islam spread really fast, and it changed the lives of a lot of people in a very short amount of time, and the growth was exponential, and today [it's the] second largest religion in the world. So I do understand the Christian concern: if [demons] did originate false religions, and Islam according to Christians is a false religion, then naturally it would make sense for it to be demonic.", "I imagine the average Muslim simply stating: how could Islam be demonic when Islam encourages things like giving to charity? Do demons appreciate kindness to the poor? Islam ended [infanticide]; is it something about demons wanting to save the lives of children? What's going on there? And those are very good questions, but there is another important question that definitely needs to be asked, or another set of questions: what's up with Islam promoting monotheism and fighting polytheism? What's up with Islam promoting or affirming this belief in an afterlife? What's up with Islam affirming previous prophets of the Bible? My argument isn't that Islam is simply promoting goodness and trying to get people to do good things, but Islam is affirming some very important matters that actually brings people closer to God.", "[Ibn Mas'ud] narrates [in Bukhari] that there were 360 idols around the [Kaaba] in Mecca, and the prophet would go around stabbing them. Those 360 idols were eliminated. He brought monotheism to a society that was plagued by polytheism. He had them worship the god of Abraham, confirming that [Allah] is the god of Abraham. Does that sound demonic to you? What's the wisdom behind that? You don't have to believe that Allah is the god of Abraham, but that's what Muhammad, peace be upon him, told them to do.", "Avery and other Christian apologists enjoy mentioning this idea of the Quran allegedly confirming the authority and preservation of the Bible. Okay, let's assume that's the case. Why would demons do that? Why would Satan want these pagan Arabs to accept the Bible as authoritative and preserved? How does that make any sense? Your guest showed you an obvious contradiction in the actions of Satan, and your best answer was: \"That's because Satan is dumb.\"", "Well, according to Avery, that's one of the tricks of Satan: it's to quote scripture. Well, the problem with that is that Jesus at the time believed in scripture. It's not like Satan is trying to convince Jesus of scripture. That's why your analogy absolutely falls flat on its face. The Arabs, the polytheist Arabs, didn't believe in the scripture, so why is Satan trying to convince them that the Bible is preserved and authoritative? How does that make any sense?", "The most ironic thing, though, about this specific argument is that this is actually, at the moment, from what I've seen, the strongest argument for the preservation of the Bible. These Christian apologist seem to have given up on proving that the Bible is historically accurate through [conventional] means, so what they do is, when having discussions with Muslims, they're like, \"Well, Quran affirms the Bible.\" So this argument, or the actions of Satan according to you guys, is actually the strongest proof for the preservation of the Bible. How ironic is that? But wait, let me guess what the response is: it's showing you how dumb Satan is and how sovereign God is.",), "Farid Responds, “Godlogic: \"The Quran is Satan's Work!\"”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=mGaA2rHTKX0", ((0, "0:00–0:31"), (35, "0:35–1:13"), (84, "1:24–2:36"), (156, "2:36–3:28"), (210, "3:30–4:50"), (398, "6:38–7:10"), (430, "7:10–7:57"),))

