#passage(("Allahﷻ says in the Quran that He created the Heavens and the Earth in six days (alA'raf7:54): إِنَّ رَبَّكُمُ ٱللَّهُ ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَ ٱلسَّمَـٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ فِى سِتَّةِ أَيَّامٍۢ (Your Lord is Allah Who created the Heavens and the Earth in six days). The verses in Surah Fussilat doesn't mention the number eight anywhere. Why are they saying the number eight? I'll tell you: it's a simple problem in calculation.", "So the verses in the Quran (Fussilat41:9-12) say that Allah (created the earth in two days). Then the next verse says that Allah did certain things on the earth (we're still talking about the creation on the earth), and then it says (in four days). So these four days are not four that are added to the first two. I don't mean it's 2+4, it's not that. The four include the first two, because we're still talking about the creation of the earth. Allah is saying (do you disbelieve in Allah Who created the earth in two days). Then Allah says (He's placed mountains, and has placed other things on the earth in four days).", "Nobody in Islamic history who's read these verses, or no book of exegesis in Islamic history, has ever said that these are four extra days plus the first two. Everyone says these four days INCLUDE the two first days that are mentioned. We're still on the same topic. Then the next two days, about the heaven, is the new topic. So we have four days, two included in the four, and then we have two. So it's six days!", "This is also mentioned in many books of exegesis, like atTabari mentions this. And he mentions an Arabic saying: \"I married a woman yesterday, and today two.\" When he says today two, he doesn't mean he married two extra women, and now they are three. No! He means one yesterday and one today. This is common in the Arabic language. I think it's even common in language in general!", "Imagine me, I'm giving you money, and I say: I'll give you two pounds (and then I'm counting the money I'm giving you). Here, one, two pounds