#passage(("In Islam, and I would say also in common sense, not just in Islam, there's two types of knowledge. There's knowing something before it happens and there's knowing something after it happens. Me knowing something before it happens is not the same as me observing it and seeing it and knowing it after it's happening. These are two types of knowledge. For example, I can know that Adam is going to visit me on Thursday. There's no doubt that I know that. Now when Adam comes and I see him and I experience him etc., now I know that Adam has visited. These are two different types of knowledge, not the same exact category and type of knowledge.", "So the Quran here gives two differentiation between two different types of knowledge: the [knowledge] of the unseen and the seen. The Quran gives knowing something of the unseen, or prior to it happening, and knowing something after it appears. So Allah already knows it before it takes place, and Allah knows it now, the different type of knowledge, after it occurs and after it happens. None of the verses that you can find will negate Allah's knowledge of something. There's no verse out there that says Allah doesn't know something. But the verses that says Allah will know refers to the knowledge that I told you: the knowledge of something after that thing happens. Scholars have talked about this for thousands of years. Not bringing anything new out here.", "[Pickthall]: \"Or deemed ye that ye would enter paradise, while yet Allah knoweth not those of you who really strive, nor not those of you who are steadfast.\" Of course, it's a terrible translation. Every other translation out there doesn't say the same thing. For example: \"without Allah proving which of you truly struggle\"; [Sahih] International: \"Allah have not yet made evident those of you who fight\"; \"without God first proving which of you\". So why do I need to leave every other translation out there, which is literally not saying the same thing? And focusing