#passage(("I'll tell you something: the most fascinating thing to me. In fact, I may say, to me personally, maybe this is the number one evidence that Islam is the truth.", "Hindu scriptures, for example, telling you there's a chariot with seven horses pulling the sun; they also tell you, for example, that planet earth, there's a massive [bull] with these two horns, he's holding planet earth. Nobody sees it, but it's there. They have measurements for planets in their books, with the kilometers or whatever; they're all false, of course: how big is the sun, how big is it. Some scriptures will tell you the mountains used to fly, but one of their gods, he put them straight; the mountains used to fly in the air. So you'll have all of these types of beliefs. You hear them now, you think absurdities. You think this is absurd, but it's not absurd to the people 5,000 years ago. If you were sitting with me 5,000 years ago and I was like, \"Oh, the mountains used to fly,\" you believe me. If you were with me, let's say, 3,000 years ago (Job), and I come and I tell you, \"Actually the humans were [poured] like milk and [curdled] like cheese,\" you're not going to think much of this.", "The Greeks: they talk to you about Aristotle and [Galen] and all of these people. They had some of the most ridiculous beliefs that you couldn't even imagine, but they believed them, believing in embryology a baby was like a miniature baby, small baby, like the human figures, and then you become bigger. But for them it was not ridiculous. Why? They did not have the advancements that we have today.", "So that is when I tell you that, for me, the number one thing (I would say the strongest evidence) is that the Quran does not reflect the absurd beliefs of its people. It does not reflect the absurd beliefs of all humans on earth that time. Humans on earth believed the earth was flat. The Arabs: if you throw a bird in the air, if it goes right, then it's good, we should do this; if it goes left: no, let's not go. \"I'm going on a trip now. I have a bird. I'm going to throw it. If it flies right, I'm going on a trip. If it flies left, I'm not going on a trip.\" They have all sorts of ridiculous beliefs. They believe all sorts of nonsense about planets, about stars, about the universe, about the galaxies.", "I'm not surprised these other books say these things, because they're not absurd from their time. However, if you're Prophet Muhammad 1,400 years ago, there's no cars, no telescopes, no microscopes, no technology, no even printing machines. Nothing. So if [you're] going to make up a religion, and you will talk about every single thing that you can think about: you'll talk about the human, as the Quran does, their makeup, their hearts. You'll talk about mountains and their makeup and their functions. You'll talk about the stars. You'll talk about the universe. You'll talk about water. You'll talk about embryology. You'll talk about cattle and cows and sheep and animals. We have over 6,000 verses in the Quran, talking about every single topic. You talk about bees; you talk about every single thing you can think about. Not just things today or in the future, but things about the past, describing previous civilizations, previous nations.", "Now, is it humanly possible, with your 1,400 years ago knowledge? We've learned so many issues and mistakes, and we realize that these were issues and mistakes. With that level of knowledge, would you be able to produce a scripture that today, 1,400 years later, nearly 1,500 years later, it does not have a single error or mistake? Would you be able to do that? That is the thing which I believe for me is the most convincing.", "Of course, there's many evidences I've discussed, but for me, it is impossible for prophet Muhammad to be a human in that time. We don't even need to talk about the fact that he couldn't read or write, as the majority of the people in that region, because they were illiterate people in general. In Mecca there was no schools and libraries and, like I said, printing books or whatever; that was normal. Let alone all of that: even if he was the most educated person of his time, even if he read all the books, Aristotle, Galen, Socrates, everybody; he went to China and he learned from their books (which he never done any of that); imagine he did all of that and he gathered all the knowledge of the people on earth: all that knowledge is deficient. He would go and learn from Aristotle the things I'm telling you, and people will laugh today. Imagine the Quran: he went and he copied from Aristotle, \"Human being is a miniature baby that becomes bigger,\" and that is written in the Quran. People will come and laugh at us, just like we laughed at pouring like milk and [curdling] like cheese. This will be the knowledge of the prophet's time. He will reflect what his people said.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “How Do You Know Which Religion Is From God? A Detailed Comprehensive Analysis | The Muslim Lantern”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9F5gS1yWIRI", ((2093, "34:53–35:09"), (2113, "35:13–36:15"), (2179, "36:19–36:45"), (2205, "36:45–37:40"), (2266, "37:46–38:55"), (2335, "38:55–39:30"), (2370, "39:30–40:35"),))
