#passage(("The argument he says is the following: he says that if you throw spaghetti on the wall, some of it is going to stick. Which means what? The Prophetﷺ was making up statements, according to him; he was throwing spaghetti at the wall. And then he says to you that if you look at ancient civilisations, you will find one thing here or there, that is, you would say, \"Wow, this is prophetic!\" So if you go according to his own statement: if you go to ancient civilisations' books, you will find 99 wrong statements and one statement here or there that is like \"Wow!\" So then the Quran should be, like he said, full of fabricated, wrong statements, and then just one statement that is correct here and there.", "He is pretending as if the Quran, like the ancient books that he says, has tons of claims which are all wrong, and they happened to get one claim that is correct. That is not our argument. Our argument is that everything in the Quran is accurate, is correct, and that the information of the Quran is not one or two things. But the information of the Quran is accurate, and it is in line with biological life, it is in line with the life that Allahﷻ has created around us. That is our claim. So it is not that just one thing happened to stick when you throw enough spaghetti at the wall.", "And this is the other thing. This is a common claim of Atheists. They would say to you, \"This person said it 'this' years ago, this person said it 'that' years ago.\" The question is: how did the Prophetﷺ get this information? Did he study every ancient civilisation? And if he did study every ancient civilisation, how did he know which correct views to take and which wrong beliefs to leave? Because most of the beliefs of these ancient civilisations were incorrect. So you have one of two options: either to say that these things were revealed to him, or that the Prophetﷺ studied every civilisation that existed, and after studying every civilisation that existed, he already knew what was right, so he was picking what was right from them, which is just ridiculous.", "And then the other claim he made there is: why didn't he tell us about Germ Theory? If the Quran was to tell you about every specific disease, every specific cure, every specific useful information that you can have, how big of a book will it be? Is the Quran a user manual for every specific detail of human life? Is the Quran saying to you, \"I'm going to mention to you every single disease that is going to come and how to go against that disease\"? The Quran gives you general principles that deal with anything that is an issue. The Prophetﷺ said, for example, \"There is no disease except that there is a cure.\" So it tells you that if you ever encounter a disease, there is a cure, so you should be searching for that cure. And he says, \"تَدَاوَوْا\" (Cure yourselves), meaning go to doctors, cure yourselves. And he recommends certain things for people to cure themselves, like حجامة (Hijama: Cupping) and other things, and honey, and all of these things which can help cure the individual. But to come and say every specific name of the disease you want to find in the Quran, isn't that just a ridiculous statement to make?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Quran Scientific Claim Debunked? Bida'ah Is Good? | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5z_gp4XDYzs", ((1799, "29:59–30:32"), (1842, "30:42–31:05"), (1870, "31:10–31:45"), (1921, "32:01–32:50"),))

