#passage(("Any [refutation] is not meant to show that the Quran is a scientific miracle; in fact, those of us who have been working on this channel disagree with such a notion in the first place. Much of the content that [Ridvan] has made pertains to verses that are ambiguous, [mutashabihat], whose meanings are in the end ultimately known in their entirety only to Allah, as is in accordance with verse number 7 in surah 3. Science generally is of a changeable nature, and although it is a great method for gaining knowledge about the known universe, it is not a measurement of determining absolute truths, or sometimes even truths in the conventional meaning of the word in the first place.", "We can basically divide our answers into four categories that will get rid of those points which he makes in the video that are irrelevant. The four categories are:", "Category 1, non-science: those arguments that have nothing to do with science. For example, number 60: [Ridvan attempts] to claim that the Quran is scientifically inaccurate because it says that the heaven has been adorned with stars, that the stars are an adornment. He then argues that the stars are huge in size and therefore cannot be an ornament or an adornment. We simply respond by saying the size of an object does not take away from its beauty. Many writers in history have used the word adornment to describe natural phenomena that they are amazed with. This has nothing to do with the size of the object.", "Category number two: the points which have been already refuted. This refers to those claims that have been refuted in our previous videos.", "Category number three: Allah is the ultimate and direct cause. This refers to those points which can be simply addressed by saying that we as Muslims believe [Allah is] the direct cause of all events. Let's take as an example point number 15: [it states that Allah] steers the ships of the human beings. All this means is that the realities of the ship moving, rotating, etc., are directly brought into reality by Allah alone. Imam al-Ghazali, the 12th century Islamic [philosopher], elaborates that just because two or more events precede or follow each other, such as a wind moving and a ship moving along with the direction of the wind, does not imply that the wind is the cause of the ships' movement. All this means is that God creates a continuity in reality. The same logic applies for the other points, like in point 16: despite us seeing the continuous reality of a bird's muscle strength, its wing structure and the functioning of pressure enabling it to fly, we believe that all of these are separately, continuously caused by Allah directly and not by each other, including the flight itself, which to our naked eye seems to be the product of the mentioned continuities.", "Category [four]: Judgment Day. This category pertains to those points where [Ridvan] claims impossibility of certain events, even though those events ought to occur on Judgment Day, which is a period in time when realities shifted and changed. This category refers to those events that are ontologically possible, but [Ridvan] calls them impossible without substantiating that claim. For example, number 26: [Ridvan] claims the mountains cannot be pounded and scattered into ash and cannot be moved from their place. First of all, there's nothing inconceivable about a mountain being destroyed and scattered into ashes: every mass of matter can be transformed into a different mass of matter. But one fails to understand that Muslims believe God is omnipotent, and that this means God can do anything, wherein the word \"thing\" refers to any possible existence. Impossibilities are contradictions in terms, such as a squared circle or an existing non-existent thing: ideas about in-conceivabilities that cannot exist, as they are not things in the first place. Moreover, such a reality of mountains being scattered happening on the day of judgment is no surprise, as the day of judgment is a period of immense manifestation of God's attributes of mercy and wrath, where huge changes in reality will occur to demonstrate God's superiority over creation.", "In order for [Ridvan], or for anyone else for that matter, to disprove the Quran using science, two things must be in place. First, the science that is being used must be certain. Second, the Quranic verse [that] supposedly [conflicts] with that science ought to be understood only in that way and no other. From the onset, this is [a] near impossible task, as the Quranic verses have always had a range of possible meanings, and Muslim scholars for the last 1,000 and 400 years have tried to explain the possible meanings of these verses. This is not to say that every verse in the Quran can be interpreted in an infinite number of ways without any parameters to limit the scope of meaning; there is, of course, a scope and a limit.", "Also, another distinction to take away: just like every statement of a scientist, it is not necessary that a scientific statement is being made by them, such as Stephen Hawking saying philosophy is dead, or Dawkins saying Darwin's theory makes them intellectually satisfied atheists; likewise, not every statement of a Quranic scholar is a Quranic statement. Moreover, of the countless Islamic scholars that have existed in the last 1000 and 400 years, there have been times when these same scholars have projected their understanding of nature into the Quran, rather than derive conclusions from it, similar to [how] some Muslims claim that the Big Bang is found in the Quran.", "Secondly, the science that is being used to challenge the Quran must reach certainty. Sadly, the history and philosophy of science shows this is not the case. Science does not give us truth, rather workable models which are always changing. This is due to problems within the philosophy of science, which leads to scientific conclusions always being liable to revision. There are three pertinent problems that philosophers have highlighted. First, the problem of induction, which means that you can always get a new novel observation that can challenge a firmly held theory. Second, [underdetermination] of theory by observation, which means that you can have multiple interpretations or theories using the same data. Third, the problem of unconceived alternatives: there are alternatives to our best theories which are not brought to light, as they are [unconceived] due to our conceptual limitations at any given time.", "[Philosophers] have been vocal in their opposition of truth being linked to science. Philosophers of science Julian Porter and Phillip Pitcher drive the point home in their book Philosophy of Science, as they say: \"Science is revisable; hence to talk of scientific proof is dangerous, because the term fosters the idea of conclusions that are graven in stone.\" It is important to make distinction here about scientific theories and direct observations. Direct observations, like the shape of the earth, going around the Sun, layers of the atmosphere, are obviously certain and won't change. However, scientific theories always can.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] The Science Mistakes of the Quran - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6RTehKGfdCU", ((28, "0:28–1:07"), (67, "1:07–1:33"), (93, "1:33–2:12"), (133, "2:13–2:19"), (139, "2:19–3:26"), (206, "3:26–4:43"), (287, "4:47–5:31"), (331, "5:31–6:10"), (370, "6:10–7:02"), (425, "7:05–7:41"),))
