#passage(("From an Islamic perspective, the Quran is an absolute thing. It's an absolute Truth. It's an absolute Speech of God. Whatever you're going to present that is scientific (when you say scientific, you mean the information that we have today: today, we believe x, y, and z, whatever that is), what we believe today, that you can present, cannot be put against something which is absolutely True, because they don't have the same epistemological value, knowledge value, essentially. So we have the Quran, which is absolutely true, absolute reality. So whatever the Quran says is absolutely true; it does not change, cannot change. Science is subject to change, limited, based on a limited method. We cannot use something limited to say that something absolute is incorrect.", "A very simple example for that: you had the sun, and the sun was static, did not have an orbit. That was a belief that we held 50 years ago. It was being taught in universities. It's been taught in books, and everyone believed that was the case. The Quran said that the sun has an orbit, it's moving (alAnbiya21:33). And that was wrong that time. So if I were to bring you 50 years ago, and we had this conversation, you say, \"Oh, I have a scientific error! The Quran is saying the sun is moving.\" That's what you would be saying. But you will be incorrect, because our belief that time was incorrect, and what the Quran was saying was accurate and was correct. So therefore, I'm saying whatever you're going to present scientifically, based on that, is flawed, and it's not an argument that can be used against Islam.", "So if the Quran is true, then that whole lesson or arguments that you have is not important anymore. It's not necessary. You can throw it in the bin, if the Quran is true.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Muslim Claims The Quran Is Full Of Scientific Mistakes?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=9P6vTJhCSlI", ((475, "7:55–8:46"), (531, "8:51–9:37"), (662, "11:02–11:07"),))
