#passage(("Many of the Arabs of today, let's say who speak modern Arabic, they think they understand the classical Arabic language. They think they understand the classical Arabic, or the Quranic Arabic, and their language, which they're speaking today, which actually has been changed, obviously, due to the time passing by and the meaning of the words etc. changing. In order for you to know the meaning of the words, the classical words of the Arabic language, you have to go back to dictionaries.", "So, the first thing here: he starts with defining the word سماء and saying that Sama' means \"the sky\". This is clear evidence that this individual does not know the Arabic language, does not understand the Arabic language. Because if you go to classical dictionaries of the Arabic language, like لسان العرب (Lisan al'Arab) or any classical dictionary of Arabic, they will say to you that Sama’ comes from سما/علا. It means: when something rises or something is above. Sama' is used for that which is above you. The Arabs can use it literally for the ceiling above you, and you can see that usage all over the Quran. Allah uses the word Sama’ for different things that are above us.", "For example, Allahﷻ uses the word Sama' for the سحاب (Sahab: clouds). Allahﷻ says (Almuminun23:18) وَأَنزَلْنَا مِنَ ٱلسَّمَآءِ مَآءًۢ (And we send down from the Sama’ water). The water comes from the Sahab. The Quran also says that the clouds, the Sahab, are between the Sama’ and the Ard (Earth) (Albaqara2:164). So here, it's referring to what he's calling the planets, as the celestial bodies, or the Universe, or whatever term you want to use. So between the Universe and between the Earth, the clouds are there; they are in between, and the Quran describes them that way. So anything which is above you, in this case the clouds, are called Sama’.", "Allah also says in the Quran (Almulk67: 5) وَلَقَدْ زَيَّنَّا ٱلسَّمَآءَ ٱلدُّنْيَا بِمَصَـٰبِيحَ (We have adorned the lowest Sama’ with lamps), referring to the stars. So here it is referring to this Cosmos that we're observing. Where are the stars? Are they in the sky? Or are they in the Universe? They are in that Universe or darkness that we see above us. This is what is called the ٱلسَّمَآءَ ٱلدُّنْيَا in the Quran, clearly, which is what this verse is referring to.", "Also, السماء is used for that which is above everything. For example, Jannah (Paradise). Allahﷻ says (Adhariyyat51:22) وَفِى ٱلسَّمَآءِ رِزْقُكُمْ وَمَا تُوعَدُونَ (And in the Sama’ is your provision and that which you're promised), which is Jannah. Jannah is above everything, is above the Seven Samawat (heavens), that we believe in as Muslims. So Sama’ is used clearly here, in the Quran and in the Arabic language, for that which is above you. And in the case of this verse, it is referring to, whatever it is, if you want to use the term Cosmos, or darkness, dark matter, whatever we see around us. This is what this verse is specifically referring to. It's not referring to the \"sky\".",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| No Bible Contradictions? Following Mazhab? Mahdi Is Dajjal and more.. | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=oDQFEUUc7kQ", ((52, "0:52–1:16"), (82, "1:22–1:49"), (115, "1:55–2:36"), (160, "2:40–3:05"), (190, "3:10–3:46"),))
