#passage(("If you go to the verse, it talks about a specific human being, a specific individual who's called ذُو القَرْنَيْن (DhulQarnayn). Allah says in the Quran: حَتَّىٰٓ إِذَا بَلَغَ مَغْرِبَ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَجَدَهَا تَغْرُبُ فِى عَيْنٍ حَمِئَةٍۢ وَوَجَدَ عِندَهَا قَوْمًۭا ۗ He says: (when \"he\") -referring specifically now to DhulQarnayn- (on his journey when he reached the place where the sun is setting, he found–). Now, وَجَدَهَا here: what does وَجَدَ mean? (He found it sitting in a muddy spring) -in a muddy pool, in a muddy lake, whatever term you want to use-. Now, what is the Quran referring to?", "Now we go to any lexicon, any Arabic/English lexicon. It doesn't have to be from someone who's a Muslim. Go to Lanes lexicon, for example, and search up the word وَجَدَ, which is found in the verse: وَجَدَهَا. Now it will say to you: found it, attained it, discovered it, perceived it, saw it, experienced it, became sensible of it. All of these are accurate meanings of the word وَجَدَ in the Arabic language. So, when someone says وَجَدتُّ الله (I found Allah), it doesn't mean actually he found God in a physical sense right in front of him. So this verse in the Quran, when it says that (he found), it means: he perceived, he saw. This is what the Arabic language is saying. It's talking about the journey, the life story of a person, an individual called DhulQarnayn. That person, when he was walking, he saw that. It talks about his perception. It doesn't talk about the celestial body itself and the movement.", "The Quran, in probably tens if not hundreds of different places, mentions the Sun. None of these places does the Quran specifically say \"a muddy spring\". And also Allah says in the Quran (Yasin36:38) وَٱلشَّمْسُ تَجْرِى لِمُسْتَقَرٍّۢ لَّهَا (And the sun is moving in its due course). Nowhere in the Quran would you find that it says that due course is a muddy lake. It doesn't exist in the Quran. The scripture all over the place is not talking about that.", "Now, sun setting is a term that we use in language, even today. If now I googled \"What time is sunset?\" (which I do most of the time so I can know the prayer times): What time is sunset here? What time is sunset there? It's language! Google is not going to say: You're incorrect, the sun doesn't actually set! The Quran is revealed in the language of the Arabs. The way the Arabs use terms and descriptions is the same way the Quran described events. Allah says in the Quran (ashShuara26:195) بِلِسَانٍ عَرَبِىٍّۢ مُّبِينٍۢ (With a clear Arabic language); it goes in these descriptions and rules of the Arabic language.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| The Quran Has Multiple Errors?! Omar And The Pen & Paper Incident| Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=_Q0bo07LFNs", ((1231, "20:31–21:06"), (1270, "21:10–22:06"), (1329, "22:09–22:35"), (1363, "22:43–23:12"),))
