#passage(("First of all, we cannot be 100% sure in all cases as to what the word [ard] in the Quran refers to. This is why the context of verses is key for us in deciding the meaning of the word, translated as either earth or land. Secondly, where the Quran mentions how the earth is spread, the word spread does not indicate flatness for a thing. For example, we see that a fire spread throughout the forest.", "When it comes to the verse that says the earth is a bed, this is not explicit language; it cannot be used to conclude that the earth is flat. The reason the word [firash] is used, which means something to recline on, or a bed, is simply because the earth is comfortable to walk on; we are able to rest on it. Also, this could be referring to the desert land, because sand is something we can lay upon. The same logic applies to calling the earth a carpet; in other words, the earth is workable. As for the verse in surah 88, its context refers to the Arabic landscape, and the proof for this is that the Prophet is being addressed afterwards and told that he's not a controller over them, referring to the Arab pagans.", "In order to argue for an Islamic scholarly consensus of the earth being flat, [Ridvan] makes two major mistakes here. First of all, the [Jalalayn] is a linguistic tafseer. Secondly, the two [Jalals] were people that lived in the ninth century after [Hijra], or the 15th century. Earlier scholars provide us with the idea that there is a consensus among scholars that the earth is actually spherical.", "[Razi] says the following in his [Tafsir] Al Kabir, vol 19, page 131: \"If it is said: do the words 'and the earth We spread out' indicate that it is flat? We would respond yes, because the earth, even though it is round, is an enormous sphere, and each little part to this enormous sphere, [when] it is looked at, appears to be flat. As that is the case, this will dispel what they mentioned of confusion. The evidence for that is in the verse in which Allah says 'and the mountains as pegs' (in surah naba, surah number 78, verse number 7). He called them pegs, even though these mountains may have large flat surfaces, and the same is true in this case.\" In other words, what the Imam is saying here is that when we use language such as \"the mountains are pegs\", we're not saying that these things are literal stakes put into the ground.", "As for the Quran itself, there is nothing in the Quran that is completely explicit about the earth. Some verses can be used to mean that it is flat, while others can be used to argue that it is a globe. For example, [Ibn Hazm] uses the word used in surah zumar verse 5 in regards to the earth being covered with day and night as evidence, since [takwir] has always been used by ancient Arabs for something being wrapped or folded around objects such as a turban, never for wrapping something around something that is not [round]. And finally, it is extremely important to note that the [root] Kawara of the word yukawa-rô only occurs three times in the Quran; in all three times, they refers twice to the earth and once to the Sun, which are both round objects. It is safe to assume, based on this evidence, that the Quranic view is that the earth indeed is a sphere and is round.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] The Science Mistakes of the Quran - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6RTehKGfdCU", ((2338, "38:58–39:21"), (2367, "39:27–40:10"), (2451, "40:51–41:20"), (2482, "41:22–42:11"), (2531, "42:11–43:09"),))
