#passage(("I'm going to be going through Michael Jones' response to a very famous argument for Islam, which has to do with how the Quran intentionally corrects the biblical usage of the term Pharaoh. Michael is actually admitting that the Quran does get it correct when it refers to the Pharaoh of Joseph as a king instead of a Pharaoh. However, Michael says that when the Quran does this, it's nothing special, because it seems like it's just a coincidence.", "Now, I don't even know why Michael is comparing this to a coin flip. He's making Genesis sound like some sort of multiple-choice test with blank spaces: fill in the blanks with a Pharaoh or a king. That's not exactly what's going on here. By the way, if you actually flip a coin five times in succession, with heads representing king and tails representing Pharaoh, there's a 3% chance that you're going to get king five times in a row. But this is what the page actually looks like. It says: Pharaoh, Pharaoh, Pharaoh, Pharaoh. Now, let's compare this with the Quran, and you can tell me if you think that there was a 70% chance for these corrections.", "So, the section is the one right before the first usage of the term king in the Quran for the story of Joseph, where Joseph is in prison and two men in custody tell him about their dreams. One man tells Joseph that he dreamt that he will squeeze grapes into the cup of Pharaoh. Genesis 40:13 says, \"Within 3 days, Pharaoh will lift up your head and restore you to your position and you will put Pharaoh's cup in his hand just as you used to do when you were his cupbearer.\" Quran 12:41 says, \"O two companions of prison, as for one of you, he will give drink to his master of wine.\" Genesis mentions Pharaoh twice. The Quran avoids it and uses master. Genesis 40:14 says, \"But when all goes well with you, remember me and show me kindness. Mention me to Pharaoh.\" Quran 12:42 says, \"Mention me before your master, but Satan made him forget the mention to his master.\" Once again, Genesis says Pharaoh. The Quran avoids it and uses master twice. Genesis 40 uses Pharaoh four more times in the chapter. None of that ends up in the Quran, of course.", "Then Genesis 41:1 says, \"When two full years had passed, Pharaoh had a dream.\" The Quran says, \"And he remained in prison several years, and the king said, 'Indeed, I have seen in a dream.'\" Here the Quran clearly substitutes out the word Pharaoh for the word king. You find the word Pharaoh coming up in Genesis like 70 times. The Quran either skips it or it switches it to master or lord or in some cases king. Not once does the Quran copy the word Pharaoh.", "But I'm not done yet, because the same happens in the story of Abraham. And this is what most people overlook. In Genesis 12, Abraham's wife is taken by a pharaoh of Egypt. We find this historical individual being referred to as Pharaoh multiple times. Of course, there were no pharaohs in Egypt at the time. And this time, the Hadith corrects the narrative, not the Quran. As we find in Sahih al-Bukhari, the prophet, peace be upon him, refers to the man as a tyrant instead of referring to him as Pharaoh.", "By the way, let me give you an example of how Michael approaches these literary practices when you find them in the Bible. When these literary practices are mentioned, when it has nothing to do with Islam, this is a sign of authenticity. However, when the Quran demonstrates greater accuracy, nah, this is just a coincidence. Michael provides another reason why he believes the Quran avoids the usage of the term Pharaoh. Michael believes that the other possibility that the Quran did this is because it considers the term Pharaoh to be a mistake.",), "Farid Responds, “Quran Fixes the Bible's Historical Error AGAIN!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xAmZLS72HR0", ((0, "0:00–2:22"), (199, "3:19–3:59"), (243, "4:03–5:23"), (323, "5:23–6:03"), (363, "6:03–6:36"), (399, "6:39–8:36"),))
