#passage(("Michael is providing a new argument here, which is that the Quran made the mistake, or the author of the Quran assumed, that Pharaoh is just a name and not a title. In other words, when the Bible mentions Abraham and the Pharaoh, Joseph and the Pharaoh, and Moses and the Pharaoh, the author of the Quran is thinking, \"Hmm, Pharaoh keeps coming up so many times. Looks like these are just mistakes. I'm going to avoid doing that, and I'm going to replace the term Pharaoh with king.\" And he provides two pieces of evidence for this.", "When the Quran uses the term Firaun, it doesn't use the definite article al. It doesn't say al Firaun. And because it doesn't say al Firaun, it is to be considered a name. Ironically, Michael admits that this is how it is used in the Bible as well. There's no definite article. It's not included in the Hebrew. Well, according to classical third-century expert grammarian al-Mubarrad, words like Pharaoh, those that are definite noun from another tongue, a definite article cannot be added to it. He specifically mentions Firaun as an example. It would be incorrect in Arabic grammar to say al Firaun.", "But wait, Michael has another reason why he believes that Firaun is a name according to the Quran. You mean just like how house of Pharaoh is used in Genesis? Looks like the author of Genesis also thinks that Pharaoh is just a name. You have no good reason for assuming that Pharaoh is considered to be a name according to the Quran.", "But I'll give you a simple reason as to why it is considered to be a title according to the author of the Quran. You see, according to you, the prophet, peace be upon him, overheard people speak of Abraham and Pharaoh, Joseph and Pharaoh, and Moses and Pharaoh twice, because remember, we have two Pharaohs in Exodus. Now, naturally, if you hear that all the kings of a nation are referred to with the same name, you're going to assume that this is a title and not a name, especially since that was common at the time. They used to refer to the kings of the Byzantines as Caesar, even if his name wasn't Caesar. The same would apply to the ruler of Abyssinia. He would always be referred to as an Najashi. We even have this report from Anas bin Malik. He would say that when an Najashi passed away, you had another an Najashi who was a different an Najashi. The natural conclusion is that this has to be a title. And the second reason is the Quran actually gets it right with the Pharaoh of Moses while avoiding the anachronistic usages. Please don't say it's a coincidence.", "By the way, since we're on the topic of Quranic corrections, here's another one. According to the Exodus narrative, Moses is raised in the house of Pharaoh. He kills an Egyptian and flees to Midian. During that time, Pharaoh passes away. After he is given his task by God, he returns to Egypt and speaks to the new Pharaoh. And he is 80 years of age at the time. According to the book of Acts, we have additional details that mention that he spent 40 years in Egypt, then another 40 years in Midian. Now, the major issue here is that, according to Michael Jones himself, the pharaoh of the Exodus is Ramses II. However, the problem is with the first pharaoh, the one that took Moses in. Remember, according to the biblical narrative, Moses lived with him for 40 years. The issue though is that Seti I only ruled for 11 years. The pharaoh before him, Ramses I, only ruled for 1 year. And interestingly, it's only one of these two pharaohs that actually built the city of Ramses, which is mentioned in Exodus, and neither of them ruled for 40 years.", "Now, alhamdulillah, the Quran doesn't have any of these issues. There's nothing in the Quran that suggests two pharaohs in the first place. Instead, the pharaoh that takes in Moses is the same pharaoh of the Exodus. There are no events that conflict with the historical timeline. And you can actually fit all those events into the life of Ramses II, into his rule specifically, because he actually ruled for 66 years. And that is how the Quran corrects the Bible, once again, fitting all these events into the life of a single pharaoh instead of two pharaohs splitting it into 40 years and another 40 years.", "But just in case you're wondering, why would the Bible even split it into two pharaohs in the first place? What's the motivation for doing this? Well, in my opinion, and this is just speculation, this may actually just be tied to the age of Pharaoh. You see, in the beginning of Exodus 2, he already has a daughter that is bright enough, old enough to identify Israelite children. If she is that old, then maybe she's around 15, which would make Pharaoh in his 30s. If you add in another 40 years in which Moses lives with him, then another 40 years of time of Moses in Midian, then Pharaoh would be over 110 years old, maybe even closer to 120. So, instead of dealing with an extremely old Pharaoh, the bright thing to do would be to split the narrative by having two different younger Pharaohs. But that's just my personal theory. The facts remain: the Exodus chronology conflicts with history, and the Quran corrects it.",), "Farid Responds, “Quran Fixes the Bible's Historical Error AGAIN!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xAmZLS72HR0", ((568, "9:28–10:11"), (618, "10:18–11:00"), (660, "11:00–11:38"), (698, "11:38–12:53"), (773, "12:53–14:28"), (868, "14:28–15:13"), (913, "15:13–16:22"),))
