#passage(("That's a significant thing, that Pharaoh claimed to be God. Since the Bible was recording everything about what the Pharaoh was doing, it would record this magnificent event in which he was claiming to be God.", "I'm shattering your premise. You've built a whole big premise, that these are coming from the Apocrypha. I'm shattering the premise rather than dealing with every single story, which is a waste of time. I can shatter the whole premise, which I've just done, to show that the stories in the Quran are not taking from the Apocrypha; there are many stories that are not Apocrypha. That's the first thing.", "The second thing is: to say that God said to Moses you will be Elohim, how did you correlate that with Elohim being Pharaoh? Pharaoh being Elohim, which is complete nonsense. God is saying, according to you, that Moses will be Elohim, not Pharaoh. The term Pharaoh, historically, in the period of Joseph etc., was used for the house of the king, and then it was used for the king. That doesn't mean God, it has nothing to do with God there. King and deity are two separate things.", "Show me one Christian scholar that understood what you understood, that Pharaoh here refers to God and deity and Pharaoh claims to be God according to this understanding. Not after Islam, before Islam, because Islam makes that claim later on, so someone can take that to be a source. I'm saying without Islam, before Islam, can you find me someone historically that claims that, Jewish or Christian? Because this is a very long time you had until Prophet Muhammad came. Until Prophet Muhammad ﷺ came, you had Jewish historians who will understand better than you what Pharaoh means. Can you show me a Jewish historian that understood that Pharaoh there in the Bible, the commentaries of the Bible, means that the Pharaoh of Moses claimed to be God? And by the way, before Ramses II, the Pharaohs did not claim to be God, so that would be even inaccurate historically.",), "The Muslim Lan