#passage(("So here is another clear example of how he's jumbling and mixing everything up. You have Haman, this Haman that he talks about in the story of Esther, who's a Persian minister. You have this entire story about him. Then you have the name Haman mentioned in the Quran as the minister of Pharaoh. And he says, \"Look, there's a mix-up. The person copying is mixing up.\" You're the one who's mixing up. Just because two people have the same name doesn't mean the Quran is talking about that person. Just because the boxer was called Muhammad Ali, that doesn't mean we're the same person. This is just common sense.", "The Quran mentions Haman as a minister of Pharaoh, and it doesn't have a story about him. Now when you go to this Haman in the book of Esther, the story is completely different. Because in the Quran Haman is a minister of Pharaoh. Pharaoh is the one who's doing the evil, and he's just commanding his minister to build for him. So he's a minister who's dealing with architecture and building, and he said to him, \"Build me a specific thing.\" This is his entire role here. This is what the verse is mentioning about him in the Quran. Now when you go to the Bible, you got this Persian minister: there's an entire story about him being an evil guy who's plotting to eliminate all the Jews or something like that. And he plots a specific story. And then Esther tells the king about him, and he's captured and he's executed. So he's the villain in the story. He's the main villain in the story. The Haman in the Quran is not a villain in the story. He's just a minister of building that Pharaoh tells him to do a specific thing. So clearly there's no mixing up happening here. It's you trying to pretend that there is a mixing up happening just because two people have the same name.", "Most people would say that the pharaoh at the time of Moses was Ramses II, around that period. And at the time of Ramses II there was a minister who was called Bak-en-Khonsu, who was the minister of building for Pharaoh. So, if we take this narrative that the pharaoh at the time of Moses is Ramses II, then Haman, that the Quran is saying is the minister of Pharaoh, would be this person, Bak-en-Khonsu. And this person had a title. He had the title as the high priest of Amun. He spent his entire ministry serving Amun. And he was a minister of architecture, and he was very advanced in architecture and building and all of that. So it fits the Islamic narrative.", "What about the name? You have Bak-en-Khonsu, and the name Haman: where is the correlation? The correlation will happen when you start translating the name. If he was a servant of Amun, how do you say servant? You say servant by saying ham. And Amun, how do you say Amun in Egyptian? You'd say amana. So ham amana: Haman in the Arabic can be the Arabized version, easily, of the Egyptian ham amana, which means servant of Amun. And then the Quran would not only be historically accurate, you would even have to ask the question: how is the Quran familiar with this title and this name, since the Rosetta Stone was buried until 1799 and we couldn't even read hieroglyphics? So there is no mixing going on at all. Quran is historically accurate. And you can know for a fact, when you look at these details, that it is just you trying to make this patch up work, in a way of trying to justify your narrative, which doesn't historically add up, when you try to claim that Prophet Muhammad was copying.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Wess Huff Claims The Quran Was Copied From The Bible | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=hb-kHiXbGxI", ((1461, "24:21–25:00"), (1506, "25:06–26:13"), (1575, "26:15–26:59"), (1619, "26:59–28:09"),))
