#passage(("The Quran says something, and according to him history is saying something. Therefore, that's a contradiction. That's not called a contradiction. Contradiction is where you have two statements that are opposite to one another. You're claiming that there is a historical error in the Quran, where the Quran speaks about a specific group, in this case, and this group did not exist at that time. That's not a contradiction. That's a historical error.", "And this guy goes on to say, \"Moses is speaking to a Samaritan woman.\" There's nothing about a woman in the Quran there. This is a Samiri. It doesn't say it's a woman. This guy never picked up the Quran or read it from beginning to end. He just went on and Googled something.", "So let's get actually to the so-called historical error. He claims that the Samaritan people, they existed 722 or 720 BCE, according to him. Cannot happen, because Moses was 1200 or whatever, and therefore you have a historical error, according to this guy. Now we ask him, \"Where did you get this idea, this proof that they existed in the 8th century?\" What's his historical great source? It's the Bible. They go and bring 2 Kings, and they say 2 Kings is the evidence, specifically chapter 17, that the Samaritans existed in 8th century. So essentially, according to him, it's a contradiction because my book says something and your book says something else, therefore this must be wrong. This is the level of argumentation that some of these Christians apply.", "And we don't need to accept the historical narrative of the Bible. The Old Testament is even more corrupted than the New Testament. So why do we have to accept its narrative of history to begin with? So 2 Kings, who's the author of 2 Kings? According to the textual criticism scholars, with that agreement, it's anonymous author. Read for example the Interpreter's Bible, volume 3, and it will tell you in the introduction: it's written by anonymous author, it's been corrupted and redacted multiple times, and the author is separated from the time of the event. And somehow they bring this and they pretend as if, therefore, you guys have to accept this historical narrative, and therefore the Quran is wrong.", "By the way, if you read for example the Midrash of Genesis Rabbah, you go to the section 94, you'll have a rabbi there. The Jewish people, children of Israel, and the Samaritans, they have enmity between each other. So what the children of Israel do is, when they have an enmity with a group, they exclude them from the children of Israel. They say, \"You're not from the children of Israel.\" But do the Samaritans themselves claim that they are from children of Israel or not? If you go to that Midrash I mentioned, you will see a rabbi is arguing with a Samaritan, and he's telling him, \"Where do you guys come from?\" And they say, \"We trace back to Joseph.\" So it refutes this nonsensical claim that they come in the 8th century. This is their claim about themselves. So this nonsensical claim that you're making is false according to them.", "What's really funny is when you go to Second Kings and you look at the Hebrew word used for those group of people, the Hebrew word is Shamruni. This is the name that is given to them. But the Samaritans, they call themselves Shamrim, not Shamruni. Which, by the way, Shamrim comes back to Shamar, which refers to protectors or keepers. That's their name, according to them and their beliefs. So, for example, if you go to the Encyclopedia Judaica, it will tell you this. There is a difference between the Samareans and the Samaritans, and you have to make a distinction between the word Shamrunim and the word Shamrim; Shamrunim is the one mentioned in Second Kings. You have to make distinction between these two groups. They're not the exact same thing. The ones that are exiled and the religious sect are not the same.", "You'll find the same thing being mentioned in the World Biblical Commentary, Second Kings: it's wise to make a distinction between the religious sect and the community in the north as a whole. And they say that this word Shamrunim is been attributed to these group of people as a form of a parody, to mock the name Shamar, which is about to protect and keep. So there is a difference between the Samaritans according to Samaritans and Samaritans according to who? According to the Jews and the Christians. These sources I'm giving you are Christian sources and Jewish sources. So this guy comes and says, \"The Second Kings says this\": anonymous unknown author, that is separated hundreds of years from the source, in a writing that is redacted multiple and multiple times. And it's a misrepresentation, because this is a group and this is a completely different group. But somehow the Quran is wrong because Second Kings says something.", "And these guys want to talk about historical errors. Go to the Bible that talks about the time of Joseph and calls the Pharaoh when it was the period of the Hyksos. He could not have been a Pharaoh, because the term Pharaoh was used later on in the Egyptian dynasty. It was used later on, at the time of Moses, in the New Kingdom. And Joseph was in the second intermediate kingdom. You want to come and tell us about historical errors? If I started talking here about the historical errors in the Bible, I'm not going to stop till the end of time. But again, that's not a contradiction. So, so-called historical error have been completely refuted.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Christian Finds Five Contradictions In The Quran? | Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7hyNVF13R2o", ((177, "2:57–3:17"), (199, "3:19–3:29"), (210, "3:30–4:20"), (260, "4:20–5:03"), (304, "5:04–5:58"), (360, "6:00–6:44"), (405, "6:45–7:32"), (452, "7:32–8:05"),))

