#passage(("When something is correcting something, then one thing is wrong. So if it is wrong, and the kid is actually not Isaac, it's Ishmael, and the Quran corrects that, where's the argument you're making? Where does it say the covenant is the covenant of the Messiah? That wasn't on the altar.", "Let's go to Genesis:22. \"Where is the lamb for offering?\" Abraham answered, \"God will provide the lamb, my son.\" \"And the two of them went on together.\" \"They reached the place.\" \"Abraham built an altar there and arranged the wood\" (Genesis22:9). \"He bound his son Isaac, laid him on the altar, on top of the wood.\" \"Then he reached out his hand and took the knife.\" \"The angel of the Lord called out to him from heaven, Abraham! Abraham! Here I am, he replied\" (Genesis22:10-11). \"Do not lay a hand on the boy.\" \"Abraham looked up and there in a thicket he saw a ram\" (Genesis22:12-13). \"Abraham called to the Lord.\" \"The angel of the Lord called to Abraham from heaven a second time.\" A second time. \"And said, I swear by myself, declares the lord, that because you have done this and have not withheld your son\" (Genesis22:15-16), \"I will surely bless you, make your descendants as numerous\", \"and through your offspring\", through your offspring, Abraham, \"all nations will be blessed\" (Genesis22:17-18), \"because you have obeyed me.\" \"Abraham returned to his servants, and they set off together\" (Genesis22:19).", "Where does it say that this covenant of Abraham is Isaac's covenant of Jesus being the Messiah? How did you make this connection? Abraham puts Isaac on the altar, then the angel comes and tells Abraham: covenant to Abraham. That is his covenant. It talks about Abraham. How did you jump from that covenant of Abraham to a covenant of Isaac? And not only just a covenant of Isaac, but a covenant of Isaac about Jesus being the Messiah, from his descendants! The \"trust me bro\" argument doesn't work.", "Your argument was: on the altar, the covenant of Jesus coming as the Messiah was made; therefore, the Quran is wrong because it says it's Ishmael, and it's supposed to be Isaac. So show me now, where does it say that the covenant of the Messiah, specifically, was made on the altar, to Isaac? You're unable to show that, because it doesn't exist. I don't believe that is the covenant of the Messiah. There's many covenants: a covenant as the Messenger of God, a covenant as a Prophet. I believe in other covenants than the ones you believe in. We don't believe in the same thing.", "Your argument is based on the fact that, on the covenant, it was Isaac, and the covenant of the Messiah was being made. So when I ask you to present that for me, that's not a fallacy of exact word. That's me asking for evidence substantiating your entire claim. Because if you don't show me that this is a covenant about the Messiah, then your whole argument falls apart. There're multiple covenants in the Bible. Is there a covenant done with Ishmael? Yes! Is there a covenant done with Abraham? Yes! Is there a covenant done with Noah? Yes! There's many covenants. You're telling me it's a specific covenant about the Messiah; you need to prove, in this case, it was a specific covenant. There's many everlasting covenants. There's everlasting teachings, like for instance about circumcision, with Abraham (Gen17:13): covenants that are everlasting. You are unable to show the argument that you made. It's as simple as that.", "I could agree with you, even though I don't, this covenant is about the Messiah, but this is not on the altar. This is generally God saying that he will establish a covenant. Your argument is linked to the altar. You need to establish it on the altar, because, like you said, the issue comes from the altar, or with the difference between Ishmael and Isaac, which is your argument against the Quran. You brought a general verse, where God said, in the future, that he's going to have a covenant with Isaac. That doesn't necessitate that covenant was on the altar.", "Who was the older son, Isaac or Ishmael? Ishmael is the older son of Abraham. Let's say you have two sons, let's say Mark and Luke, and Mark is older than Luke. And we have someone that said to you: bring your only son with you (Gen22:2). Could that son be Luke, the younger boy? Because if I say only son, they cannot be two kids. There's no false premises there. It's just common sense, that if I said to you \"your only son\", then I'm referring to the older one by definition. Now, why does the Old Testament explicitly tell Abraham to take his only son to the altar? And how can that logically refer to Isaac, when it says only son, and Isaac was the younger son?", "Was he the son of Abraham? Ishmael wasn't part of Abraham's family? What kind of nonsense is that? What Israeli lineage? Israel is Jacob. He wasn't born yet! He's from the children of Isaac. You see how Christians have to jump through hoops when we show how inconsistent your logic is? Do you know why this verse exists, \"your only son\" (Gen22:2)? This verse exists to try and show Christians who have common sense how accurate the Quran is, and how the scripture has been changed. Because when the Quran says Ishmael, this verse, \"your only son\", was referring to the true only son, who was Ishmael, who was actually the one taken there. But because we have the Israelites, and we have their racism, if you want to call it, and love for their own lineage, they changed the name, because they would refuse to accept that the person who was taken for the sacrifice was Ishmael. They changed it to Isaac, someone who is from their own lineage, going back up from Jacob.", "It's impossible that this is referring to Isaac. It can only refer to Ishmael by common sense. Whether you want to call it corrupted or not (we do believe it's corrupted, yes), I'm saying common sense would necessitate that this refers to the older child, because it cannot refer to the younger one.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Dishonest Christian Insults Muslim After Getting Thoroughly Refuted! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=sfuH6zFYpaQ", ((540, "9:00–10:07"), (621, "10:21–11:29"), (702, "11:42–12:40"), (823, "13:43–14:55"), (908, "15:08–16:04"), (1007, "16:47–17:14"), (1078, "17:58–19:56"), (1210, "20:10–21:24"), (1341, "22:21–22:32"),))
