#passage(("[Ridvan] says that the Quran describes [Mariam] three times incorrectly. It describes her as the sister of Harun, Aaron, the daughter of Imran, and the daughter of the wife of Imran. [Ridvan] finds the last two points, which is actually one point, to be problematic, since the father of Mary is Yawakim and not Imran. What is the proof that his name was Yawakim? Christian writings. Don't you mean the Bible? Oh, you mean it isn't in the Bible? You mean that same piece that the Christians reject as a forgery? I wouldn't even trust the canonical gospels, let alone a forgery.", "There's one more piece of evidence that can be used against the name of Yawakim. [Ridvan] unknowingly mentions this while speaking about the history of this matter. This hadith refutes in two ways. Firstly, the Christians of Najaran did not object to Mariam being referred to as the daughter of Imran. Why? Because they did not hold the belief that her father's name was Yawakim. To conclude, the Quran affirms the name of Imran for the father of Mariam, and there is no valid historical evidence to refute this claim.", "As for the point of Mariam being referred to as Harun, well, [Ridvan] just answered it himself with this hadith. The prophet, peace be upon him, explained this matter when he was asked about it 1,400 years ago. And to be honest, in my opinion, this in itself is a sign of his prophethood, as we'll soon see. Early Christians were familiar with this practice, even though the Christians of Najaran weren't. An example of this can be found in [Guillaume Dye's] piece on the Quran and its hypertextuality: Aaron, Harun himself, is referred to as the brother of Mary. Not surprisingly, [Dye] points out that the typology here is intentional. Keep in mind that Mary here is the Virgin Mary and not the sister of Musa.", "I want to go a bit further and explain why the theory of the Quran confusing the mother of Isa, Jesus, and the sister of Moses is a ridiculous theory. The first and most obvious issue with this theory is that it would mean that the Quran assumes that Musa is the uncle of Isa. Now there is nothing in the Quran or hadiths that would suggest that the prophet, peace be upon him, held this belief. We do not find a single instance of Isa or Mariam having their stories interconnect with [Musa] and Harun either. Moreover, the Quran indicates that there is a gap between [Musa] and [Isa] in surah [Baqarah]. The prophet, peace be upon him, makes it explicitly clear that the last of the prophets was [Isa], indicating a large gap in time between these two prophets. Also, keep in mind that the only mention of the sister of Musa and Harun in the Quran is in this verse. We do not find her being referred to as the mother of [Isa]. The mother of Isa is a significant character in the Quran, and she's never mentioned in passing in the same way the actual sister of Musa and Harun was. This indicates that the author of the Quran is aware that they are not the same person.", "Multiple orientalists who have thoroughly studied the Quran and hadith, classical ones like Sale and [Geiger], and contemporary ones as well, deny that the Quran was mixing up both [Maryams]. Now perhaps the most ironic [refutation] against this absurd theory is the orientalist claim, held by the likes of Tisdall and Murad as well as contemporary Christian apologists, that the prophet, peace be upon him, had access to the proto evangelium of James. Now if that's the case, then the prophet, peace be upon him, surely would have noticed that both [Maryams] have different fathers. In other words, the inclusion of Imran as the name of [Mariam's] father was intentional.", "What I do find really funny about this whole argument is that not only is the Quran mixing up, but it is mixing up the [Maryams] that cannot be confused. Mary was a very common name, after all. In the New Testament, you have Mary the mother of Jesus, of course, Mary Magdalene, Mary of Bethany, Mary of [Clopas], Mary the mother of James and Joseph, and Mary the mother of John Mark. Now, the Quran doesn't confuse the mother of Jesus with any of the Marys found in first century Judea, but allegedly confuses her with a Mary from Egypt a thousand years earlier.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] One Mistake Destroys Islam? - Challenge Accepted!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=eJTsbcvMtis", ((86, "1:26–2:40"), (165, "2:45–4:20"), (269, "4:29–6:01"), (366, "6:06–7:25"), (453, "7:33–8:20"), (500, "8:20–9:03"),))

