=== 30.8 The Seven Sleepers of the cave
// NODE P05.C07.S08
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR308:1-235 | Farid Responds | 9
#passage(("They were starving to death; there was no other way for them to get food. The story's going to elaborate later on that they slept for 300 or 309 years. Whether they're conscious of that or not doesn't matter; it's going to make them hungry in proportion to that length of time.", "On top of that, in [ayah] 19: and similarly we awaken them that they might question one another. [Said] a speaker from among them: how long have you remained here? They said: we have remained the day or a part of a day. They said: your Lord is most knowing of how long you remained, so send one of you with this silver coin of yours to the city, and let him look to which is the best of food and bring you provision from it, and let him be cautious, and let no one be aware of you. Indeed, if they come to know of you, they will stone you or return you to their religion, and never would you succeed ever.", "So what we can see from this passage in the Quran is [they] were aware of the danger of going back to the city; they're very well aware of what's going on. The fact is simply that they need food, and there's no other way for them to get food for the seven of them. What are you gonna say, that they should have hunted some animals or something like that? That's not really easy when it comes to feeding seven people. This was more practical for them, [for] one of them to go to the city disguised in some kind of way, so that he avoids people figuring him out. Simple as that.", "AP appeals to the Christian narrative of the story, which says that there were seven sleepers in the cave. He says that the Quran does not give us that detail and therefore is lacking in knowledge of what happened. But this is simply not the case. The Quran is not indicating that there were seven sleepers of the cave; it's simply saying that people around Arabia were arguing about them being three, five and six. This is why it says in the ayah: they're going to say there were three, the fourth of them being their dog; and they will say there were five, the [sixth] of them being their dog, guessing at the unseen; and they will say there were seven, and the [eighth] of them was their dog.", "But there are multiple points to be made here in regards to the rhetoric of the Quran and the main intention behind mentioning this detail. First of all, one of the points of this portion of the surah is to teach people to not look at unnecessary detail, because people were arguing about the number of the people in the cave, they were arguing about the color of the dog that they had, they were arguing about where the cave was, but they were missing the main point, which is the portion of the resurrection, of the fact that they were resurrected from death back into life in a kind of figurative sense. So the main point of the story is to teach people that they shouldn't look at these details, because it says later on in the ayah: say, my lord is most knowing of their number; none knows them except a few, so do not argue about them except with an obvious argument, and do not inquire about them among the speculators from anyone.", "On top of that, our scholars did point out that from a rhetoric point of view, the two sentences that mentioned them being three and five are mentioned before the clause that says guessing at the unseen, and then the sentence about them being seven is mentioned afterwards, which means that implicitly the ones that are mentioning that there were seven are not included in the category of those who are denied by the clause guessing at the unseen. So there's a good reason for us, due to the type of sequencing that's going on in the ayah, to accept the idea that there were seven. So [AP's] argument fails as usual.", "What [AP does] here is show us how ignorant he is of linguistics, and Arabic language in particular. He says that in the verse that says: these people of ours have taken besides him, or from besides him, gods, that due to this segment of the verse it is implied that the Romans worshipped God and other gods along with him, because of the prepositional phrase [min dunihi], which means from besides. This is simply not the case, though, because the proper prepositional phrase that we would use in English, and a similar proposition that we would use in Arabic, would be along with, which in Arabic would be [ma'a], which just means with. I'll give you an example: in surah 15, verse number 96, it says: and those who set up with God another deity, or another God, they will get to know.", "So if we wanted to say that somebody worships something with God, and worships God as well, we would use [ma'a] and not [min duni]. [Min duni] would imply that they don't worship God, that they worship something besides him, although it doesn't have to be the case. It's not necessary that they worship God along with the gods that they worship besides God; it's simply not necessary, [due] to the nature of the language itself.", "I'll give you an easier example to understand this. Let's say I have two sentences describing a similar situation. I wanted to say that I drank juice with milk: I would say [ma'a], I drank the juice with milk, along with milk. To say I drank the juice instead of the milk, I didn't drink the milk, the milk has nothing to do with me, I would say min [duni], if we wanted to use the Quranic language.",), "Farid Responds, “The Men who Slept 300 Years (Seven Sleepers) [DEBUNKED] - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-yCPifGQqSw", ((68, "1:08–1:27"), (87, "1:27–2:07"), (127, "2:07–2:46"), (220, "3:40–4:23"), (265, "4:25–5:24"), (324, "5:24–6:06"), (414, "6:54–7:56"), (476, "7:56–8:28"), (508, "8:28–9:03"),))
