#passage(("Why would Theodosius respond to something that is most probably considered a rumor by him? Because these stories of long sleepers were famous in previous traditions; even the Greeks had them. Aristotle mentions things like this in his book on physics. Long sleepers are stories that were circulated by many people. Now, this per se doesn't mean that the story of a long sleeper is impossible; it simply means that an emperor such as Theodosius will have no reason to listen to that story and go himself to affirm something.", "And the good thing is, the Quran doesn't mention [emperors]. So this idea of [emperors] coming to check the cave sleepers is absurd, that I agree, but you can't just use it now to disprove the Quran, assuming that the story is true in order to say that the Quran is contradicting reality. This is not the case. You have a bad understanding of what it means to be a historical defeater.", "Had it not been the case that the Quran was revealed, I would not have believed in the story myself. But the thing is, when we have a good argument for the Quran being a revelation from God, being something that is revealed to a prophet who points to miracles around him that are occurring to [prove] his prophethood in relation to God who is sending him, this means that the [things] that God reveals is a manifestation of God's speech. And when God speaks about history he is completely accurate, because God knows all of history, and his speech would be the ultimate historical defeater for any previous fact.", "There is also an implicit fallacy that you're committing here. It's called the post hoc ergo propter hoc fallacy: the idea that because X event [precedes] Y event, therefore [Y] event must be caused by X event. This is not the case. You're saying that the narrative given by the Christians precedes the Quranic narrative, and therefore the narrator of the Christians has to construct the narrative of the Quran. It would be quite ridiculous to claim that all of these stories are just simple appeals to the Christians [and] the Jews. No, they serve as stories that God reveals that are either affirmations of certain elements of other narratives in history or negations of certain narratives that occur in history. They serve as part of the historical defeater, which is the miracle of the Quran being revealed on Prophet Muhammad, peace be upon him.", "This discussion should be on a much higher intellectual level, on whether the prophet is a prophet of God or not. Using arguments like this is basically the post hoc ergo propter hoc fallacy, and it's not going to work.",), "Farid Responds, “The Men who Slept 300 Years (Seven Sleepers) [DEBUNKED] - Apostate Prophet”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-yCPifGQqSw", ((686, "11:26–11:59"), (719, "11:59–12:24"), (747, "12:27–13:04"), (784, "13:04–14:01"), (841, "14:01–14:16"),))

