#passage(("This is a [strawman] argument. This is not what Muslims believe in. That's not what the [exegetes] say, and that's not what the verses are saying.", "The Quran is absolutely clear, telling you that the prophet is the one who explains the book. That part is very clear. The Quran tells you the one who explains the Quran is the prophet. So if you're ignoring that part, then it's not that the Quran is not clear. It's you intentionally avoiding the context that the Quran tells you to follow. So the Quran is absolutely clear, but you are cherrypicking what you like and avoiding the context and rest of the verses that clarifies what is intended.", "Nowhere does it tell you today to follow it. It tells you there's a clear chronological order. That's why it says \"after that we send this\", and then \"after that\", \"in the footsteps we sent\". If you avoid that, then I'm sorry to say you are intentionally misreading, because you want to misread. That has nothing to do with the clarity of the Quran. I've been reading the Quran my entire life. Nobody's misunderstood this to mean for the Jews to follow the scripture, because everybody reads the Quran. All over, the Quran is telling them to follow Prophet Muhammad, saying that the scriptures has been corrupted, saying the Quran is a criterion, a differentiator of truth and falsehood. Nobody gets confused except the person who wants to get confused.", "If you think this argument is going to work, it doesn't work with anybody, because Muslims know the scripture. You don't have an argument when you read things in the context. When you go to the [exegesis], when you read the two next verses, everything falls apart. So all you really need to do is to be a genuine person and actually read things in their proper context.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Educated Christian Challenges Muslim On Bible Corruption And Textual Criticism! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gYYmqGjrSmo", ((1784, "29:44–29:49"), (1828, "30:28