#passage(("Mutashabiha means that they allow for more than one meaning. But we have the method to understand the meaning of these verses. The method is the verses are clear in the Quran, and Prophet Muhammad himself interprets some of the Quran in a way to explain to us this principle. This principle is taken from Prophet himself.", "I'll give you an example that the Prophet himself explained. Allah says in the Quran, in chapter six of the Quran, if I remember correctly. It says: those who believe and do not mix Zulm (Zulm means injustice), they do not mix Zulm, injustice, with their faith, they will have safety on the day of judgement. Meaning that those are people who would be safe. Now the companions came to the prophet Muhammad and they said, \"Oh prophet of Allah, and who of us did not do injustice?\" Every human being does injustice. So they were scared, because we have all done some sort of injustice to someone. So, if this is the case, how do we understand this verse?", "So here now the verse can have an explanation that the people are not understanding, because the word Zulm there can have more than one meaning. This is what the Quran is talking about. Mutashabi means the verse can have more than one meaning; a word can have more than one meaning. So Zulm here can mean injustice, but it can also mean shirk, polytheism, associating with God. Where do we get that from? The prophet tells them. Allah says in the Quran, \"Indeed shirk, associating with God, is a great Zulm.\" It's a great injustice. So now Allah tells us in the Quran that he uses this word Zulm, and it's found in many parts of the Quran, by the way, where Allah uses the word Zulm as a reference to shirk, which is polytheism or associating with God. So now what did we do? We took a verse that has more than one meaning and we returned it to a clear verse, the one about shirk, and then we understood what the other verse means. This is the principle that the Quran establishes. So it's not like we cannot access their meaning. We can access their meaning, but we have to follow the Quranic principle and the prophetic explanation.", "It means it can look like something that it isn't. You can get confused. You can look at it and understand a different meaning than the meaning intended. That's what the verse means: more than one meaning in this case. This is what the mutashabih refers to.", "The same verse tells you who has the knowledge of it. This is what the verse says, chapter 3 of the Quran, verse 7: He's the one who revealed to you the book. From [it] are foundational verses; they are the mother of the book. Others are mutashabih: can allow for more than one meaning. So, those who have a disease in their heart, they follow the verses that have more than one meaning. They pick something that follows their desires. So, Allah says: seeking disbelief, seeking evil, seeking trials and tribulations (they have an evil intent), and to misinterpret it. But no one knows its true interpretation except Allah and the people who have firm knowledge, or they have established knowledge: the scholars who are firm in their knowledge. So, Allah tells us here that the interpretation is known by who? By these scholars. The scholar has the interpretation and understands. All the verses of the Quran, we can find their meaning. If you don't find it with this scholar, you will find it with that scholar. However, the knowledge is present within this nation, because Allah sent down this book to us and commanded us to reflect upon it. It make no sense for Allah to reveal a book and command you to reflect upon it if you can't find its interpretation.", "If you put in enough effort to seek the truth you will always arrive to Islam.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident Agnostic Grills A Muslim On The Quran Copying The Bible! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=24D1h0UflFg", ((1579, "26:19–26:37"), (1601, "26:41–27:37"), (1657, "27:37–28:42"), (1737, "28:57–29:12"), (1757, "29:17–30:48"), (1868, "31:08–31:10"),))
