// U ML234:242-328 | The Muslim Lantern | 4
#passage(("In Islam there is something called اجتهاد (Ijtihad: independent reasoning), if you want to translate it as that, independent reasoning, where you have a scholar looking at the text and trying to derive rulings or interpretation. This Ijtihad, it is not allowed in any text which is explicitly clear. There's something called أصول الفقه (Principles of Jurisprudence), in which we have text, and we have different categories for text. We have something called قطعي الثبوت and قطعي الدلالة (explicitly significant). They are different categories for text. One of them is, for example, explicit clear-cut text, صريح الدلالة; it's explicitly clear literature (There is no interpretation there). لا اجتهاد مع النص: there is no interpretation when it comes to that text that is قطعي الثبوت وقطعي الدلالة أو صريح الدلالة. You cannot have a \"but I think\". So when the Quran, for example, says pray five times a day, no one can come: \"But my interpretation of five there is actually the celestial number of bodies.\"", "The Quran is revealed in the Arabic language. These are the rules of the Arabic language: things are taken literally unless there is a reason that takes you out of that. Therefore, we treat the Quran the same way. Allah says (aShu'ara26:195) بِلِسَانٍ عَرَبِىٍّۢ مُّبِينٍۢ (In a clear Arabic tongue). It is in the rules of Arabic grammar, of the Arabic language, of the Arabic understanding. Otherwise, language has no meaning. You say to me \"Pray\", I'll say to you: \"Actually 'pray' is from a root word in the Arabic language, صِلة (Silah: connection), and Silah actually means connection, and connection means connect to God five times a day. So I'm going to connect to God, like a Buddhist, five times a day and make up my own religion.\" \"Prophet\" actually there does not mean an actual human being that was walking. And by the way, these are people who are bringing this nonsensical interpretation. I'm not even making this up: you've got some crazy people on YouTube who makes a channel and then tries to bring his own understanding of what the text says, and it's irrelevant; we're not interested!", "The first book, for example, that was written in Principles of Jurisprudence was written by someone called Imam ashShafi'i, who was very early, in the first 200 or 300 years of Islam. He is a very early scholar. And Imam ashShafi'i wrote that book for one reason; there's a piece of poetry that explains this. It says: و أول من ألفه في الكتب محمد بن شافع المطلب (And the first person to author in Principles of Jurisprudence was ashShafi'i), وغيره كان له سليقه مثل الذي للعرب من خليقه (The people who came before them understood these rules naturally). When Islam spread in the way it spread (you go from China to Morocco, all of these Muslims all over the world), there were many people who were not understanding these principles. So these basic principles that the early generations already understood without needing to put it in writing, he put it in writing to make it easy for people who came later on. So the reason ashShafi'i put that was not because of disagreements; these things were agreed upon. And these Principles, you would see, are derived from the Quran and Hadith, all of them. So it's not his opinion.", "If you just understand what Islam is and what Islam teaches, I think things become easy.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Claims The Qur'an Contradicts Science?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7tq4SvKcBbs", ((800, "13:20–14:26"), (889, "14:49–15:41"), (954, "15:54–17:13"), (1056, "17:36"),))
