#passage(("We do have an interpreter of the Quran, and that interpreter of the Quran is the prophet. Allah tells us in the Quran explicitly in multiple places. We have around six, five, six ways to explain the Quran. This has always been our tradition.", "First is explaining the Quran with the Quran. The Quran, unlike the Bible for example, only has God's speech, doesn't have anybody else's speech. Every word in the Quran is Allah's words, is God's speech. Allah may quote someone in the Quran, but still his words; it's like me quoting you: still my words. So all the speeches in the Quran is God's words. The hadith, or the sunnah, is the prophetic tradition, is what the prophet said, what the prophet taught. We separate that from the Quran. When I say \"Allah said\", I'm referring to specifically the Quran.", "Allah says in chapter 3 of the Quran, verse 7, that you have foundational verses of the Quran and verses which have more than one meaning. And the people have diseases on their heart: they go to the verses which have more than one meaning and they try to misinterpret the verse so they can find something evil. But Allah tells us what we can do. What we can do then is we take the verse that has more than one meaning and return it to the foundational verses, because the foundational verses would explain the verses that have more than one meaning, because there's other verses that talk about the same thing, and if you follow them you would realize what the meaning is. So we have foundational verses in the Quran, clear, explicit, and verse which has two meanings, one align with the rest of the verses and one doesn't. So you interpret that verse in line with the other verses. That's the right way of interpreting the Quran: interpreting the Quran with the Quran. That's the first way.", "The second way to interpret the Quran or understand the Quran is the sunnah, is the life of the prophet, is his tradition: what he taught us. Allah tells us in the Quran explicitly tha