#passage(("There is no verse in the Quran that says (and if it was to be translated, it would be) \"this is Arabic and foreign\", which doesn't really make sense. The only two possible verses in the Quran that could be referring to are verse 44 in chapter 41 and verse 103 in chapter 16.", "\"And if we had made it a non-Arabic Quran, they would have said, 'Why are its verses not explained in detail in our language? Is it a foreign recitation and this is an Arab messenger?'\" So this is a conditional statement. Allah in the Quran says: and if we had made it a non-Arabic Quran, if the language of the Quran was not Arabic, then these [polytheists] would have said, \"How come this is an Arab messenger but the book that he's coming with is not Arabic?\" The verse doesn't say that the [polytheists] came to the prophet and told him, \"We cannot understand this Quran because there are a bunch of words that are foreign right here and we don't know what they say.\" They didn't say this.", "And the second possible verse is verse [16:103]: \"And we certainly know that they say it is only a human being who teaches the prophet. The tongue of the one they refer to is foreign, and this Quran is in clear Arabic language.\" So the polytheist were accusing the prophet of learning the stories of the Quran from a young Roman slave. They weren't accusing him of borrowing or inventing new words or using foreign words that they know nothing of. And the Quran answers them by saying that this young boy has a foreign tongue, he cannot speak Arabic properly, and this Quran is in clear Arabic language. So it doesn't make sense. And in fact the prophet, [peace] be upon him, was calling him to Islam, not the other way around.", "I think it's important to say that Arthur [Jeffery] is a Christian orientalist. He published his book on the foreign vocabulary of the Quran in 1938, I think, and when he sometimes cannot find an origin of a certain word, he says \"he seems to have invented it\", referring to the author of the Quran of course. And this is a [non sequitur], because the way he concludes this is that since a certain word is not found in pre-Islamic poetry, therefore it seems that he invented it. First of all, not all the pre-Islamic Poetry reached Us in the 21st century. Second, not all the words of the Arabic language were used in pre-Islamic poetry. The Arabic language is so vast that it's impossible that the entire list of the Arabic vocabulary was used by the pre-Islamic Poets. Therefore pointing at a word and saying \"this is made up, this is invented\" doesn't necessarily follow.", "And let's remember who's saying that it's invented. Is it [Sibawayh]? No, it's freaking Arthur [Jeffery], a Christian orientalist who published this book in 1938. So in the end, keep in mind that no matter how confident someone is speaking, don't be fooled, and double check and triple check what he says, especially if it's one of these Evangelical ex Muslims.",), "Farid Responds, “Ex-Muslim: Quran made up Words! [REFUTED]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=bUmPWXd5P5Y", ((390, "6:30–6:52"), (415, "6:55–7:43"), (463, "7:43–8:30"), (513, "8:33–9:39"), (579, "9:39–10:07"),))
