#passage(("In the Quran, there's many words that are Arabised. And when a word is Arabised, whether you talk about the names of many Prophets, like نوح (Noah), and إبراهيم (Abraham), all of these words can be Arabised into the Arabic language, and then we don't say this word's not an Arabic word. So when you say Injil is not an Arabic word, I don't agree with that terminology, because the Quran is in Arabic language, all of it.", "A lot of the words, for example, in English: the word cat is from the word قِط. The word sugar is from سُكَّر. Coffee: قهوة. I can go on and on and tell you so many words in the English language. Nobody's going to come and say coffee is not an English word. Nobody's going to come and say sugar is not an English word. Nobody's going to come and say algorithm isn't an English word, even if it's taken from alKhawarizmi, and they translated it, took it to Latin, and took it into English, and then now they're using it in the English language. So you can Arabise a word, take a word from one language into another language, and then it becomes a part of the dictionary, lexicon of that language. It doesn't make it a different language.", "Now, why does the Quran refer to it as the Injil? Because this is how the people at the time know it as. This is what they would understand. This was also their Arabisation of the word. You had people who spoke Arabic who were Christians, or people who spoke Arabic who were Jewish, and this is the term that they would use. So the Quran would use the term that people would understand. But still, I would say it's an Arabic word. It's not a word that is not Arabic.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Shares His Experience With Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kEUpYAh5b3E", ((1011, "16:51–17:16"), (1046, "17:26–18:07"), (1093, "18:13–18:36"),))
