#passage(("Even when you say that Evangelion is specifically a Greek word, how can you prove that that word did not exist in Aramaic and then it was translated into Greek? Because we know that the Roman Empire adopted Christianity, so Christianity was there before the usage of the Greek language. So, whatever word that Jesus used, if he spoke Aramaic (you would have some scholars say, \"Oh, he spoke ancient Hebrew\"; most scholars would say he spoke Aramaic), then what did he refer to the good news as? And then you would know that those Greek people took that word from it. So, is it easily possible to say that he was using a word close to the word Injil, very close, if not even the exact same pronunciation of it, because they're all Semitic languages, Arabic, Hebrew and Aramaic, and then that they translated, or changed that word, or made it into Greek, into Evangelion? So, you could be looking at things the other way around.", "If we were to accept there's a different word that was used, the original word that was used in Aramaic, if you take that approach, and you say that's actually a Greek word and then people started using it, and it became the common word, and this is what people refer to it as: if the other word, whatever it was, if the Quran uses it, people wouldn't understand what this is even. \"We don't know what you're talking about!\" If you use a word that nobody uses anymore, nobody knows what it is. It died now with the Aramaic language or something. Nobody is going to understand what you're saying.", "But what matters is this: what is the Islamic definition of the word Injil? Which would be whatever was given to Jesus at that time, and not necessarily something that was written. So, to remove any of that mixture of confusion between it and the Bible (which we're already saying it's a completely different word anyways), you just look at how Islam defines that Injil, versus what Christians today define that Injil.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Arab Christian Shares His Experience With Islam And Christianity! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kEUpYAh5b3E", ((1620, "27:00–27:58"), (1691, "28:11–28:43"), (1727, "28:47–29:10"),))

