#passage(("In the Arabic language, when you use the word everything, it does not literally mean every single thing. When the Quran uses the word, it does not necessitate it is literally everything. It can refer to everything related to that thing. It does not necessitate everything in existence. There's books written on that: a book written about Arabic grammar in reference to the usages of the word in the Arabic language, when in the Arabic language someone uses everything or Allah uses everything. It does not necessitate literally everything.", "And I'll give you an example from the Quran: chapter 46, verse 25. It says it destroys everything. This is reference to the wind, because the verse before it talks about the wind, that punishment that Allah sent upon them to destroy their nation. Now, did that wind destroy the heavens and the earth? Did that wind destroy the angels? Did that wind destroy the jin? Did that wind destroy the universe? So everything there does not mean literally everything, because you could see in the same verse it says, then you could see only their houses left. So if the wind destroyed everything, how is the houses left? So here is the proof, not just from the Arabic language but from the Quran itself, that the word everything does not necessitate literally everything.", "But that's not also only in the Quran; that's also in language, even in English. If I say, for example, I have a fight with my wife and we're about to separate (this is a hypothetical situation), so I say to her, \"How can you leave me? I gave you everything.\" Of course, I didn't give her everything. I didn't give her all my money. I didn't give her all my belongings. What I mean by everything is I did so much good things. The word everything, even in English or in Russian, you have the same expression. I'm pretty sure that you would say everything: \"I did everything for you. I gave you everything. This is everything I have.\" It doesn't mean literally everything.", "So, the Quran is crystal clear. There's many verses in the Quran where everything is used, and in the Arabic language everybody knows who speaks Arabic that doesn't mean literally everything. So the Quran is referring to biological things, biological life. The jin is not biological life, the angels are not biological life; this is in reference to biological life. Is it true that everything biologically is made from Water?",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Confident \"Ex-Muslim\" Confronts Muslim Over Quran Contradictions! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=lIKAFhvv2iU", ((957, "15:57–16:55"), (1015, "16:55–18:30"), (1110, "18:30–19:16"), (1159, "19:19–19:53"),))
