#passage(("Christian [Prince] quotes six translations of the Quran. He claims that all of them are wrong and then provides his own translation. From the choice of words, he suffers from a truly limited vocabulary, a vocabulary not suitable for a religious text but rather one that is more suitable for a middle school playground.", "He makes the claim that the word [ghasiq] should be translated as male genitalia, and provides sources for his claim. The first source is the exegesis of [Ibn Atiyya], which says that [Mujahid] and [al-Hasan], all major commentators, held the view that [this] means night. He then points out that Nakash attributed to [Ibn Abbas] that the [ghasiq] is referring to male genitalia. If we actually do some research, we've come to the conclusion that [Nakash] is someone that is accused of lying. There are also three centuries between [Nakash] and [Ibn Abbas], and therefore we cannot accept this as a valid attribution to him.", "Christian [Prince] then provides a second source. Moreover, again, by returning to this work, we find that the author claims that it is well known that [Ibn Abbas] said that the [ghasiq] is referring to the [night]. On the bottom of the page, we find that scholars all rejected this attribution. Again, why would Christian Prince be so deceptive with this attribution? It is because he couldn't care less about objectivity, and most of you don't know any Arabic, and those that do are gonna look it up.", "Now that we're done with these false attributions, we can move on to the context of the verse itself. Chapter 113 says: \"Say: I seek refuge in the lord of the daybreak, from the evil of that which he created, from the evil of the darkness when it is intense.\" Observe the context. What is likelier: is it to seek refuge in the lord of the daybreak from darkness, or is it to seek refuge from male genitalia? Why does Christian Prince purposefully choose a misattributed interpretation for his translation? Again, he does not care about objectivity; he only cares about making Islam look as bad as possible.",), "Farid Responds, “Christian Prince Challenged Me to Refute This!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=DWbut8m7nBk", ((144, "2:24–2:45"), (166, "2:46–3:47"), (230, "3:50–5:07"), (307, "5:07–6:02"),))
