=== 31.8 The word Allahumma
// NODE P05.C08.S08
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR167:56-640 | Farid Responds | 19
#passage(("So first of all, what is [allahumma]? Well, [allahumma] is a statement that we find in the Quran and the hadith, statements of the companions, classical Arabic poetry; we find it in dictionaries. The authors of the dictionaries, grammarians, they define it as O Allah.", "Now, according to [Atchan], the first person to ever make this claim was a major grammarian, and he invented many, many beautiful things and contributed to the Arabic language studies, many useful sciences and many useful knowledge. He is the one who made this claim; he provided absolutely no justification for this claim. Now, first of all, I'd like to point out that he isn't just a [grammarian]; no, he's the original lexicographer, he's the author of the first Arabic dictionary. So to simply shrug him off as if he's not the source: no, he is the most important or one of the most important classical sources of Arabic. You can't just simply dismiss what he's saying.", "Apparently, according to [Atchan's] criteria, every statement that you find in a lexicon needs to be attributed to the Quran or derived from the Quran or attribute to a companion. Now, that's not the case. We go through dictionaries all the time, and what we usually find in dictionaries is word and meaning; you don't usually find an attribution, you simply find the meaning of the words. But now we find [Atchan] coming up with this new criteria for one simple reason: it's to push you away from the established definition of the term allahumma. Keep in mind that he's pretty much creating this criteria.", "Another thing to keep in mind is his claim that no one has said this before. Well, the author of the first Arabic lexicon, naturally he's going to say things before the vast majority of people. Is that how we're going to treat the Arabic language? That can be said about tens of thousands of words they heard from al-Farahidi, who died in 174. \"Remember, this is not part of the companions or tabi or tabi tabirin.\" He is a pious predecessor; he was a student of [Ayyub as-Sakhtiyani], he's also a student of [Asim]; check out his biography in [Siyar A'lam an-Nubala]. But more importantly, is this a new criteria by [Atchan], that you have to be a pious predecessor in order for your definitions to hold weight? Again, that's something that no one has ever said, no one's ever claimed. [Atchan] is making up rules as he goes along.", "Now, the reason why this is so ironic is because [Atchan] relies on late dictionaries. I put this cut and paste segment from a lexicon called [Lisan] al-Arab; Ibn [Manzur] al-[Ifriqi] is the one who composed and assembled this lexicon; it's a very old traditional dictionary. So, as we can see here, he's relying on Ibn [Manzur], who came in the eighth century, six centuries after; however, it wasn't an issue for him to rely on his lexicon.", "Now, before ending this section, I'd like to point out that Khalil isn't the only person: [Sibawayh], the greatest grammarian of all time, also held the same view, as [al-Zajjaj] points out, all reliable grammarians hold the same view. There's no reason for anyone to claim that everyone is copying.", "Now, one question comes to mind: where did [Sibawayh] and the grammarians come up with this knowledge from? Because, remember, not all definitions are extracted from the Quran; the Quran doesn't necessarily define every single term, not every definition comes from the statements of companions. Now, according to the Merriam-Webster dictionary, we find that a word gets into a dictionary when it is used by many people who all agree that it means the same thing. So they saw how people were using that word, and based upon that they defined allahumma as oh Allah. In reality, Arabs all around were using the word consistently in that same fashion.", "So after around an hour of research I came up with a list of 70 scholars who use the word [allahumma], and this is from 26 different sources. I stopped at around 70 because, to be honest, I was getting tired. Now, all these scholars were using the word allahumma in that same context, at the beginning of a dua: instead of starting a du'a with ya Allah, they would often use the words allahumma. Now, if [Atchan] wants to make the claim that all these scholars were [polytheists], then he can be my guest. Well, actually, [Atchan] does make that claim.", "Now, [Atchan] may try to claim that all these attributions are false attributions to these scholars; however, that would be a stretch. Remember, we're not even dealing with prophetic hadiths. There's no reason to assume that all these attributions are false; that would imply that there's some sort of mass conspiracy. But [Atchan] does make claims like that. Unfortunately, [Atchan], when making claims like this, doesn't really pinpoint where the fabrications come from. So apparently the Jews and the Christians came together and, under the watchful eye of the Umayyads, they tricked the people into saying [allahumma]. How does that make any sense? How could they even get away with this? And furthermore, the works I'm quoting aren't even [Umayyad]; later on there are [Abbasid] works and whatnot. So I'm hoping that [Atchan] would provide some sort of response to this; I'm hoping that he'd say something more than \"oh, the Jews did it\".", "Notice, [Atchan] doesn't even provide a single source or the name of a single [scholar] that says allahumma means Elohim. It's as if allahumma sounds like Elohim, it's probably the same thing. I'll be honest, I searched for the term and I could not find a scholar that referred to [allahumma] as Elohim. So on one hand we have [allahumma] means Allah, and this is a statement made by all the grammarians and all the lexicographers that came after; on the other hand we have [allahumma] means Elohim, and I don't think anyone even says this. He ties it with Elohim, which, yes, I guess can be problematic, but here is the issue with that: it's just a claim, there's no [dalil] for this.", "[Atchan] isn't even consistent with his definition of allahumma; apparently he will say that it means something different. So apparently, according to some contexts, [allahumma] means \"may you be cursed\". So which is it, Elohim, or is it \"may you be cursed\"? He can't simply pluck definitions out of the air in order to suit whatever he wishes for them to suit.", "So the reason why [Atchan] is trying to claim that the word [allahumma] cannot be found in classical poetry is because he's trying to argue that the word is an arabization of the word Elohim. Now, also keep in mind that the terms [bismika allahumma] were very common in Arabia, and they were used by the people of Quraish pre-Islam. So, for example, when the prophet, peace be upon him, was boycotted by the kuffar and they stuck a parchment upon the Kaaba, the words [bismika allahumma] were written on that parchment; you can refer to [Ibn Hisham's] seerah for that. Also, in the treaty, they told the prophet: don't write [bismillah], but do write [bismika allahumma]. You can find this hadith inside Muslim and many other sources as well. Furthermore, there are archaeological findings in which the words [bismika allahumma] were found. An example of one of those, that was discovered just a few years ago: archaeologist Muhammad Dawi affirms that it's pre-Islamic. So all this refutes the claim that [allahumma] was first used in the Quran.", "Oh, and one more thing: Allah confirms that the pagan Arabs used the word [allahumma], which shows that pre-Islamic Arabs use the term. Why are they saying this? Because it's a part of their speech; it means ya Allah. And before fans of [Atchan] say something like \"oh, but the mushriks are saying allahumma, therefore we shouldn't be using the word allahumma\": well, guess what, the mushriks used the word Allah as well. So what, you're gonna stop using the word Allah?", "So, after establishing the classical usage through the statements of the companions and lexicographers and whatnot, I think it's time to move on to what we find in the Quran. The term allahumma can be found five times in the Quran, and by switching out the term allahumma by the words ya Allah or O Allah, which is the definition provided by [Khalil], you find that these fit in perfectly fine with the context. So let's take a look at some of these verses. \"Say, Allah, owner of the sovereignty, you give sovereignty to whom you will and you take sovereignty away from whom you will.\" You can switch out the word [allahumma] with the words O Allah; makes perfect sense. \"Said Jesus, the son of Mary, allahuma, our lord, sent down to us a table.\" Similarly, you can switch out [allahumma] with the words O Allah our lord; makes perfect sense. The same with the other three verses: \"And remember when they said, O Allah, if this should be the truth from you, then rain down upon us stones from the sky.\" \"[Their] call therein will be: exalted are you, O Allah, and their greeting therein will be peace.\" \"Say, O Allah, creator of the heavens and the earth, knower of the unseen and the witnessed, you will judge between your servants concerning that over which they used to differ.\" These all fit in perfectly with the verses.", "But now let's take the examples that [Atchan] brings and see if any of these make any sense. \"Say, judges, owner of sovereignty\": now it doesn't make sense. \"Said Jesus, the son of Mary, may you be cursed, our lord\": that makes absolutely no sense. \"And remember when they said, rulers, if this should be from you, then rain down upon the stones from the sky or bring us a painful punishment\": doesn't make sense. \"Their call therein will be: exalted are you, may you be cursed\": it makes absolutely no sense. \"Say, gods, creator of the heavens and earth\": now doesn't make any sense.", "But now let's see how [Atchan] tries to escape this. No, the verse doesn't say the people say allahumma; the verse clearly says \"you say\", instructing the prophet to say this. And he's not saying that they said this, the kuffar said; he's saying \"you say this\". Now, you see the issue here: that is pretty much claiming that Allah is a terrible communicator, because instead of Allah saying \"O Muhammad, reflect upon what they say, they say allahumma\", Allah is saying to the prophet \"say [allahumma]\".", "Remember, as I was saying earlier, the issue with [Atchan] is his inconsistency: he used to claim that [allahumma] means Elohim, and in the same video he's saying [allahumma] means \"may you be cursed\". This problem with inconsistency is a theme that we find continuously in [Atchan's] videos. Now, if according to [Atchan's] theory this word really did mean Elohim, then Allah is affirming that the term is a legitimate term that we can actually use. Notice that Jesus, the son of Mary, isn't saying \"may they be cursed for saying Elohim\"; he's not saying that, he's saying [allahumma], ya Allah, oh God, oh Allah. That's what he's saying in the verse. So again, if we are being duped into [shirk] as [Atchan] claims, then we can't blame the scholars, we can't blame the Jews and the Christians; we have to blame the apparent meaning of the Quran.", "Indeed, those who do not expect the meeting with us and are satisfied with the life of this world and feel secure therein, and those who are heedless of our signs, for those their refuge will be fire because of what they used to earn. So that's referring to a group of people. Here's the second group: indeed, those who believed and done righteous deeds, their lord will guide them because of their faith; beneath them rivers will flow in the gardens of pleasure. Their call therein will be: exalted are you, O Allah, and their greeting therein will be peace, and the last of their call will be praised to Allah, lord of the worlds. This is referring to a second group.", "Now [Atchan] proposes something else in its entirety, and he claims that this isn't referring to two groups, this is referring to three groups. According to all [exegetes], verse number seven is about one group of people with a bunch of descriptions, and in verse number eight it's saying they're all gonna go to hellfire; and the second set of verses are about the people that deserve paradise and them going to paradise, and they will be saying allahumma. But according to [Atchan], the fire is for the first group in the first verse, while the second group that's referring to, that's all about this other section way down below, and it's the second group that are the [ghafilin], that are the people that are not aware, and they are going to be the group that says allahumma. Now look at this jumbled up order that creates confusion. Once again, Allah has no intention to confuse us. And the question is: what is the wisdom behind Allah doing this? Well, according to [Atchan] and his methodology, it just seems like Allah is just a bad communicator.",), "Farid Responds, “Hany Atchan Accuses All Muslims of Shirk”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=5rhSqn6GDuU", ((137, "2:17–2:36"), (158, "2:38–3:31"), (231, "3:51–4:28"), (272, "4:32–5:44"), (344, "5:44–6:19"), (379, "6:19–6:39"), (410, "6:50–7:50"), (470, "7:50–8:23"), (515, "8:35–9:51"), (630, "10:30–12:28"), (756, "12:36–13:17"), (836, "13:56–15:48"), (948, "15:48–16:19"), (985, "16:25–17:51"), (1071, "17:51–18:31"), (1111, "18:31–20:31"), (1313, "21:53–22:53"), (1385, "23:05–23:46"), (1426, "23:46–25:47"),))

