#passage(("This is a chronological order. The Quran is making a statement that: we sent the Torah, we said to the people, judge with the Torah; then we sent Jesus, and we sent to the people at the time of Jesus the Gospel.", "You can do that in the Quran: you can talk about things in the past tense about the future even. This is stylistic in the Arabic language. In the Quran, Allah talks about things that will happen in the future in Hellfire, and Allah say: they said (قالوا) (alMulk67:9-11). That doesn't mean it happened in the past!", "How are you supposed to understand? You go to a person of knowledge, not me necessarily, but you go to a scholar, a person of knowledge, like the Quran teaches you (anNahl16:43). It tells you: (Ask the people of knowledge if you do not know). Allah explicitly mentions that in the Quran: go to the scholars. Allah tells us the scholars explain the Quran. So if you don't understand, what do you do? You go to the people of knowledge. Open any Tafsir (exegesis) of the Quran, go to atTabari, go to alQurtubi; I'm not making up anything new here. All of them will tell you that \"we said to the people of the time of Jesus\": here it talks about historical chronological order, that the people at the time of Jesus should judge with it. Your position is not logically valid: when we use the next verse, and the verse after, your position crumbles and falls apart.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Christian Attempts To Trap A Muslim Using The Quran But It Backfires! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=baWW4lwWyjU", ((828, "13:48–13:57"), (852, "14:12–14:27"), (876, "14:36–15:38"),))
