#passage(("Sam Shimon and [CP] attempt to make it seem that there's a purposeful mistranslation of the segment of the Ayah, wherein they claim that the correct translation is \"besides Allah and the Messiah\", i.e. they claim that the Quran says that a group of people took their monks and rabbis as Lords besides God and the Messiah, implying trinitarianism. This is simply not the reality, though, for a very simple reason: cases. The Arabic language has three cases, in translation nominative, genitive and accusative. To skip all the grammar that was too boring for CP to listen to in class, I'm going to address the particular usage of the genitive and accusative cases in this verse.", "First of all, when a preposition is used in Arabic and is then followed by a noun, it is a rule that the noun is then going to be in the [majrur] case and not the [mansub] case. I cannot use the suffix a for the singular nouns after a preposition, as then the noun would be in the [mansub] case.", "Now let's return to the verse. Notice how the word ends with the suffix, and not e: this means that the word al-[Masih] is in the accusative case. Following from what we previously mentioned, this means that al-[Masih] cannot be connected; since [min duni] means \"from besides\", the wording \"from besides the Messiah\" cannot apply here.", "So one might ask, what does this mean? Let's go back to the beginning of the verse. The verse starts with the words which means \"they took\". The word is a plural past tense transformative double transitive verb, which means that the verb requires its object to be in the accusative case. This verb transitions its action onto two objects. For example, this would mean \"I took the book as a friend\", or \"I took his view as a foundation for something\".", "Now let's look at the Ayah: this means that they took their rabbis and their monks as Lords besides Allah, and also they took the Messiah as a lord besides Allah. The word \"also\" in Arabic is unnecessary here, as the conjunction is found to separate it from Allah, and the word al-[Masih] is in the [mansub] case, which means that it can only be an object that receives the action of the verb and cannot be a second member of the connection. This verse is essentially saying that they took their rabbis, their monks and the Messiah as Lords besides Allah, but in a different, yet grammatically consistent, order in the Arabic language.",), "Farid Responds, “Does Quran Teach Worship of Jesus?”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=2XMMpkKPz5U", ((100, "1:40–2:24"), (144, "2:24–3:07"), (192, "3:12–3:41"), (235, "3:55–4:39"), (289, "4:49–5:55"),))
// U FR067:1-48 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("I haven't had too many conversations with Avery, but I think I had two or three, and they're quite short; this was all in Clubhouse. One of them is burnt to my mind because of how ridiculous the situation was. We were in the Clubhouse room and there were several Arab Muslims on the panel, and he claimed, believe it or not, that according to Islam, according to the Quran, Muslims are supposed to worship Jesus. He quoted verse 31. So imagine this: [Avery] is actually saying to a group of Arab Muslims that this is evidence that we're supposed to worship Jesus, and the reason why he's assuming this is because of the order of the words. So I proceeded to explain to him that word order is irrelevant in Arabic grammar.", "And then, alhamdulillah, there was a Hispanic Christian who was actually learning Arabic, I think his name was Aaron, and he came up and he said: the Muslims are actually right. So it was really refreshing to see that. So we brought this to [Avery's] attention, but he couldn't care less; he wasn't convinced. He already made up his mind way before he ever entered the room; he sincerely believed that Muslims that follow the Quran were ignoring this one verse that is allegedly teaching us to worship Jesus.",), "Farid Responds, “Godlogic Teaches Arabs Arabic!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=CMPHDRV6Fi8", ((0, "0:00–1:02"), (75, "1:15–1:56"),))

