#passage(("Allah says in the Quran (azZumar39:4): If Allah wanted to take a son, لَّاصْطَفَىٰ مِمَّا يَخْلُقُ مَا يَشَاءُ (He will choose from His Creation). So He will choose one of the Creation, and that Creation can be referred to as a son. So it's not going to be an actual son that is biologically given, or begotten, or any of these things, but it's going to be a chosen being from the created being. So it's going to be created: something created that God will choose from what he created, and that will be referred to as a son. That's what the verse of the Quran says. But the verse in the Quran then says (Glory be to Him!) (azZumar39:4), or basically, He's high above that. So that's not something that God would do.", "That talks about the impossibility. It says (azZukhruf43:81) قُلْ إِن كَانَ لِلرَّحْمَٰنِ وَلَدٌ (say, IF the Creator Had a son). So the Creator doesn't have a son, but \"if\". So if I said, if I'm hungry, I would eat, that doesn't mean I'm hungry. That hypothetical \"if\" can be an impossibility. So for example, I can say, \"if I could fly, I'd go to the moon\". That doesn't mean that this is a possibility, and it doesn't mean that's something I could do. So this verse explores something which is an impossibility. It's not that we have an issue with the idea of God having a son or not; it's just that it's an impossibility. So the verse is essentially saying: if this was a possibility, if something this could happen, there would be no problem in us worshipping that son. Why not? You cannot have a son that is also a God. But this verse is essentially saying: if that impossibility was true, and somehow he can be created and be God at the same time, which is an impossibility, then he will be worshipped, because he will be God! But it's an impossibility.", "One verse talks about the rational IMPOSSIBILITY of having a child, through that child also being God, meaning begotten or any of that. So this verse explores an impossibility. The other verse talks about a different type of son, choosing something that is created, so it's not going to be a literal God. That's not going to be the son in the conventional meaning of the word. So you cannot mix these two verses, which is in separate Suras (chapters) and separate occasions, talking about different things. Allah will refer to it as a son. You can refer to it in a way like adoption, something similar to adoption: OK, I'm going to take one of those Creation and it will be referred to as the son of God. That's what it means. That's why Allah uses the word اصْطَفَىٰ, which means (to choose). So Allah will choose one of the created things.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “\"Innocent\" Christian Gets Caught Repeatedly Lying On Stream! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=t3a-sPh0yYQ", ((173, "2:53–3:40"), (239, "3:59–5:15"), (342, "5:42–6:51"),))
