#passage(("Do you see how your argument all falls down? Because your argument was based on misattribution, not a mistranslation: misattribution to the Quran. So the first issue is that you built on a false premise, because you specified it to food, and your premise or misunderstanding is that this verse is saying that Jesus made foods that were unclean clean now, which means you cannot eat them before, now you can eat them. And then you started building the argument and say: OK, in the Old Testament there were foods that are not clean, and one of the closest followers of Jesus is still keeping them, so that indicates that Jesus was still keeping them, so that does not make any sense! Now, the whole premise crumbles if the verse is not talking about food anyways: it's saying that he made certain things which were impermissible permissible, and the verse is not specifying in the Quran which are these specific things which are made permissible or impermissible. The second thing that would make the premise fall down even further is that the Old Testament is not trustworthy and the New Testament is not trustworthy, so I don't need to accept anything that it says the closest disciple of Jesus said, or that it says the Old Testament says about what was clean and what was not clean.", "I do not know; Allah didn't tell me and the Prophet didn't tell me; there's nothing in the Quran or Hadith that specifically states the exact laws that Jesus, let's say, abrogated or brought new commands. But when you say Jesus did, it's not Jesus doing anything, it's God doing it. The same God that said \"don't eat\" is the same God in this case. If Allah said \"don't eat this\" or \"eat this\" at any time or specific time, it's God's command; He does what He likes, does what He wants! God is All-Wise: if God says \"don't eat now and eat tomorrow\", OK, God said it, I'll do. It’s as simple as that! No Muslim can have a position without evidence.", "There are certain verses, other verses in the Quran, that mention certain things that Allahﷻ made Haram (impermissible) upon the children of Israel. But if you want to say specifically Jesus said certain foods, now leave the commentary, because the commentary is not evidence; what is evidence is the text itself, either the Quran or the Prophet, what we call \"Revelation\". So, unless you find the Hadith specifically or verse from the Quran that specifically mentions that this is specifically only about food: the Arabic language does not use anything to do with food there, it's to do with Halal and Haram. ولأحل from \"Halal/ permissible\", لكم (for you) بعض (some) الذي (that) حُرّم (made impermissible) عليكم (for you): this is literally word for word; that's what I can work with.", "You see how the whole argument of yours was based on mis-definition of terms. First, the idea of clean and not clean versus permissible and impermissible. Then the second part of it is the idea of what is the definition of sin: what defines the sin is going against the command of God. So, if God commanded something else to Jesus, that would not be God commanding doing a sin, or that will not be an action of doing a sin. The sin would be \"not to eat\", because God's command now is \"to eat\". So you see now it's very important to define what is morally right, what is morally wrong, and what morality is, what sin is. Because when you get the Islamic definition, you can avoid these misunderstandings of what is a law, this cannot change, for example, versus a command of God, which then you can have abrogation: that He would command something else at a certain point of time, and that same thing He would command a different command regarding it in a different point.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Ex-Jew Claims Rational Problems With Islam?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8isq12mnPYs", ((1675, "27:55–29:09"), (1766, "29:26–30:12"), (1821, "30:21–31:12"), (1909, "31:49–32:43"),))
