#passage(("If it did include Jews and Christians, then this verse would not make any sense. The Prophet wouldn't be talking to them and telling them (O, People of the Book, why are you arguing?). So that means they're arguing about Abraham; they're disagreeing with the teachings of Islam. And like I said, the Quran explicitly calls them polytheists. That's why the verse says: (And he was not of the polytheists). He was a monotheist: you guys are being polytheistic; don't be polytheistic, because Abraham was a monotheist. So it doesn't say that Islam includes them, but it says what they believe right now has been corrupted and changed, and the TRUE religion of Abraham, the true religion of The Prophets of God, is pure Monotheism, that they are no longer upon, and they became polytheists.", "Now, he's brought verses which are refuting what he says, because these verses are talking to the Christians and Jews, and it says they disbelieve, and the next verse says they disbelieve and say Allah is the third of three. So he's bringing these verses, which is actually refuting his narrative, where The Prophet is telling them that you guys are disbelievers because you believe these things. So it's not that they're also Muslims. Or would it make sense if he would call the Muslims and the disbelievers at the same time, which is the two opposites? So, obviously, that's not accurate at all, and it's a false representation of what The Quran is saying.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Clip-React| Responding To Professor Jiang's Lecture On The Islamic Golden Age | Muhammed Ali!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=6SVGKQ-xiGs", ((2931, "48:51–49:31"), (3028, "50:28–50:59"),))
