#passage(("God directly created him. No one is saying come from nothing; we're saying God created him. So Allahﷻ fashioned him. Allahﷻ says in the Quran (Al-Baqarah2:34): Allah commanded the angels to prostrate to Adam, and Satan also was there, and he was commanded to prostrate to Adam. When he did not prostrate, Allahﷻ said to him: (Sad38:75) مَا مَنَعَكَ أَن تَسْجُدَ لِمَا خَلَقْتُ بِيَدَىَّ ۖ (What stopped you from prostrating to which I have created with my hands). So it's explicitly there, saying that God created him with His hands. Allah says in the Quran explicitly: (asSajdah32:7) وَبَدَأَ خَلْقَ ٱلْإِنسَـٰنِ مِن طِينٍ (And he started the creation of human beings from a clay-like substance), or mud-like substance; clear cut, mentioned in the Quran. ثُمَّ جَعَلَ نَسْلَهُۥ مِن سُلَـٰلَةٍۢ مِّن مَّآءٍۢ مَّهِينٍ (Then he made his progeny from a liquid) (asSajdah32:8), which is essentially referring to semen and sperm, etc.; clear cut in the Quran. Allahﷻ, He says: (anNisa4:1) ٱلَّذِى خَلَقَكُم مِّن نَّفْسٍۢ وَٰحِدَةٍ (He's the One Who created you from one soul) وَخَلَقَ مِنْهَا زَوْجَهَا (And He created its mate from it). In the narrations it's also mentioned the creation of Eve, and how Eve was created from the rib of Adam. This is all explicitly mentioned in the Quran and Hadith.", "There is no room for a metaphor there. Essentially, if you will say this is not literal, then you can say anything is not literal. I can say God is not literal, prayer is not literal, this is not literal, that is not literal. Essentially, in Islam, or actually in every language or any basic logic, things are taken to their apparent, unless there is a reason that moves you into metaphorical or allegorical meaning. So you take information the way they are. If I say to you, \"Give me a bottle of water\", you don't think I'm referring to \"throw water at my face!\" You understand that \"Give me a bottle of water\" means \"give me a bottle of water\". So we take language to its apparent meaning. Now, we don't deviate from that except if there is a reason. For example, if I say \"there was a lion giving a lecture\", now you deviate from the literal meaning into a metaphorical meaning, because you understand that the lions don't speak; therefore I'm probably referring to a brave man giving a very strong lecture, that's why I use the term \"lion\" for him. You move now into a meaning which is not literal. So when it comes to language, when it comes to the Quran or Sunnah or Hadith, we take the literal meaning of everything except if there is evidence: evidence from the Quran and Hadith, not my understanding! Because every Tom, Dick, and Harry can bring his own understanding from the Quran and Hadith that takes us from that literal meaning into any other. And yes, this is the case with Adam, and this is the case with Evolution; this is the case with anything in the Quran, not just this.",), "The Muslim Lantern, “Agnostic Claims The Qur'an Contradicts Science?! Muhammed Ali”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=7tq4SvKcBbs", ((248, "4:08–5:19"), (319, "5:19–6:49"),))
