#passage(("When Allah speaks about: the love of the calf was rooted in their hearts because of their disbelief. It's ajib. Ponder upon this verse: isn't it strange that Allah is saying it like this? What does it mean, they drank in their hearts? The imagery here: they soaked it up, they soaked up with their hearts, in their hearts, due to their disbelief. It means that the love is referring to the love here, the love of the kufur, or the love for the [ijl], all of this. But why does it use the word \"they drank\" with their hearts?", "So if you go to Exodus in the Bible, it says that after they worship the calf, Musa had it destroyed, and he took the ashes and they put the ashes into the river or into the sea, and then he made them drink the [water] that had the ash off the golden calf. So the term here is referring to a literal drinking, but it says, meaning that it's speaking about love. So what the verse is doing when it's using the word is it's using it both metaphorically and literally. And the Quran has a lot of amazing things like this. And what's really cool about this: a lot of classical Arab, they don't know this; the [Yahud], they knew this, who are familiar with this, because they're familiar with their own scripture.",), "Farid Responds, “Amazing Quran Intertextuality and Double Meaning”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=xZEcZj7-8ww", ((21, "0:21–2:24"), (153, "2:33–4:14"),))
