#passage(("Back when [Ridvan] was a Muslim, he was approached by an atheist that asked him to read a verse. [Ridvan] didn't have an answer, was deeply affected, and ultimately left Islam. Yes, this verse was actually one of the reasons why [Ridvan], who was a devout Muslim for years, is no longer a Muslim.", "Oh, you who have believed, do not enter the houses of the prophet except when you are permitted for a meal, without awaiting its readiness. When you are invited, then enter; and when you have eaten, disperse, without seeking to remain for conversation. Indeed, that behavior was troubling the prophet. Allah is not shy of the truth. And it is not conceivable or lawful for you to harm the messenger of Allah or to marry his wives after him ever. Indeed, that would be in the sight of Allah [an] enormity.", "[Ridvan] makes a couple of major errors that need to be pointed out. Is this verse serving the prophet, peace be upon him? Yeah, nobody can deny this. Is that problematic? Of course not. Who gave [Ridvan] the authority in the first place to determine what is appropriate or not appropriate for the creator of the universe? If Allah wanted to provide comfort to his link to mankind, then that is his prerogative. That being said, there are other verses in the Quran that admonish the prophet, peace be upon him, like 867, 661, and 81. So it isn't like the Quran is always favorable to him in the first place. Yes, the text is directed towards how one should conduct themselves with the prophet, peace be upon him; however, these are guidelines that have been immortalized for our own benefit as well.", "Firstly, there is nothing in the report that says that the prophet, peace be upon him, was prevented from having intercourse with his wife. This is a baseless claim, since there is nothing in the context about intercourse. More importantly, the verse was revealed after the guests left. So, no, the prophet, peace be upon him, didn't use the verse for people to leave; rather, the verse was revealed after the guests left. [Anas] bin Malik comments: they hastened towards the door and all of them went out, and then came Allah's messenger, sallallahu alaihi wasallam, and he hung a curtain and went in, and I was sitting in his apartment, and he did not stay but for a short while; he then came to me, and these verses were revealed. So, once again, the verse was not used to remove the guests from the house. Nor was the prophet, peace be upon him, having intercourse with his wife with [Anas bin] Malik in the same room.",), "Farid Responds, “[DEBUNKED] Funniest Verse in the Quran”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=qI4ZMm0frRY", ((11, "0:11–0:35"), (39, "0:39–1:24"), (195, "3:15–4:23"), (296, "4:56–5:53"),))
// U FR349:1-148 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Among the definitions of what it means to be a liar is to be a person that purposefully covers up information pertaining to truth in order to deceive another person or more people. And so he does not read the verse fully. He reads, quote unquote, \"Oh you [who] believe, when you consult the messenger in private, spend something\", instead of continuing with the verse by reading \"spend something in charity before your private consultation\". He stops at the words \"spend something\" because he knows subconsciously that if he reads the rest, he's going to be refuted. As a side note, he does not read the verse fully for the rest of the thirty minute video, and this is precisely what CP does here: he doesn't quote the verse fully because it refutes him. In essence, he's trying to make it seem as if Allah ordered people to give money to the Prophet when they wish to talk to him, but this is simply not the case, as the verse is talking about sadaqa/charity, and [sadaqa] cannot be given to the [Prophet] or his family. And the proof for this is the hadith of Abu Huraira in Sahih al-Bukhari, where it says that Hasan ibn Ali took a date from the dates given in charity and put it in his mouth; the prophet, sallahu wa sallam, said: expel it from your mouth; don't you know that we do not eat a thing which is given in charity? In the Arabic, the word uses [sadaqa], and the same word is used in the Quran.", "So here Prince says that the prophet stole underwear or a bikini. It is blatantly clear to anyone reading that what is in question is a red velvet cloth, in Arabic [qatifa hamra]; this doesn't mean underwear. On top of this, CP claims the Sahaba accused the Prophet of being fraudulent, but this isn't the case, as the text says: when some red velvet cloth went missing on the day of [Badr], and some people began to say \"perhaps the Prophet took it\", the following was revealed: \"it is not for a prophet to be fraudulent\", [yaghulla]; or a variant reading, it has the passive, [yughalla], meaning to attribute [ghulul] or fraud to him, to the Prophet. So basically what happened is the Sahaba had this feeling; this doesn't mean they accuse him of being fraudulent. But Allah, to emphasise how this is impossible, to even think that the [Prophet] have took something, let alone for him to be fraudulent, is something that does not befit his honor or his character, so the verses were revealed. Also notice how it says \"some people\", meaning not all of the Sahaba, and how it says also \"perhaps the [Prophet] took it\": they're just assuming, they're not accusing anyone.",), "Farid Responds, “Christian Prince: Prophet Stole Bikini [Lies #24-27]”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=8cr1rqDYZm8", ((76, "1:16–2:36"), (156, "2:36–5:42"),))
