#passage(("A few weeks ago I uploaded a very short video with me and Zachary going through the satanic verses, dealing with the prophets of the Old Testament teaching forms of paganism. I, on the other hand, simply put up a challenge, which was something along the lines of: prove that these satanic verses occurred, and occurred in Islam. One single authentic report is all that I required. In any case, a few days later I come across a video that lists something like 50 sources for these satanic verses. Not a single one of them is authentic.", "Now, for those that aren't aware, this is how the satanic verses incident goes. The prophet, peace be upon him, is in Makkah. This is during the Meccan era, and he's reciting [Surah Najm], a chapter of the Quran. And when he's reciting it, when he mentions the false goddesses of the pagans (of course, the verses in [Surah Najm] condemn the false idols), in this particular incident Satan comes and whispers to the prophet, peace be upon him, some other words [in] praise of the idols, and the prophet, peace be upon him, is confused, and due to this he recites these, and pagans are rejoicing, happy that Muhammad, peace be upon him, started to praise their idols. And Gabriel comes to him and rebukes him and tells him: recite what he just recited. And he said: I didn't teach you those things. And then that's pretty much it: Gabriel corrects the prophet's recitation.",), "Farid Responds, “Origins of the Satanic Verses”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GGkG_cqGnLU", ((4, "0:04–0:49"), (58, "0:58–2:09"),))
// U FR253:714-741 | Farid Responds | 1
#passage(("A challenge today to all the Christian apologists: one authentic report about the satanic verses, that's all I'm asking for. There's nothing for them to appeal to; the story is once again pure desperation. Solomon's also a prophet, by the way. So teaching polytheism doesn't conflict with one being a prophet in the Old Testament.",), "Farid Responds, “David Wood's Top Ten Arguments Debunked!”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=YPvZ_xNePzo", ((1505, "25:05–26:03"),))
