=== 32.2 Chains of narration and the origin of the story
// NODE P05.C09.S02
#author("Farid Responds")
// U FR137:54-97 | Farid Responds | 2
#passage(("Those that want to claim that there's legitimacy to these reports, what they do is they try to pile on to you everything including the kitchen sink, in order to make it seem like this is something that has been reported by so many people; there's no way this could not have happened. These critics of Islam, instead of saying [Muqatil] is one source, they say: you have four sources, four references of this incident, when reality is just one person, who's unreliable, who is declared to be a liar by multiple major scholars of hadith.", "Another form of them doing this is they'll be quoting a single source through multiple different paths. So for example, they'll call it Tabari's path to [Mu'tamir] from [Dawud ibn Abi Hind] from Abu Aliyah, which is fine, and then they'll quote [Ibn] Sallam's report from [Hammad] from [Dawud] from Abu Aliyah. Now everything here is from one single source, who's a second generation Muslim that never witnessed the events, and therefore you can't really rely on them. If you actually go through these reports, they're referred to as [mursal]. What that means is they're disconnected. These reports are authentically attributed to these narrators; however, these narrators never heard from the prophet, peace be upon him. They were not there to witness these events.",), "Farid Responds, “Origins of the Satanic Verses”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GGkG_cqGnLU", ((132, "2:12–2:52"), (175, "2:55–3:57"),))
// U FR137:169-397 | Farid Responds | 13
#passage(("The satanic verses has come through multiple [mursal] paths, from the narrations of [Qatadah]. Each of them pass away from between the years 90 after hijra to around the year 110, 115, something along those lines. So they pretty much all die around 100 years after the incident took place.", "On the other hand, the first generation of Muslims narrated something that provides a different perspective of the events, and this is what they narrated. The companion of the prophet, peace be upon him, [narrates]: the prophet, peace be upon him, recited Surat [Najm], and everyone with him prostrated, including the kuffar, with the exception of [Umayyah]. Ibn Abbas, also a companion, [narrates] from the path of [Ikrimah] that the prophet, peace be upon him, prostrated, as well as the Muslims, [polytheists], humans and jinn. Abu Hurairah, also a companion, narrated that the prophet, peace be upon him, prostrated, and everyone prostrated with him except for two men. Those two men, they simply wanted to stand out.", "This rendition of events, without the wild details, isn't something that's only specific to the first generation; it was also narrated later on. You have of course Muslim, who narrates the incident in the same way as the companions of the prophet, peace be upon him. So what we have is four cases of the companions of the prophet, peace be upon him, narrating the incident of the satanic verses without any mentioning of the satanic verses. One of them was even a polytheist at the time.", "So we can come to the conclusion through this that the prophet, peace be upon him, did recite the correct [Surah Najm] without any details of Satan interfering or anything like that, and that's how the incident was recorded, that's what was known, that's what was accepted, and it later on evolved into something else. The idea of the satanic verses is too big of a detail to leave out. Originally the focus was: the prophet recited, the [polytheists] prostrated. That was the focus of the reports. However, in the second version, this evolved version, the focus is not on the idea of them simply prostrating; the focus is on this idea of Satan getting involved and putting in words into the Quran. It's quite a shift.", "Now someone may be asking: what would cause the [polytheists] to prostrate in the first place? To understand that, what you need to keep in mind is that this idea of pagans or classical Arabs prostrating to poetry wasn't that strange. You had [al-Farazdaq], who used to prostrate when listening to the poetry of [Labid], and they would ask him: why are you prostrating? And he would say: I am aware of the prostrations of poetry in the same way that you are aware of the prostrations of the Quran. One of the rulers of the Umayyad dynasty would also say, when speaking about the poetry of [Dhul-Rummah]: if this poetry was known to the people of [Jahiliyyah], the pre-Islamic Arabs, they would also be prostrating to it. So with that comes to mind that this idea of prostrating and admiration of eloquent speech wasn't foreign to classical Arabs.", "Secondly, the prostration of the prophet, peace be upon him, in for example Abu [Aliyah's] rendition of the satanic verses occurs right after he recites the praise for the pagan gods. However, that doesn't really match with the chapter, and with this the people of Mecca, the Muslims and the non-Muslims, prostrated to Allah. Remember, this order occurs at the end of the chapter and not at the end of the first third of the chapter.", "Do the second generation Muslims that narrated this hadith believe in it? That's not necessarily the case. You'll often find narrators narrating hadiths from their teachers; however, when they're narrating hadith without mentioning a source, they'll narrate something quite different, and I've come across several examples of that occurring. One thing that I do find concerning is that you'll find these [Tabi'in], who are known to mention their sources, they're for some reason not mentioning their sources for this specific report. That should be a cause for concern.", "Where did these reports come from? They may have come from Meccan propaganda. When the prophet, peace be upon him, prostrated, and the [mushrikin] prostrated as well, they may have simply been asked: why do you prostrate? And they would have answered something along the lines of: it's because Muhammad was praising our gods. And that idea continued and spread among the Meccans, and it was eventually picked up: the second [generation] of Muslims simply picked it up. Ibn Ashur reports that Ibn [al-Zib'ara], who was the top Meccan poet of the time, may be the person that created those verses in the first place, but of course there is no authentic chain for us to confirm that theory. Of course I'm just speculating, and there's ultimately no way to know who made this up and where it came from.", "Isn't this authentic from the report of Ibn Abbas? The narration that is authentic from Ibn Abbas is the narration that we find in Bukhari, in which he's simply speaking about the prophet, peace be upon him, prostrating, without going into detail as to why, and of course without the addition of the satanic verses. The report about the satanic verses comes through Jubair, the student of Ibn Abbas. [Tabari] quotes it from a path from [Shu'bah] from Abu [Bishr] from [Sa'id]. Those that narrated from [Shu'bah], including his top students like Abdul Samad and Abu [Dawud], are all narrating it in this fashion, and [Bazzar] points out that attributing this report to [Ibn] Abbas through the path of [Sa'id ibn] Jubeir is merely a mistake.", "Now this is supported by another path, which comes through [Uthman] bin [Aswad], who narrates from [Sa'id ibn] Jubeir without attributing it to Ibn Abbas. Wahidi narrates one of these chains in [Asbab al-Nuzul], and Ibn [Mardawayh] narrates another one in his newly published tafsir. Keep in mind that Ibn [Mardawayh] also quotes a report that has [Sa'id ibn Jubayr] linking it to Ibn Abbas, but that conflicts with the two previous reports from Uthman. This of course conflicts with the report that I mentioned previously. It also conflicts with the report of [Ikrimah] that is mentioned in Sahih Bukhari.", "So why do such mistakes occur? There is a term for this, which is referred to as [suluk al-jaddah], which translates to \"following the path\": because it's his main teacher, they make the assumption that the narrations of Saeed are from Ibn Abbas. Of course, in this case it's not.", "What are my thoughts on Salman Rushdie's book? It's a novel; I couldn't care less.", "Does [Albani] authenticate the hadith? [Albani] authenticates the origin of the report, which mentions nothing about satanic verses. For his response to the actual satanic verses, that's available in Arabic.",), "Farid Responds, “Origins of the Satanic Verses”", "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=GGkG_cqGnLU", ((391, "6:31–6:59"), (421, "7:01–7:44"), (481, "8:01–8:31"), (513, "8:33–9:27"), (570, "9:30–10:29"), (629, "10:29–11:41"), (709, "11:49–12:26"), (750, "12:30–13:29"), (811, "13:31–14:23"), (863, "14:23–15:00"), (900, "15:00–15:24"), (926, "15:26–15:31"), (940, "15:40–15:58"),))

